      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-11-14_WidnesExaminer_art0011" n="art0011">
        <p>FURNISH AT vvORSI,EY'S.</p>
        <p>“THE PANTHEON. "f GAYTHORN, MANCHRSTRII.I</p>
        <p>HIGH-CLASS POLLARD OAK FURNITURE HIGH-CLASS ROSEWOOD FURNITURE. HIGH-CLASS CHIPPENDALE FURNITURE. HIGH-CLASS SHERATON FURNITURE. HIGH-CLASS LOUIS XIV. FURNITURE. HIGH-CLASS CARVED OAK FURNITURE. HIGH-CLASS OFFICE FURNITURE. HIGH-CLASS AMERICAN FURNITURE. HIGH-CLASS SCOTCH FURNITURE.</p>
        <p>W ORSLEY'S MAGNIFICENT DISPLAY</p>
        <p>THE GREATEST SHOW IN LANCASHIRE.</p>
        <p>THE SENSATION OF 1893.</p>
        <p>DOUBL THE STOCK OF ANY HOUSE IN THE NORTH.</p>
        <p>WEEKLY CHAT.—Continuing the article of "A Good Fur- nishing House,"which appeared in our "Weekly Chat" of the previous advertisemeet, and which was a portion of a des- en i !Jtion of a visit. to "The rantheon." we will now proneed to sit ' xt sorest on the list ; thistis the re ese.eing room Department.rhis department is the most importanttof all to the Ladies. Here all the delightfuLartistio tastes are gratified to the full. The exquisite, refined, poetic tense of beauty is ministered 'es !ts the artistic blending of colours, in the viewing of the el m ens of High-class Cabinet Carving. Every lady who es esssful in furnishing her Drawing-room in the modern assto, style has good reason to be proud of her taste. The old-fashioned idea of furnish- ing a Drawing-room with ponderous, heavy furniture is a thing of the past. The modern idea (and it is undoubtedly an improvement in taste) is to furnish it, a delicate, artistic manner.to have every pieceof furnittre in the Drawing-room of a different design. Instead ot the old ponderous sofa or couch, a cozy corner now ministers to the public taste in- stead of she old mastic e marble-top cheffonier, with big plate glass back, there is new to be seen a beautiful Louis XIV. Carved China-bedecked Cabinet.with cut iously shaped mirrors and crystal plates, of vary unique design, with plenty of shelves nooks, and corners for the display of ehina and bric-a-brac • numerous brackets fitted in corners, with spaces for the display of photographs, now go to adorn the Drawing- room. Family portraits and heavy pictures are things of the past. In place of these old-fashioned ideas we now decorate the Drawing-room with beautiful ornaments, jugs, paintings on mosaic, etc. ; and if any pictures are used at all, they must be very light in colour and very artistic in shape. The mantelpiece and the overmantel nowadays form the most attractive and decorative part of the Drawing-room. First of all, you will require the mantel-board. It must be draped in the best style, and according to the quality and beauty of the mantelpieee It the mantelpiece is all wood, then the drapery can be dispensed with, and instead numerous little shelves adorned with china will assist in a marked decree the decorative features of the Drawing-room. The over- mantel must necessarily be in keeping with the mantelpiece. Loose or hanging styles are specially suitable for quaintness. Even the pianoforte in the Drawing-room nowadays is placed in a different fashion to theold style. It is generally made one of the most beautiful objects in the room. Instead of being placed against the wall, it is now placed sideways in The room, the back of the instrument being decorated with beautiful drapery and photographs. Instead of the old- fashioned idea of a nine-piece Drawing-mein Suite, all up- holstered in the same material, and of heavy, cumbrous frame-work, we now admire beautiful occasional chairs, all of a different design and colour. Exquisite stuffoyer and beau- tifully upholstered easy chairs, an stuffed in curled horse- hair and feathers, supersede altogether our forefathers' old- fashioned ideas of beauty and comfort. Worsley's at "The Pantheon," claim to have the /shinty to carry out these later' ideas, and to furnish the Drawing-room in the best style re- quired, equal to the very best fireas in the country, with only one marked difference that of the cost. Worsley's possess a stock of DraWing-room Furniture second to none in the country. They have the very Finest Specimens ot High-class Cabinet Work; the most Beautiful, Exquisite, and Artistic Upholstering Materials procurable ; an unrivalled Stock of. Curtains and Drapery; Carpets and Bugs of the very Finest Description. . _ .</p>
        <p>All those requiring Drawing-room Furniture are strongly advised. in the interests of education and experience in the •• Art of Furnishing." to pay a vfsit to "The Pantheon," Gaythorn.</p>
        <p>DRAWING-ROOM SUITES. ontinuing our description of the Drawing-room Fur- nifure as a contrast ln prices, and to show what a wonderful stock there is at Worsley's, which embraces every variety o price and quality, we will now quote slew suites of Drawing- -50,m Furniture. We ciin supply a Drawing-room suite. up- holstered in rich recherche velvet, suitable for a tradesman, for 6 guineas. This price seems to be almost incredible ; but, nevertheless, we can absolutely guarantee the stability of this suise, and we undertase to return the money to any pur- chaser who can point out any defe .t. We absolutely guaran- tee this suite to wear well. To show the immense variety of Worsley's stock, we have Drawing-room suites from £4 153. up to 50 guineas. There are over 100 suites on show, uphols- tered in all the newest materials, for 10, 15, or 20 guineas. Worsley's can supply you with a suite of Drawing-room Fur- niture irreproachable in style and incomparable in price and quality. Cabinets, Worseley's can Lupply from £2 15s. upto £75, embracing every style procurAle in the market today, in walnut, mahogany, Chippendale, Sneraton. and rosewood inlAid. Occasional Tables, Card Tables, Wine Tables, Book Tables, Writing Tables, and Davenports. Secretaries, Music Cabinets, in all the newest styles, a-e on show at Worsley in the greatest variety. Worsley's cater as well for the trades- man, who mtist stud p econoray.as for the merchant,to whom style and quality is &amp;more important consideration than price. The Drawing-room display at "The Pantheon" has mused tint- versal admiration from critics and connoiseurs. livery visitor to Worsley's Showrooms have expresse 1 themselves in terms of enthusiastic delight. Our article to-day is specially written to draw attention to the Drawing-i oom Furnishing Depart- ment. Worsley's have on show 100 Overmantels,from 138,6 d. up to £25. Worseley's can furnish a drawing•roorn in exquis- ite style, comprising Suite of Furniture,Cabinet. Overmantel, Occasi nal Table, Music Cabinet, for £25. complete. Anyone who has any intention 'of purchasing Drawing-room Furni- ture should not miss the opportunity of visiting Worsley's before deciding. All the goods are marked in plain figures, and whether they intend to purchase or simply visiting for the purpose of enjoying and appreciating the beautiful dis- pl,y, their visit cannot but ben-fit them'. It will be interest- ing and instructive. They can act as their own salesmen. They are at perfect liberty to please themselves whether they buy or not. All goods delivered carriage paid. Competent workmen will be sent to fit up the articles on delivery in the best possible mannei.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-11-14_WidnesExaminer_art0012" n="art0012">
        <p>CARPET DEPARTMENT. The Carpet Department is one of the largest depart- ments at "The Pantheon," and in order to draw public attention to this fact, we shall continue each day to quote our prices, in order that the publie cqn compare them with the prices as ticketed in the show windows of our competitors. The Carpet Showroom at Worsley's is the fineA out of Lon- don. All the productions of the very best makers of the world are ix strong evidence here. Carpets to suit all classes —the rich and the pour, the tradesman and the merchant, The gentleman who has been placed in command of this de- partment is a gentleman of great experience, and every order placed with " Worsley's " will have his persomal attention. Most carpet departments develop an immense stock which is soiled, and detracts to a very considerable extent from the value. There is not a single yard of stock at Worsley's which is a month oIL Every carpet delivered will be guaranteed to be spotlessly clean. Special Brussels, 2s 40 per yard. Extra Cut Line Brussels, 2s 7id per yard. Superior Brussels, 25 llid per yar;t. Five-frame Brussels, 3s 4d per yard. Best Five-frame Brussels, 35 6d per yard. British-made Axminstere, 35 3,1 and 35 Ed per yard. Best Royal Axminsters, 35 DO per yard (usually Old retail at 5s llid in Lancashire). Best Wooi-bacl: Aria, 5s 61 per yard. The Crompton Axminster, is I,d per yard. The Crompton Axminster is specially recommended as the finest Carpet exta• t, the most beautiful designs, and the loveliest colours procurable. Inspection invited. The Princess beamless Axminster Squares. 121 t uy Eft, £4 195 61.</p>
        <p>Lot 5 Excellent HOUSE of FURNITURE, suitable for young couple. with a view to econotny. All the furniture is substantial. guarantec,d for five 'years ; money returneel if not satisfactory. The Dining or Sitting Room contains a heavy dining-room suite, with massive carved framework, very strongly upholstered in leather ; couch, pair of easy chairs, and four single chairs ; worth £B. A dining tab-le, with patent screw, extending to 5 feet ; usually sold retail for £2 10s. A massive sideboard in walnut, with brilliant plate glass back; worth £6 les. A hand•ome richly-coloured border* carpet and rug to match, cornice with rings. ends, and brackets ; Nottingham lace curtains. The bedroom is beautifolly turnished with a solid walnut bedroom suite, consisting of a wardrobe with a plate-glass centre door, with compartments specially fitted for hanging dresses ; a beautiful dressing table and washstand to match, the dressing table enclosed with drawers, and fitted with jewel drawers and crystal mir -or attached; the wash- stand, enclosed with table de nuft, fitted with Carrara marble and Minton tile back two chairs, and towel- rails attached to washstand. This suite alone is worth £ll ii. neat brass and enamelled bedstead, fitted com- plete with mattress and complete set of bedding. Set of chamber ware, pair of lace curtains, bedroom fender, carpets, and bearthrug. The Kitchen is nicely furnished a square kitchen table with with a set of kitchen chairs, white top, fender and irons, top bar and 9hpan, a square of oilcloth. tour yards by three yards. There is also oilcloth for hall and landing, cocoa mat, and two bedroom mats. The lot packed and delivered free of charge for £26 9s. 6d.; may be returned if not satisfactory. WORSLIIIY'S have also complete HOUSES of FURNI- TURE at 1010.,£15,£20.£3 , "), £4O £5O, up to £SCO. Lists of these sent post free on application.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-11-14_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
