      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-11-14_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>CAPITAL " CHAT.</head>
        <p>CAPITAL " CHAT.</p>
        <p>A very curious and interesting contribution to tie "made in Germany" controversy reaches me row an Englishman whe has long been resident in ,outh America. Although not himself actually ngaged in commerce, he is in a position to know tie business methods employed in some at least of tie South American republics, and in particular in 'eru and Bolivia. In both these countries there an,he says, be no question that Germany is pass- rig Great Britain in the commercial race; but he dds that Italian and Slavonic traders are becom- ng more numerous, and that the reason why 3ritish trade is suffering is not any inferiority in Iritish goods. but the illicit practices in which raders of other nationalities do not hesitate to en- sage. It is, he says, an open secret that in both iolivia and Peru unscrupulous traders are in league sith the Custom-house officials to defraud the re- venue. "No honourable merchant," he writes, can now do business in thEse countries, for the •eason mentioned ;" and he adds that in Bolivia his illicit traffic with the Custom-house officials is o thoroughly organised that the revenue is de- rauded of two-thirds of the duties that ought to be :ollected. Under honest rule these South American republics would be quite able to pay their way; but -he corruption among Government officials, from he highest to the lowest, is notorious; and illicit leafing of the kind I have described is, in the )pinion of my informant, "an important factor in he increase of German trade." This is a state of hings which does not, of course, exist in all coun- ries,so that even if full allowance be made for it, it an only repre,ent one aspect of the question; but it is at least worthy of attention as affecting the trade with South America.</p>
        <p>I find that diplomivists in London are just now ' much fuller of Prince Bismark and his series of Amazing revelations than of the Mansion House -peech or the Armenian question. To them these blazing indiscretions " have come as a succession ,)f explosions which leave great rents in the ground all around them, and destroy the most cherished of all diplomatic traditions—secrecy. Oue member of the Diplomatic Crops said to me that these revela- tions are not only sowing suspicion and distrust far and,wide in Europe, but are exciting the same feel- ings between individual diplomatists. They show that no nation can trust its closest alli-s, and that after all there is something to be said for "isolation" as a policy. Certainly no distrust of ' England on the Continent, of which we hear so much, can equal the distrust by Continental nations of each other created by Prince Bismark's ruthless disregard of the diplomatic proprieties.</p>
        <p>The public are inclined to exaggerate the incomes of leading counsel at the Bar, but. the estimate for one eminent advocate, placed at £20,000 a year, cannot he far wide of the mark. In the past week his fees would run into thousands of pounds. For the defence of Mrs Castle, the wealthy American who pleaded guilty at Clerkenwell Sessions, £l,OOO was marked on his brief. A divorce case lasted right through the week, and this would also bring him about a similar amount. He had also lucrative business in hand in other courts.</p>
        <p>The great scheme of relief for the Voluntary Fchor-ls promulgated by the unanimous "voice of the Church has fallen still lower. In private con- versation it fails to fin I complete support in any of the camps into which the Church is really divided It is treated with laughter and contempt by Radicals and Educationists. It is condemned, for one reason or another, by nearly all the organs tavpurable to some new concession to denomina- tionftlists. In view of this failure to come to any real agreement it is agiin being confidently stated that the bill of the Government will be a very ,mall one, if indeed there should be a bill at all.</p>
        <p>Is not Lord George Hamilton a little imprudent in giving his sanction to a very objectionable proceed- ing of the Ealing Urban District. Council, who are levying a voluntary school rate of threepence in the pound for the support o the schools in the district? This proceeding seems to him to be "wholly un- objectionable," while he makes the suggestion that the words " and is not compulsory "might be in- serted in the demand note. The opinion of Lord George, who represents the India Office, has fortu- nately, no weight in a matter of this kind, and it will be for the Local Government Board, at least in the first instance, to say whether such a custom is to be sanctioned or not. If they also take Lord George's view, other means will probably be found for keeping within the limits of their powers those district councils who resort to this method of foisting a rate upon the public. Should these high Tory jinks be tolerated during the present-Minis- terial regime, they will come to a sharp and sudden end when that regime is over. But the practice, if brought before a court of law, would not bear five minutes' argument, except through the courtesy of a judge,</p>
        <p>Nearly all the comments in political circles here on Lord Salisburyie speech are favourable in a greater or less degree. Satisfaction is almost universally expressed upon the settlement of the X.enezuelan difficulty, the general feeling being that it was a trivial but dangerous matter, and that it was well to settle it on any terms, and get it out of the way. But a certain aggressive section shake their heads over the acktuSwledgment by this country of the claim by the United States to inter- fere in the quarrels of independent States on the American continent.</p>
        <p>The Conservatives here are well content with their victory, such as it is, at Bradford. In Liberal circles there are divided opinions and much ani- mated discussion. On one point there is general agreement, and-hat is that Mr. Bilson has made a splendid fight under most difficult circumstances, and that the Liberal party is much indebted to him. It is the question of the lesson and moral to be drawn from the result which excites discussion. There is very little sympathy here with the sugges- tion that the large support obtained by the Inde- pendent Labour party in this and previous elections in tt e borough would justify the Liberal party in giving the candidature of one of the seats to the Labour party.</p>
        <p>The Bishop of Marlborough has got into trouble by telling a tale about the kidnapping of a Catholic priest when he was about to enter the Church of England. The Bishop did not know that any re- Porters ;were present, and is much disconcerted to find the story which he confided to a small meeting blazoned forth in print. He is now trying to escape from the responsibility for it, but of course the Catholic community,from the Cardinal downwards, is in a high state of indignation. They are probing the matter to the bottom, and believing that they have caught an Anglican bishop tripping, are not likely to let it drop till the rash prelate has done penance for his offence.</p>
        <p>There is a feeling that the leaders of the Liberal party, not only in Bradford but in many Yorkshire constituencies, are to blame for lbe bad manage- ment which has allowed such Marge amount of organised dissatisfaction to grow up in the Liberal ranks. It is stated that this is not the growth of a day, and that the Independent_Labour party has not created the situation, but rather have stepped in, late in the day, to occupy a place which had long been in course of preparation for anyone who would put himself at the head of,a, revolt against "official- bra." Yorkshire Liberal leers will not ba dict- ated to by London, but in London, where there has been a much more Sympathetic attitude towards the working class element in the Liberal party, the general feeling is that the capitalist and individual- ist section of the Liberal .party in the great York- shire Radical Constituencies ought,by a wider toler- ance of all views and !Elections, to heal the differ- ences which have arisen and stop the cleavage which is likely to appear.</p>
        <p>The polling in the Eastern division of Bradford on Tuesday resulted in the election of Captain Greville, the Conservative candidate. The votes were given as fol- lows :—Captain Greville, 4,921 ; Mr. Bilsoe, 4,526 J. K. Hardie, 1,953. At the general election Mr. Reed (C.,nservative) received 5,843 votes and Mr. W. S. Cane 5,139.</p>
