      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-11-14_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>SUNDERED HEARTS;</head>
        <p>THE SUNDERED HEARTS;</p>
        <p>OR, A WAR OF THE HOUSEHOLD. _ By the Author of "The Double Life,' "The Bailiff's Scheme," &amp;c., &amp;o.</p>
        <p>CHAPTER XXVI. A MOMENTOUS QUESTION.</p>
        <p>Felix Warner entered Lord Champney's sick. chamber with a hushed tread, an anxious look, and a countenance indicative of the keenest apparent distress.</p>
        <p>Yet in his heart was a wild hope that he shouldi find his kinsman nigh unto death—that the obstacles between him and the Champney title and este.tes was about to be removed.</p>
        <p>In obedience to the baron's gestut,',. the butler left the room. Warner came forward to til 411111:11 on which his lordship was lying, a sudden Loom darkening the secretary's eyes as he marked the invalid's easy position. " Ah ! then it is not so had as I feared, Sidney I" he exclaimed, grasping the white and listless hand of his kinsman. " Rugg's message was so vague! I—l feared you were dying !" The baron smiled bitterly. "1 have been near death, Felix," he answered, wearily—" very near And you have been very near to becoming Lord Champney." Warner uttered an ejaculation as of pain. "Don't, Sidney—don't !" he cried, distressfully. "Have I ever given you cause to speak so to ow? Have you ever seen in me a desire to succeed to the position which you so worthily fill? Have I ever exhibited a spark of envy on account of your honours and riches ?"</p>
        <p>"Never—never !" cried Lord Champney, a generous glow lighting up his pale face, while he pressed Warner's hand with kindly welcome. "You are a true friend, Felix—the most patient, unselfish and trustworthy of man. lam glad that I shall be .worthily succeeded when the time comes. I did not mean my words as a reproach old fellow. You are too sensitive—too tender-hearted."</p>
        <p>"I shall never succeed you, Sidney," said Warner, with a mournful accent, relinquishing his lordship's hand and seating himself. "You have a splendid physique. You are not greatly older than I. I shall be the first to drop off the family tree, after all. But about this accident of yours. It was an accident? You are all right now ?"</p>
        <p>The baron smiled faintly. Warner's show of seemingly honest distress touched him nearly. It was very pleasant to be cared for by some one, and Lord Champney's noble soul went out in a tender gratitude to his hypocritical kinsman, to whose faults he had always been blind. "I am on the road to recovery," he answered, "but lamby no means well. All my anxieties are preying upon me, and wearing me out. But about the accident. I was out in a great storm yesterday in the yacht, and was east on the Cranny rocks. It was a narrow escape, as you may think, and I wonder at it myself." "And the Lady Barbara. ?" asked Warner, breath- lessly. "What has been the effect of all this upon her ?"</p>
        <p>A deeper glow covered the baron's noble face. A glorious light crept into his eyes.</p>
        <p>" Felix," he said; in a reverent whisper, "Bar- bara was on the sands praying that I might ba saved. It was Barbara who saved my life. And all last night, although I had previously repulsed her, while I lay sleeping she watched over me, and pursed nie as a mother watches over her sick child."</p>
        <p>Warner arose and paced the floor. His face was ghastly pale, and he looked like one before whom a sudden chasm_ yawns._</p>
        <p>"I always defended the Lady Barbara, you remember, Sidney ?" he said, huskily. " You did, Felix—you did." "And now I am to congratulate you on your reconciliation ?" asked Warner, yet more huskily. "You have forgiven and forgotten her ladyship's Innocent and thoughtless flirtations—" Lord Champney put up his hand quickly, his forehead contracting with sudden mental pain. "Listen to me, Felix," he said, covering his eyes with his hand. "I need counsel. I need the opinion of an honest, impartial person—one who e;teenis both Barbara and me. When I met my adventure yesterday I was on my return from Cromer, where I had been to challenge that dastardly wretch, .Efflugham— Warner started.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-11-14_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>"You have fonght_with the colonel, then ?"</head>
        <p>"You have fonght_with the colonel, then ?"</p>
        <p>"No. I appoi;ited to meet him to-day at noon, at the Cranny beach. Of course I could not go. And he had the audacity to call here to-day and inquire if I arrived home safely yesterday." "But why did you challenge him?" Lord Chainpney replied by telling the story of Effingham's proceedings toward the Lady Barbara, as viewed from the husband's stand-point. When he had finished, Warner was ominously silent. But there was a red gleam in his eyes that told of a rejoicing spirit within. " What do you think of all this?" asked the baron, eagerly—" of the picture in his desk, the letters, and her note of warr!ing to him? I want to look at the question aside from any jealous anger, if I can. Is there any excuse to be found for Barbara? Are her acts capable of any innocent construction?"</p>
        <p>"That's a strange question, under the circum- stances," remi.rked Warner. "Is it? I told you of her devotion to me last night. I cannot reconcile the two—her devotion to me, and the incontrovertible evidence of her love for Effingham r Warner appeared to reflect. "I don't know what to say, Sidney," he ob- served, slowly, after a pause. "The case looks dark—positively black. A person must go ac- cording to evidence. I admire her ladyship. I like her, but for her treatment of you. Can it be possible that she watched over you last night to blind her household to the real condition of affairs! Or is it that she is anxious to—to become a widow ? "</p>
        <p>Lord Champney's countenance fell. He began to think that the tears and caresses that had been lavished on him- during the preceding night by his wife were, after all, but the vagaries of a dream.</p>
        <p>It was strange that always, after discussing the Lady Barbara with her professed champion, Felix Warner, Lord Champney invariably regarded her with both anger and jealousy, and deemed her capable of any iniquity. "It may be," he said, with a groan. "Of course, she never loved me. Can she be so anxious for me to die, that she may marry Effingham' ? " "1 have heard of such things," said Warner, with seeming sorrow. "The Lady Barbara is a high-strung, passionate creature, who does not like to be haulkdd or disappointed. If she likes Effingham, as what you have said proves, then, my poor Sidney, I cannot say anything to comfort you. You must bear your tronble.' "But I won't hear it ! I can't bear it 1 I 'll have that man's blood—the doting villain I When I sent for you, I fared I should be long ill, and I meant you to tight him in my stead, but I am already feeling •better and I 'll avenge my own wrongs!, Hush, Sidney. This agitation is hurtful to you. Has the Lady Barbara been in to see you to-day?' "No—not once?' Warner's brow began to lighten a little. " After all, I am back in good time 1" he thought. "it was well I came." He walked back and forth softly, reflecting upon his several " irons in the fire," and wishing that Saltair were nearer to Loudon, so that he could keep a close watch upon Lord Champney and upon Dora at the same time. Suddenly a bright thought struck him. "Sidney," he exclaimed, halting near the couch, "you have not been to Champney since you came home ?" "No," was the wearily uttered response. "I have not been there in years. It was there I took Barbara after our marriage." "Take her there again, Sidney," said Warner. "At Saltair you are in her domain. Take her to your own house, and perhaps the memory of her first visit there may revive her early love. At any rate, if Effingham should not follow her, you could reasonably suppose that she had broken off with him. Start abruptly, so that he could have no op- portunity to learn her movements save through herself, and then, if he does follow her, judge the worst."</p>
        <p>Lord Chatnpney aroused himself. "A splendid idea," he muttered. "But would . she go ? " "Urge her—command her. Assert your au- thority. Tell her you shall believe her guilty un- less she does go." "I'll do it. I'll tell her in the morning. I can- not see her to-night. Felix, this business wears bn me more than you would think. PerhapS I might have kept her love if I had been wiser at the be- ginning. If I had only brought back her little child when she told me to, it would have been alive</p>
        <p>now, I don't doubt; and, for the child's sake, she would have loved the father. It would have been a bond between us." _ _</p>
        <p>"It would," assented Warner, walking slowly. "She might have loved you for the child's sake. You haven't a pictu,e of the little creature? You couldn't procure one of her?" "No—no. It was too young to have its picture taken. She was only a few months old when she died. I might suggest the features to an artist," added the baron, with a sudden inspiration. "I remember perfectly how she looked—the wee thing. I went out to the Surrey Farm to see her often. Sir Graham Gallagher, our physician—he's knighted now, you know—said he had never seen a lovelier, brighter child than ours. It formed a perfect con- trast to the child of Mrs. Narr—"</p>
        <p>Warner stood transfixed. "To whose child, did you say?" he demanded, hoarsely. "To the child of her nurse, Mrs. Narr."</p>
        <p>Warner caught at the back of a chair for sup- port. "Was that the name of your child's nurse? he asked, in a shrill whisper. "Yes. It was Narr—Catherine Narr." "By Heaven ! " Lord Champney was amazed. Warner's emotion was so excessive that his lordship could only stare at him mutely. " Narr !" repeated Warner, turning his ghastly face away. "...Varr!" "Yes. But what is the matter, Felix? Is it the name affects you so? Are you ill?" "A sudden faintness—that is all." Warner sat down at the open window, hiding his face behind the folds of the curtain. " There ! I'm better," he said at length. "Go on." "But what affected you so? The name—" "I never heard the name but once before," said Warner. "It was at Havre. A drunken English- man, named Narr, was stabbed in a fight with a German sailor, and was taken to the hospital. I was called upon to appear at the inquest. It was a horrible experience, and 1 can never think of it without a filing of deathly sickness." , This fictipn, coined on the spot to account for his extremt, agitation, was uttered vith seeming honesty which sufficiently imposed on Lord Champney. "It IC a. 3 an unpleasant experience," he said. "I don't wonder you don't like to recall it. The name is not usual. That man might have been the hus- band of Catherine Narr. I understand he was a dissolute man. The woman Narr had beenmaid to Sir Graham's wife, and he had every confidence in her, else I should never have trusted her with my little one. Yet the woman was a pleasant, decent looking country person, clean, and well- mannered. But, as I remember her, she had a pair of bold back eyes, hard and glittering like jet beads."</p>
        <p>Warner shivered. He remembered those eyes, as he had seen them that morning. "The children were unlike in appearance, I think you sid, Sidney 1" he remarked. "They were very unlike. My child had a deli- cate, dainty beauty peculiarly her own. Her fea- tures resembled mine in some part—Barbara's in others. She had soft, large eyes, like the wood voilet in colour, and limpid as a forest spring. The other child, Mrs. Narr's, was a stout, sturdy, roly- poly infant, with its mother's bead-black eyes, and its father's red hair. She must be an odd looking creature if she has lived !" _ _</p>
        <p>"You do rict think the Narr girl would ever have become a dainty, high bred, slender creature, with a keen intellect, a proud and noble nature, and a great generous soul ? " asked Warner, in a low tone. _</p>
        <p>Lord Charnpney opened his eyes wide. " Impossible !" he ejaculated. ',The girl might make a good woman, but never such a person as you describe. Why, the fine nature was lacking. The parents did not have it, and bow then could they transmit it to their offspring ? If the girl had been brought up away from her parents, and in an intellectual atmospherer-still her nature would be only modified, not changed." "True—true !" assented Warner, still in his low, strange voice. "Education may modify, but it cannot utterly change. By the way, Sidney, talk- ing of a picture of that 'poor, little, dead creature, whom I never saw, but over whose untimely death I mourn with you, I should thinkwe migh tobtain one that would do to give to Barbara. You know how your spoke of the 'little grave between' you, on the day you came here. I believe I could paint the picture myself. Was there anything peculiar about her? Any mark ?" "Her face was perfect, but on the right arm above the elbow, there was a little birth-mark, shaped like an irregular cross. Of course that need not be depicted,. I should never have noticed, the mark, but that Sir Graham called my attention to it, and made some jocular remarks to the effect that the child would do for a heroine, nature having provided her with a mark for identification. Poor little oen ! I hope that her life has unfolded in a brighter land than this—like a delicate flower removed to a more genial climate," and his lordship sighed. "An irregular cross on the right arm above the elbow," muttered Warner to himself, inaudibly. Lord Champney neither heard nor heeded his kinsman's words. He was recalling inh is own mind the picture of the fair and sweet little child that had never lain on its mother's bosom.</p>
        <p>"It was hard for poor Barbara," he thought. "I cannot wonder that she/hates me." There was a silence between the two for awhile, and then Warner said: "Your girl would be about seventeen now, if she had lived, would she not Sidney ?" Lord Champney replied in the affirmative. "Have you ever formed any idea of how she would have looked ?" _</p>
        <p>"Yes," said the baron, dreamily. I often think of her as she must look now in heaven. She has shy, violet eyes, a pure, sweet face, male glorious by the nobleness and grandeur of her soul, and a lovely mouth, radiant with a sweet and steady Smile. She is tender and bright and beautiful—my little lost Barbara 1" "I have seen a face such as you describe," said Warner, half unconsciously, thinking of Dora. "I know just such a being as you have depicted." "Who is she? Where is she ?" Warner looked up startled. He had spoken without thought. "I was speaking of a young lady I expect to marry," he answered. "You must not allow me a little lover's license, Sidney. Of course I think Miss ChePsom an angel." Lord Champney smiled. Warner seized the opportunity to adroitly turn the conversation, and soon after withdrew on pretence of changing his dusty travelling suit. When he had gained his own room all his sup- pressed excitement burst forth. "I must go back to town immediately ! " he mut- tered, excitedly. "Great heaven! have I flung the gem from me, thinking it paste? The girl liked me. I know she did. Has she ceased to like me since I insulted her? Is she Lord Champney's daughter? I must know. I must see her arm. By George! she shall marry me, if I have to force her into it. I am groping blindfold in a deep mystery, but light has penetrated. I cannot com- prehend the game of those Narrs, but I believe Dora is the heiress of the Champney estates, and that I must turn my attention to her, instead of wasting so much time on her parents. As the husband of the '()Teat Champney heiress, I shall not ha% eto wait for Sidney's death. The only question remaining now is—ls Dora the daughter of Lord Chamimev 1"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-11-14_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>Int (AM ER, SAAJR:I;i4A NOVEMBER 14,1b96</head>
        <p>Int (AM ER, SAAJR:I;i4A NOVEMBER 14,1b96</p>
        <p>CUTICURA REMEDIES are the most wonderful skin cures, blood pur4lers, and humour remedies ever com- pounded. They may be used on the youngest infant and most delicate invalid with gratifying and unfailing success. They afford instant relief and a speedy cure in the most torturing and disfiguring of itching, burning and scaly skin and s calp diseases. They cleanse the system by internal and external medication of every eruption and impurity, and constitute the most effective humour treatment of modern times.</p>
        <p>'-iIEtAPTER XXVII.</p>
        <p>THE LADY BARBARA IS REPULSED.</p>
        <p>On the morning subsequent to Warner's return to Saltair, and after another conversation with hie kinsman which insidiously aroused all his jealousy and bitterness of feeling, Lord Champney sent a message to the Lady Barbara requesting her presence in his chamber.</p>
        <p>A little later, Warner having withdrawn, that the husband and wife might be alone, the baroness obeyed the summons.</p>
        <p>ford Champney was fully attired for the nem sion, and was sitting in his easy chair, but his face was still ghastly in its pallor, and he looked like one who has but just passed through a terrible illness. Ms countenance was grave even to stern- ness.</p>
        <p>As his wife came in, the gloom in his eyes sud- denly lifted—as a heavy cloud parts to let through a rift of light—and he caught his breath sharply, as with sudden pain. It seemed as if the Lady Barbara had never looked more beautiful. Di•spite all his rage at and suspicions of her, she looked to her husband like an angel. •</p>
        <p>She was in her usual morning attire, wearing a floating robe of white—a pretty combination of tucks, rufft,s and lace—with blue ribbons knotted at her throat, and with her hair falling in a casumle of golden curls from her •chignon. Her every movement was, as usual, instinct with grace ; her bearing was regal, and she had a haughty, high-bred air that inight have become an eniiirt.ss, but her great charm lay in her glorrous countenance. It was pale, and the colour came and went with her thoughts, but it was grandly sweet in its imperious Evenness, and there was a aft, tremu- lous light in her eyes, as of a delicious expectancy. It was as if she had come to this meeting with the hope of a complete reconciliation with her hus- band.</p>
        <p>She approached Lord Champney slowly—it almost seemed with the timid shyness of a young girl—and held out her hand.</p>
        <p>The baron arose, advanced a few steps toward her and clasped her hand, a sudden spasm of jealous and passionate love convulsing his soul. " You are looking very ill, Sidney," said her ladyship, with an involuntary expression of con- cern. " You should be in bed—"</p>
        <p>"I am a little weak yet," returned the baron, deprecatingly. "Such an experience as I have been through is not favourable to the possession of strength. lam restless, and want a change. lam tired of Saltair."</p>
        <p>The Lady Barbara turned yet paler. " Saltair is dull," she said, turning away her face. "You must find it intolerable after so long a residence at a gay, foreign court." "It is not that," cried the ex-ambassador hastily. "I do not want gaiety—only peace. I am de- voured by unrest. It gnaws at my heart like a vulture. And yet, I suppose, the peace and rest I long for will be found only in the grave." The Lady Barbara's hand, still resting in his, trembled like an imprisoned bird. He released it, with a sudden consciousness that she was still standing. "Sit down, Barbara," he said, iu an altered voice. "You seem tired."</p>
        <p>He made a movement to push his own chair to- ward her. She declined the civility, and retreated to a seat at a liti' ) distance. Lord Champney sat down again. "Barbara," he said, wearily, leaning his pale cheek on his hand, "I did not ask you here this morning to reproach you for your faults, or to rave at you for your duplicity. It seems to me that I am done for ever with reproaches and rav- ings. The iron has entered my soul at last. But I asked you here that we may have a calm and reasonable talk together. Shall we talk to each other in all calmness, and even with kindness ?" His weary manner, the heart-hungry look in his eyes, the utter dreariness of desolation in his face, emote the heart of the proud and wronged wife with a terrible force.</p>
        <p>"Say what you will, Sidney," she said, her voice quivering in spite of her efforts to control it. "I will listen. Am lon trial again ?" "On trial? No. lam not your. judge, Bar- bara," and a keen gust of mental pain swept over his face, "my heart seems dead within me at this moment. My passions seem to have worn them- selves out at last. They have been stormy enough, and yet, perhaps, this lull is but the effect of physical weakness. I may be as bitter, and jealous, and ugly-tempered as ever to-morrow. Ah We made a terrible mistake, Barkara." "A mistake, Sidney ? " "Yes—our marriage, you know. You and I ought never to have come together. You were too proud; I too jealous and exacting. Yet God knows I loved you, Barbara, better than I loved my own soul." The wife drew her chair a pace nearer. "And I love you still," continued the husband, tremulously. "I love you with a wild, mad love —mad because it is so hopeless and so unreserved. I have poured out my heart and soul at your feet, and you rejected the offering as worthless. Per- haps it is," and he smiled bitterly; "but it was the outpouring of a rich, glowing and tropical nature, which now," he added, mournfully, "seems to be dead under the ashes of a great desolation and woe."</p>
        <p>The wife was silent, but the bitter anguish in his voice thrilled her with an answering pain. "I had that in me," continued Lord Ghampney, "of which kindly, generous, happy men are made. Yet my life is a wreck. And you are the false beacon that has wrecked me."</p>
        <p>"Say, rather, that your own passions have been your ruin," said the Lady Barbara, flushing. "I have been a true and honest wife to you, Sidney, and our unhappiness is all your own fault." Lord Champney covered his face with his hands. After a little, he looked up, and said, with a strange quietness: " I did not mean that this interview should be one of anger and recrimination. I simply wished to say that we made a mistake in marrying each other, and we must bear our trouble as beat we can. I do not like Saltair, and I am going away." "Back to Germany ? " asked the wife, in a •whisper. -" No—to my own home, at Chatnpney Mere—to the old house to which I took you when we were just married. I have been there but seldom since we parted. I may spend the rest of my days there. Will you go with me, Barbara?" The wife started. "I go to Champney Mere?" she exclaimed, in surprise. "Simply to delule people into a conviction of our happines replied Lord Chanipney, bitterly. s," "Simply to cover with a brave outside our un- satisfied hearts. I want you to go in order that the real state of our affairs may not be guessed at. I want you to go because I cannot be happy away from you. I want you to go because I must keep my family name unstained, and people already are talking of 14:tfingliarn'8 devotion to you. For your sake and for mine, I ask you to go with me !"</p>
        <p>The Lady Barbara reflected. Here was a way of escape from Effiughatn's persecutions, and she longed to embrace it. And perhaps, she thought, when they were back at Champney Mere, where the happiest days of their life had been spent, she might win her husband back to his early faith in her. " I will go 1" she answered. Lord Charnpney's face brightened. A sudden glow kindled on his cheeks. "Thank you," he said, simply. "Can you go soon—this week ?" "As well now as any time. Ada can go to town to her Aunt Estway's, as she has thought of doing. The wedding is to be here in September. You will come back for that ?"</p>
        <p>"Yes. Will it be hard for you to go with me, Barbara? You can have your separate rooms at the Mere, as you have them here. I shall never intrude upon you more than Ido here. You shall be mistress of the place, and I will try to make you contented. If you only knew how I love you," he added, with a passionate yearning. "I would die to make you happy False and cruel and treacherous as you have been, Barbara, I love yoi —I love you! Oh, Barbara, my wife 1" His voice broke down in a sob.</p>
        <p>The Lady Barbara's figure swayed a moment, like a young tree in a storm; then, with a sudden and inarticulate cry, she sprang up and ran to her husband's chair, and knelt at his side, bowing her head n his knee. " Ilai bAra, 1" cried the baton, amazed and alarmed at a clihpirty of emotion great.r than he had deemed her cap,.Ole of leeiing. " BArbara, you frighten rnf, !",</p>
        <p>, TAtly tl:cd to .tpeak, but burst into a fit of L. ,? , • • •,, f ce f.;rew ‘chiter, and he began</p>
      </div>
