        <p>To MOTHERS.—Mrs. Wi..slow's Soothing Syrup has been used over fifty years by millions of mothers to theirchildren while teething with perfect success. It will relieve the pout sufferer immediately. It is pleasant to taste ; it produces natural quiet sleep, by relieving the child from pain, and the little cherub awake 3 'as bright as a_button." Of all Chemists, Is. Id. pe bottle.—Advt.</p>
        <p>to tremble. He imagined that his wife wished to confess to him that she loved another.</p>
        <p>" What is it, Barbara ?" he whispered " You wish to—to confess? You wish to tell me that my suspicions are all based on truth? That you love Effingham ?" The Lady Barbara lifted her head with her old pride. Her face was wet with her tears. Through her grief and anguish flashed a ray of scorn. "Do not say such a thing to me again, Sidney," she exclaimed. " Must I protest my innocence again? Listen to me, my husband. Since the day I married you my heart has never once swerved from you. You have been cruelly jealous of me; you have abandoned me for years, condemning me to a life of isolation and loneliness; but never once have I ceased to think kindly of you. I respected your virtues ; I pitied your faults and follies. Let us be sensible. lam willing to overlook the past, and to start' (nal,. Let us begin a new life together." She looke" Tin at nim imploringly, her soul in her eyes. The cup of happiness was within Lord Champ- ney's grasp. He put out his has ar deliberately pushed it from him.</p>
        <p>"Do you come back to me repentant and hum- bled ?" he asked. "Will you deal with me in all honesty and fairness, ceasing to deny that which is proven, but turning from your disgraceful lover with a return of your former virtuous impulses? Will you make to me a complete con- fession of the worst, and lay bare your soul to me! If you will do these things, then, and only then, can we talk of beginning a new life together. I must know all, Barbara, before I can think of again trusting you." The proud, high-souled woman, so noble, so haughty, so sternly virtuous, so lofty and rigid in her notions of propriety, listened to these stinging words in an utter amazement.</p>
        <p>Her eyes widened, her nostrils dilated, her lips quivered in a great and just anger. She arose to her feet, all her fire and glow dying out, leaving her as if turned to stone. " You have rejected my overtures," she said, wi ii a passionate haughtiness. "I shall never make any more. You were right. All is over be- tween us. I will go with you to the Mere, but you need 'never ask me to forgive or forget your cruelty. past all forgiveness." "I do not comprehend you, Barbara. Were you inteluiing j;;.-ir, now, when you knelt by me, to pass yourself ,t 1 on Ice 3 a wronged and innocent woman ?''</p>
        <p>His lordship's voice was full of a bitter sternness and sarcasm.</p>
        <p>The Lady Barbara's face grew white with a sudden scorn.</p>
        <p>"How dare you insult me in this way ?" she demanded.</p>
        <p>"I dare say anything, madam. You forget, or possibly you did not know, that I have had the pleasure of examining the contents of Colonel Eff- ingham's private desk? He has carefully preserved all your letters and gifts—" " It is false ! I have never sent him either."</p>
        <p>His lordship smiled cruelly. " Pcrhaps you will also deny that you sent him a warning of my intention of punishing him? I saw your note sent to him by the gardener's boy, the other day ; so denial is useless." "I shall deny nothing," said the Lady Barbara, with a curling lip, and great, wild, anguished eyes. "You have condemned me without a hearing; but when a judge is prejudiced, I don't know that a hear- ing amounts to anything. But mark me, Sidney, the hour will come when you will know me as I am —an innocent, stainless, honourable woman. And in that horn-, even if you kneel at my feet, I will repulse you as you have repulsed me. I have knelt to you, and you have sneered at and taunted me with crimes I could never commit. You have sent rne from you with revilings. And as you have measured to me, so will I measure back to you." "You would make a good actress, Barbara," remarked Lord Chatnpney, with scornful bitter- ness.</p>
        <p>Her ladyship threw up her arms after an an- guished, hopeless fashion. "it is all over 1" she exclaimed. "I said rightly, when you came here—there is a grave between us! Unless my lost little child—my dead baby that 1 have mourned and wept for is brought back to me, I shall know no peace. And as I shall not meet her in this world there is no happiness for me here. 1 have only that little angel in heaven to look at; but, by the pain here," and she put her hand to her heart, "1 think we shall not be parted much longer. You would not give her back to me in this world, Sidney, but I think she will know me up there. You cannot separate us there."</p>
        <p>The wild anguish in her tones and in her face banished Lord Champney's mocking expression. He began to comprehend that she was not acting—that she was only too terribly in earnest. "Barbara," he said, more gently, "I—" She put up her hands arin, with a wild ges- ture.</p>
        <p>" Don't !" she said, hollowly. "I can bear no more. It is all over between us. I may be able to forgive you in the other world, when you can come to me with your child's hand in yours, and with her sweet face to plead for you, but not till then." She tottered toward the door. _ _ _</p>
        <p>At the same moment a knock was heard, and the butler entered, bearing a bouquet and a letter. _ _ _</p>
        <p>"My lady," he said, presenting them to her, "Colonel Effingham is below, and asks for a few minutes' interview. The flowers and the note are from hi.n." _ .</p>
        <p>The Lady Barbara composed herself by a power. ful effort. _ _</p>
        <p>"You may return the flowers and the note to him, Rug," she said, coldly. "Tell him I have no use for either. Also tell him that I decline now and always to see him." The butler bowed and retired. The Lady Barbara, without another word to her husband, swept from the room. Lord Charnpney looked after her in astonish- ment.</p>
        <p>"Was that acting ?" he thought. "1158 she cast him off? Or is she trying to hookwink me ?" He went to the window and sat down by it, screened by the lace drapery. A few minutes later he saw Effingham ride down the avenue. _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>About midway the colonel encountered Felix Warner, as the latter was slowly sauntering back to the house.</p>
        <p>Effingham raised his bat to \Varner, but Felix coolly and most decidedly cut him, declining to acknowiedgeathe salutation. How couZthe deluded baron, peeping from be- hind the screen of his curtains, imagme that this scene had been arranged between the two villains for his benefit, and that they were both conscious that he was now looking at them? How could he guess that they were in league with each o'.;htr, and playing into each other's hands ?</p>
        <p>HIM heart warmed to his relative. " Felix is a true friend," he muttered, gloomily —" the ouly friend 1 have in the world. Thank Heaven ! 1 have his honest heart to rely on. If he failed me, 1 should lose all !" Felix sauntered on to the hone. _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>Col. Effingham turned in his saddle and looked after Warner with a gaze of pi etended malignancy. "Ho hates the noble fellow," thought Lord Champney. The next mom Pitt Effinpham raised his eyes to the Lady Barbara's whitlows. As by magic hi 3 brow cleared, and he smiled and waved his ho.nd, as if in response to some big nal. _ _ _</p>
        <p>"She was acting, then," muttered Lord Champ- ncy. scowling. "She sent down that message to dc(!e.ive m?., and now she is explaining, or making it up, with Effingham. And I almost believed her."</p>
        <p>He watched Effingham's movements as if fasef- Dated. He saw him pause, as if watching intently some one at her ladyship's windows, ride back a few yards toward the house, appear to listen, bow, smile, wave his hands, and then, wheeling, ride off at a gallop. His lordship grated his teeth. "I am not well enough for that meeting yet," he thought, "but it shall come, Sooner or later ; sooner, if he forces it on me ; later, if I can put it off till I get stronger. lam in no condition to fight now, with my body a mass of bruises. But one thing is certain—one of the lovers of Barbara Champney has got to die soon." He scowled blackly. He was locking a very Nemesis in appearance, a little while after, when Felix Warner strode into the chaFtiber.</p>
        <p>A sly gleam shot into Warner's eyes as he marked the baron's position at the window. " You have not been sitting there long, my lord ?" he inquirtd, with an assumed start. His lordship nodded. "You saw him—Effingham—then ?"_ _</p>
        <p>"Yes. He had the in-solence to send flowers and a letter up to Barbaro in this room, She returned by tia.'!</p>
        <p>Warner smiled pityingly. "Yes,'? he said. "1 saw the Colonel tear UPI* ters, flowers, and all. He seemed not to 101° - what to make of it." "No wonder," returned Lord Champney, bitterly. "By the way, Felix, did you see Barbara at beg window as you came in ?" rn. Wa er assumed an expression of deep coelP" mon.</p>
        <p>"Don't ask me," he said. "You saw her, then, making motions to thsg villain ?" cried his lordship, excitedly. n't "Sidney, don't compel :m Ae to speak. I -0,, think the Lady Barbara guilty of anything than imprudence. She is a very beautiful vioe'.7. and her head is turned by Effingham's devotlu' and flattery. There is no question but that man loves her sincerely. Please don't questa me any further about my opinions. I wantatl think the best of Barbara. lam sure, Sidney, could purchase your happiness by the sacrifice my own, I would do so gladly." I am sure you would, Felix," said Lord Chia' ney, with emotion. The two clasped hands. "If my life is a wreck," continued the bar° after a pause, as Warner walked away, appareon overcome, "yours need not be. You must mart and at once. Did you see Miss Chessom &amp;am your last trip?" "Yes, and I love her more than ever. We ha. little lovers' quarrel, Sidney, and I am longing go back to her and make it up. Poor little el Her father is dead since I came up here first! "Go to her, Felix. You need not stay on account. I will meet Effingham when I am vi enough. And to-morrow, or next day, Barbo and I will go to the Mere. If Effingham follosil us there, he will do so at his peril. Go to-d 0 Felix."</p>
        <p>At this moment the butler entered, with an er velope. "It. telegram for Mr. Warner," he said. Warner seized it and tore it open. It was brief, and said simply: "All right. At Black Cottage." To this message was appended the name of Js4 Narr. Warner's face glowed luridly. "No answer," he said, smiling. The butler withdrew.</p>
        <p>"It's from Dora, Sidney," explained Warne turning to his relative. "Poor little, loving ore ture ! She feels so grieved about our quarrel, th she begs me to come back to her. You are su you can spare me to-day ? " "Quite sure. I should like to see this litt girl of yours, Felix. You must hasten yol marriage. You will join us at the Mere on Sate day ? " Warner assented, and soon after made his adis' and departed. Half an hour later, his brain busy with plottiui be was on his way to Norwich. (To be continued.)</p>
