      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-11-14_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>kIVIERIC A.N P')LITICS, &amp;c., &amp;c.</head>
        <p>kIVIERIC A.N P')LITICS, &amp;c., &amp;c.</p>
        <p>Imetimes think it would be a good thing if an</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-11-14_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <p>A</p>
        <p>LADIES' LETTER.</p>
        <p>MY DEAR NELLIE,</p>
        <p>A favourite flower for millinery just now is the chrysanthemum, and this is as it should be in • season of chrysanthemum shows. You do nob generally perceive the presence of the chrysanthe- mum until the wearer turns her head, as it is only used as a cache peine, on the left side towards the back.</p>
        <p>A very smart bonnet, with a brim of poppy velvet and a crown of fancy tinsel, is appliqued with black velvet designs, and embroidered with sequins. The bows are of black moire ribbon, and black ostrich tips stand up in the front, while a cluster of red poppies appears on the left side. Little boys should be put into tunics as soon as they emerge from baby frocks, and some extremely pretty and serviceable designs can be had. A good one might have a plain saddle yoke, and box- pleated front and back; the tunic portion being in two parts only. If a thick cloth, such as mantle beaver, serge, or Himalaya cloth, is med, it need not be lined, bet all thinner materials must be lined with sateen, the stuff and the lining being cut exactly alike, and pleated up together. One yard and a-half of double width material, and half wyard of velveteen fcr yoke, cuffs, and waistbelt, will be sufficient.</p>
        <p>A fashionable coat for a little girl is made of dark red cloth, with a dual cf...p, \ elvet. An elegant walking gowns tie faced cloth with a vest of white moire, a ja.,,.t ot ecru guipure lace, and paste buttons set in orplisecl silver. A hat to ,:latch is of mastic felt, trimmed with moird ribbons, paste ornaments match the buttons on the coat, and white ostrich feathers. White kid glove and whiteboots, with vamps of tan glace kid, complete the elegant costume,whichis lined through with white satin.</p>
        <p>A very handsome gown might be made in cedar brown cloth, braided in black, and completed by a folded sash of black moire. A lovely gown in white satin has a plain skirt, with rather a long train, and a bodice veiled with creamy guipure lace, which is studded with diamond dewdrops. The shoulder straps of satin have a box-pleating of the same lace hanging from them, which forms a short butterfly sleeve, which partly covers the top of the arm,</p>
        <p>Some of the most stylish seal-skin mantles are made in the new shape, which fits in closely to the figure at the waist and has long full wing sleeves, only, of course, they are not trimmed with jet. Other elegant models in sealskin are all made in one, in the style of a dol- man ; they are .shaped so as to fall in at the waist at the back, with the appearance of a full cape over the arms, and long stole ends in front, hang- ing down on to the skirt. This is a very becoming and suitable shape for a married lady who does not altogether care about a circular cape, and from being held in at the back and weighed down in front by the long ends, it affords much more pro- tection from the cold than the open cloaks which have been worn for some time past. Curly tips are also used for ruffles, and for trimming the high colours of the winter mantles. Indeed, the most radical change in the fashion which has yet become marked seems to be these very high collars, which are seen on all the capes and cloaks, many of them quite Elizabethan in their proportions. The mode is a becoming one, if is is not carried to excess, but feathers and chiffon, or other light and pliable materials, make much prettier collars than velvet and cloth. Long- haired fur is also very becoming, and those who do not care to have a high, full callar permanently on a winter cloak can wear feather and fur boas to get the same effect.</p>
        <p>For black Übe ribbon velvet there is a perfect) passion just now, and the favourite method of its application on the skirts and bodice of the woman. of fashion is in double or triple lines. 'Tis a mode that will soon becom2 e owing to the unfortunate cheapness of the ribbon, too popular to stay, so gather ye roses—or rather, Übe velvet—while ye may. Your loving</p>
        <p>- —N -,9'el•-:' ---.N. \ • 4 .., ,/ $.,"%, , , /-tti!-4. .-"„ -j,..., ' ''. 1 ..' 111‘, ./' !'"At, , --- / - ;',..*: I;C:2llr,c- i , i' .. : V., . \ t`iir t ' i 1 * -....',.: Ahlh'ye ve e ~,,b,, 0 /4 17' ; )S -</p>
