      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-11-14_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>WOMEN'S CHAT.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>As few of Her Majesty's household share thi. Queen's pleasure in early frosts and snowstorms, and the some- what monotonous surroundings of Balmoral, the depar- ture for Windsor was, as usual, hailed with delight. Windsor is the favourite abode ofthe Court officials, for</p>
        <p>THE PUBLIC ARE WARNED against the use of Foreigq cocoas containing alkali, producing highly coloured prepara tions offered as "pure cocoa." Cadkory's Cocoa is abso- lutely pure and unadulterated, and is therefoi e the best. The " Lancet "describes it as" the standard of highest purity at present attainable."—Advi.</p>
        <p>not only is it within easy distance of London, but there is pleasant society in the neighbourhood. The State dining-room at Windsor is one of the finest rooms in the Castle. If there are too many guests for r he "oak-room "where the Queen usually dines, the State dining-room is always used. It is beautifully decorated in cream and gold ,and in this room stands the enormous gilt punch bowl which was made exoressly for. George IV. at the cost of £lO,OOO. It holds 80 gallons, and on the marriage of the Prince of Wales, was filled with 2.1.6 bottles of mullod claret.</p>
        <p>Our Royal Family is so large. and birthdays conse- quently so numerous, that members are in a perpetual state of "give," and the amount spent yearly un presents by some of them, frequently equals the income of a well-to-do citizen. The Prince Of Wales, as is usual with him, received an enormous number of gifts on the 971,n0t only from his relations and friends but also from anonymous donors. His three daughters, as well as the Princess, invariably give their father articles which they have made themselves, and keep each piece of work a profound secret until the auspicious day arrives. The Princess of Wales always chooses her souvenirs herself, but the Queen generally relies upon the artistic taste of Princess Henry of Battenberg. Apropos. no Royal Family in Europe has such a phe- nomenal number of late summer and autumn birthdays as that of her Majesty's second son, the Duke of Co- burg. The Duke's birthday is celebrated on August 6th, the Princess Alexandra's on September Ist, the Crown Princess of Roumania's on September 9th,Prince Alfred's on October 15th, the Duchess of Saxe-Coburg's on Octo- ber 17th, and that of the Grand Duchess of Flessee on November 25th. The only member of the family born in another season,is the youngest, Princess Beatrice,who will be twelve next April. Up to last year Princess Henry of Battenbarg,wifh her sister Princess Louisa, has always arranged the Christ- mas and New Year festivities at Oshorne,bet this season she Will be much missed in the ordering of the house- hold as early in Januaty her Royal Highness purposes leaving England for the Riviera. Her Majesty's grand- daughter, Princess Victoria of Schleswig-Holstein,who is a great favourite with the Queen will,however, join the Court when it leaves Windsor for the Isle of Wight, and probably after the departure of Princess Henry some entertainment will be devised for the Queen's amuse ment.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Faudel Philips, who is a sister of Sir Edward Lawson,the principal proprietor ot the" Daily Telegraph" promifes to be in many respects, an ideal Mayoress. For miles round her beautiful home in Hertfordshire, one hears stories of her goodness to the poor, cl meat din- ners, soup kitchens, and a hundred and one other very substantial kindnesses, to those less fortunate than her- self. Mrs. Faudel Philips, among other hobbies,delights in collecting china. She has no fewer than 127 complete services, as well as hundreds of valuable separate pieces. She has also a celebrated herd of Jersey cattle which have been bred on the estate: and of which their mis- tress is naturally proud. The Lady Mayoress too, is a great lover of dogs. Speaking of dogs, the cropping of the unfortunate animals ears, has been put an en 4 to, and the docking of their tails is likely to follow suit, but there seems to be no end to the cruel devices for" improving" ? dogs, still to be abolished. With regard to collies, " prick " ears are now fashionable and many of them show traces of their ears having been glued together and weighted down at the tips, to bring them down. Surely this pro- longed torture is much more cruel than snipping a bit of the ears once and for all, The Dogs' Toilet Club in the West End of London, which not long since started up, offers its members all facilities for a careful toilet; attendants wash them,dry them ,comb them, brush them, and clip them, when they are clippable, and dress them. Frequently these parn- pered beings have men servants told off te wait upon them, and possess visiting cards, and card cases, in the pockets of their overcoats. Every sort of foolish trick is tried with their natural coats, and the only object of the silly owners seems to be, to make their dogs as un- natural and foolish as themselves. The manufacturers seem to have come to the conclu- sion that whether We like them or not tinselled materials are good for us. They abound just now, in all our lead- ing shops, but whet-her they are really becoming is a somewhat doubtful question. There is no doubt how- ever about the fact of their needing very careful blend- ing with other fabrics, as tinselled materials are so particularly liable to give a common appearance to the whole toilette unless artistically arranged.</p>
        <p>Little folks still favour poplin and plush for their coats and pelisses, and for children of three or four and up- wards' the favourite mantles are loose this winter, as last. They are to been seen in cream, ivory, all shades of biscuit, tan and brown, and the best are lined with Thibet lamb or ermine. The Puritan bonn9ts are usually close and plain, but sometimes they have bright full crowns of silk, satin, or plush. Fashionable folk are now dividing their favors pretty equally between red, and purple, but of the latter colour I would have my readers be aware, as it rarely proves becoming. The best plan whereby to find out what colours really suit us, is to take a bundle of stuffs of every possible shade and standing before the glass h )1d them to the face, noting which suits us best, and then keep to those and those only, whatever the fashion may be.</p>
        <p>The Boleo is still in great request, and as it provides a capital excuse for renovating a middle-aged frock and the exhibition of pretty embroidery and passementerie, and dainty lace, long may it continue so. Some are made straight and hardly fitted to the figure at all, while others are cut so as to set off the curves of the bust to great advantage. What with microbes and other deadly discoveries, we are so used to waking up to the fact that things we love to do and have been in the habit of considering harmless, are fraught with terrible danger,that it comes as a welcome surprise to find the afternoon nap, so dear to many of us, is regarded by the anthorities,as a harm- less and innocent practice. Indeed, not only is the "afternoon nap" considered harmless, but positively beneficial After this optimistic deliverance, the fortun- ate among us, who have the snare time, can indulge in this particular luxury with a clear conscience. -</p>
        <p>Chilblains will soon be very much en evidence if the cold weather continues, and some of my readers may be glad to know that as long at they remain unbroken there is no better application than a cut onion heated at the fire and rubbed well upon the part. After they are broken oxide of zinc ointmen.t generally heals them. Marmalade Pudding.—Take two ounces of ground rice, two ounces of stale bread crumbs, two ounces of finely mince° suet, an ounce of dripping, two table- spoonfuls of marmalade, one gill of milk, and one ounce of sugar. Mix the rice,crumbi,suet and sugar together, add the marmalade, stir well and pour in enough milk to make a stiff mixture. Place in a well-greased basin, covered tightly with a greased paper, steam for two hours and serve with sifted sugar.</p>
        <p>" MADGE."</p>
        <p>The German Embassy has sent to Lord Salisbury a communication intimating that the Emperor William has been pleased to award to Captain Henry Orchard, of London, master of the steamer Tafna, a gold watch and His Majesty's picture as a recognition of the assistance rendered to the crew of the ship Athena, of Bremen, when in distress. The Mayor of Gloucester has been asked to make the presentation on behalf of the German Government when Captain Orchard arrives in that city.</p>
        <p>HOOD'S PILLS cure constipation and Her ills. Sold by chemists. Insist upon Hoop's. is. lid,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-11-14_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>FXAMINER, SATURDAY, NOVEMBER 14. 1896.</head>
        <p>THE FXAMINER, SATURDAY, NOVEMBER 14. 1896.</p>
        <p>ORDERi AND ARROWS,</p>
        <p>When the captain of a ship orders some hands aloft to furl the :min royal the men jump to obey, as a matter of course. A sailor can climb up on a yard without hav- ing a shilling ashore or a penny in his pocket. In fact, J:ick seld.rn 312n5 articles until he has used up both cash and credit.</p>
        <p>hut when a doctor—who is a sort of captain wren one is laid uo in tin dry dock of illness—orders a patient to go abroad for the benefit of his health,it is quite another thing. A trip and sojourn away from home is an expen- sive prev,iption, and most of us can't afferd it. If .tb.q doctor s” it is a choice between that and the gralie- yard we shall have to settle on the graveyard ; it is handy by, and easy to get to. But are we really so•hard pushed P That is, as often as the doctors say we'are P Let's turn the matter over in our minds for a minute.</p>
        <p>Hero is a case that is pat to the purpose. It concerns Mr. Arthur Whiddon Melhuish, of 3, Regent's Terrace Polshe Road, Exeter ; and for the details we are in- debted to a letter written by him, dated March 7th, 1893 He confirms that, in obedience to the orders of his doctors. hP went to Cannes, in the South of France, in November, 1890, and spent the winter there. He also spent the following winter at the'same'place. He felt the better for the change; we will tell you why presently. Rut he nbtaieed no radical benefit, which also we will explain later on.</p>
        <p>It appears that this gentleman had been weak and ailing nearly all his life; not exactly ill, not wholly well —a conditiam that calls for constant caution. In March, 1890, he had a severe attack of inflammation of the lungs. Now I want the reader to honour me with his best attention, as I triUst say in a few words what ought pro- perly to take many. Shoot an arrow into the air—as straight up as you can. You can't tell where it will fall It may fall on a neighbour's head, on your own,or on a child's, or on the pavement. Everybody's blood contains more or less poisonous elements These are arrows, but unlike your wooden arrow they always strike on the weakest spot, or spots, in the body. If they hit the muscles and joints we call it rheu- matism and gout; if they hit the liver we call it Ii ver complaint or biliousness ; if they lit the kidn.ys we call it Br ight's disease: if they hit the nerves we call it nervous prostration, epilepsy, or any of fifty other nathes ; if they hit the bronchial tubes we call it bronchiti;, &amp;C. ; if they hit the air cells we call it inflammation of th* Zen s, or by-and-by, consumption. And inasmuch al these poisoned arrows pass through the delicate meshes of the lungs a thousand times every day it would be odd if they didn't hit them—wouldn't it?</p>
        <p>New, wait a bit; It follows that all the various so- called diseases above named are not diseases at all in and of themselves, but merely symptoms of one only disease —namely, that disease latch produces the poison ! Good. We will get on to the end of the story. After the attack of lung inflammation Mr. Melluish suffered from loss of appetite, pain in the chest, sides, and stomach, and dangerous constipation. He could eat only liquid food and had to take to his bed. For weeks he was so feeble that he could not rise in bed. He con- sulted one ph 5 sician after another, obtaining no more than temporary relief from medicine. Then he was ordered abroad as we have stated. His letter concludes in these words : " Whilst at Cannes I consulted a doctor, who said my ailment was weak digestion, and that I need not trouble about my lungs. But I never gained any real ground until Novem- ber, 1891,when I began to take Mother Seigel's Curative Syrup. This helped me in one week, and by continuing with it I got stronger and stronger, and am now in fair good health. This, after my relatives thought I should never recover. (Signed) Arthur Whiddon Melluish."</p>
        <p>To sum up: This gentleman's real ailment was indi- gestion and dyspepsia, from which the blood poison comes that causes nearly all disorders and palm. The air of Southern France helped him temporarily, because it is</p>
        <p>milder than ours ; it did not remove the poison. By</p>
        <p>care and the use of Mother Seigel's Curative Syrup he would have done better at home, as the result shows. So we see that it isn't the climate that kills or saves ; it is the condition of the digestion. If therefore your doctor orders you abroad for your health, tell him you will first try Mother Seigel's Curative Syrup.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-11-14_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>AROUND THE MEDITERRANEAN.</head>
        <p>AROUND THE MEDITER- RANEAN.</p>
        <p>BY DORA M. JONES. --- 11. A GLIMPSE OF MOROCCO. S.S. Midnight Sun.</p>
        <p>"Tangier," said a wandering Ulysses. who happened to be on board with us, "is the dittiest, filthiest place on the Mediterranean." It looked like a vision of fairy- land, or old romance, from our anchorage in the bay— the fortified quay, the white-walled, flat-roofed,houses one above another, and the citadel wall surrounding it, all under a sky of blazing blue. We landed on the little quay, which had nothing on it but sacks of timber, and tried to realise that Tangier was once an English pos- session, part of the dowry which Catherine of Braganza brought to Charles H. At that time it was notorious for its unhealthiness and as the training school of military ruffians. It was the Tangier dragoons who were let loose on Somersetshire after Monmouth's re- bellion. Now the climate is considered so fine that, if other things were equal, Tangier would rival Algiers as a health resort; but there is small trace of the influ- ence of any civilised power in this Mohammedan city, the most purely Eastern that we were to see west of Cairo.,</p>
        <p>Emerging from the network of narrow lanes among which we had been wandering, we came:out on a plateau close to the prison, from which a magnificent view of the town and harbour is to be had. It is here, we were told, that the logging and other public executions take place. I could not help thinking what a mockery this lovely scene must seem to the eyes of tortured wretches brought here to suffer what the Oriental calls justice. The character of the penalties inflicted aßpears from the number of handless and footless creatures who crawl about Tangier, showing their mutilated stumps and craving for tharity. We went into the prison vestibule where two or three fat Moors were standing or squatt- ing about, and guns were hanging on the walls. By one or two loopholes we were enabled to peep into the com- mon prision, which is a large, bare room fairly lighted, and in which the occupants can move about as far as the long chains attached to the feet permit. As soon as they found out that they were being locked at, they all came rushing to the peephole with loud d( mends for alms. The government does not think itself bound to supply them with food, so that those who have no private means would starve were it not for the alms they re- ceive. Even justice between man and man is unknown here-; the only logic which the judge recognises is a bribe, and if it is not forthcoming the accused man may languish in prison till death puts a term to his suffering. The various consuls have their own police force to pro- tect the interests of their own countrymen, or life would be hardly worth living in Tangier for a European. As it is there is a considerable colony of artists and invalids, occupying the pretty villas on the slopes of the hill out- side the town.</p>
        <p>One of the great sights of Tangier is the open-air market, just outside the Bab-el-Sok, or market gate. Rere the. Arab sets up three sticks, over which he stretches a bit of dirty canvas, and under this primitive shelter sets out his goods on the ground, and squats in the middle of them. Hordes of laden donkeys wander among these tents. ,The driver generally puts a sack on each side of his donkey, and another sack on the top, on 'which he finally. mounts and sticks on as he can. Very often the poor animal is scarcely Visible under hid load of merchandise. The Moorish women do not walk at all in the streets here • but the J.wish girls and matrons go about freely unveiled, and very beautiful some of them are. _ _ _</p>
        <p>After calling at the bazaar and getting a cup of tea at the hotel,we started for the quay. It was getting rather late, and the Tangerines in charge of the boats declared .it to be impossible to bring them up to the beach as the tide was down. We had therefore to be carried out to the boats, for which our Arab friends demanded exorbi taut backsheesh, and then departed with wild gyratia ns of triumph. , Our boatmen also sent the bat round b etween the shore and the ship, and threatened to keep us there indefinitely if their demands were not complied with. We were a large boatful,how- ever, and we made them hear reason. Two of our com- panions.who had co me off in a little boat by themselves, were held at ransom...by the two picturesque ruffians who rowed them' and 'r=o kept paddling them round and round at a hopeless distance from the ship until they handed over the amount demanded. At last, how ever, we found ourselves in our floating home, and free to re- volve in our astonished minds the innid ents of the strangest day some of us had ever spent. lII.—SEVILLE AND CORDOVA. S.S. Midnight Sun.</p>
        <p>We had only time for a glance at whitewalled Cadiz as it lookqd from the harbour before joining the sp,cial train which was waiting for us at the station to take us to Seville. Our way lay over a slightly undulating plain</p>
        <p>Icaked into dust by the long drought, with an occasional stretch of olive garden. The prevailing tone of the land- scape was red brown, varied with the gray of thc olives and the glaring white walls and red-tiled roof S of an occasional village. Close to the platform at one railway station there was a curious circular well, with a chain and bucket, and a cask by the side of it. Behind this was a hedge of bamboo and cactus and prickly-pear. o', the road stood one of the carts of the country, con- sist ing of the flat frame to which the wheels were at- tached, and a row of upright sticks planted in it all round for the sides. A man in a straw hat and striped cotton suit was leaning against the well, as if he had been there a thousand years, and there was no reason why he should not stay on for another tkousand, and in the distance the bare brown.sheep downs stretched away to a line of low red hills on tie horizon. In this little vignette—it may • ,steem fanciful—l read the spirit of Spain, the gipsy.of nations, scorning the aims and re- straint S of an industrial age,preferring to all allurements of material progress her desert freedom, and the time to dream over her splendid past. About mid-day we reached Seville, and here the con- trast was extreme between the arid country through which we had been travelling all the morning, and the gay and smiling scene to which we were suddenly intro- duced. If on our journey we had been reminded of the Spain of the Zingaro and the guerrilla, the streets of Seville brought before us another side of Spanish romance—the glories of its chivalrous age, the deeds of , its kings,and the achievements of its artists. The boule- vards along which we drove on leaving the station were wide and planted with trees, but as we came into the town we found the streets very narrow and crooked. The high white houses had their jalousies mostly down on account of the heat. The lower windows were grated, and the graceful iron balconies added much to the effect of the streets, more especially as they were in most cases filled with flowers or climbing plants. Here and there a group of girls looked out on us from an open terrace or loggia. As we drove after lunch through the artisans' quarters,we saw the women sitting on the door- steps with their children about them. These families live in fits or single rooms, the common 'stairs leading up direct from the door ; but in the better class of house the vestitule opens through a grated door into a large court or patio, generally with a fountain in the centre, with which the rooms on the ground floor communicate, and with a gallery running round it, into which the sleeping rooms open.</p>
        <p>On our way to the Plaza de Toros we had a glimpse of the quays lined with handsome warehouses, and the light iron bridge spanning the Guadalquiver, which, as we saw it, did not resemble the "blue and rushing" stream of the Spanish ballads, being of a dirty green and rather slup„gish-looking than otherwise. The famous scene of bull fights, was, without and within, nothing more than a well-built circus. Huge bills were pasted up on the entrance announcing next Sunday's perform- ance, and giving details of the principal artists. We went in at the performers' entrance, and through the stables where the poor horses, who were so soon to be "butchered to make a Spanish holiday," were tied each to his own manger, one hoped in placid unconsciousness of the fate reserved for them. The interior of the Plaza is a huge amphitheatre, with the royal box in the centre ef the row of seats. Opposite to this is the entrance for the bull. A narrow passage leads from this door to the dark hole in which heis kept with- out food or drink till the hour comes to bring him out to the people. The whole thing reminded one of the gladiatorial combats of old Rome, even to the march past of all the performers with which the procceedings open. Chubs, picadors, banderillos, and matadors, arrayed in their gay and fantastic garb, all walk in pro- cession round the arena with special obeisance to the royal box, and the " Morituri vos salutamus "is not out of place,for it often happens that human life is sacrificed in this deadly game. There is a double palisade round the arena, and a step about a foot high runs round the inner barrier. If a toreador is pursued by the bull his one chance is to leap the barrier with the aid of this step, and if he fails can scarcely escap3 without serious injury, or even loss of life. It is this atmosphere of danger and blood which gives the peculiar zest to this entertainment for those who witness it,and who seem in this respect, as in some others, to have preserved, more than any of the Roman nations, the character of the old Roman populace.</p>
        <p>Some of our party visited the immense tobacco fac- tory behind the Alcazar Gardens. Some five thousand women are employed here, and we were told that they shouted with laughter at the sight of the English knickerbockers. The Seville papers, who devoted a long paragraph to our party, remarked of the ladies of our party, with Spanish gallantry, that some were very fine and some vtry handsome, but appeared lost in wonder at the brilliance and variety of the bats worn by the party. Alas, the glory of our apparel was considerably dimmed by the time we had finished our invasion of the Peninsula.</p>
        <p>About eight o'clock in the evening we arrived at Cor- dova and found the whole population of the place ap- parently waiting to welome us. We drove at a foot's pace to our hotel, the " Suiza," through a dense crowd of Cordovans, who saluted us with yells of admiration or derision, we could not make out which. Even aft,r we had been allotted to our rooms and sat down to din- ner, we were watched from the dining-room windows by a large concourse of spectators. But, as our leader, Mr. Perowne pointed out in one of his happy little speeches, we formed the largest English force that had invaded the Peiainsula since Wellington's time, so that the attention we received, though flattering and a little embarrassing, was not much to be wondered at. We had heard so much Of the melancholy and deserted aspect of Cordova in comparison with Seville that it was rather a surprise to us to find so much appearance of life as we did about the place. The leather trade,which was the staple industry of Cordova in the old days, and from which the word " cordwam "is derived, seems still to be carrien on. There wore several handsome streets with geed shops, many of which had no fronts at all, only curtains which could be drawn at will. There were endless "bits" to admire in the streeta as we passed through, quaint carved gateways and picturesque minarets. But the glory of Cordova as a centre of letters and arts is gone for ever, and it was hard to realise that here in the tenth century was the ()Teat seat of Arab learning, the focus of a great and splendid civilisation. The Cathedral remains of course, in spite of its Chris- tianisation. the great monument of Moorish power in Cordova. Externally it is unimposing, with its low yellow battlemented walls, but in this it only resembles all the work of the Moors, who seemed to have cared nothing for the outward effect of their buildings in com- parison with the interior decoration. One enters this wonderful structure through a court of oranges some- what resembling that at Seville, with a beautiful foun- tain in the middle built lay Said Ben Ayab in 937. Pass- ing through you find yourself under a vast domed and vaulted rod, supported on an endless forest of columns, of every conceivable mateaial and d..-Aign,Greek, Roman Christian, of marble and porphyry and lapis and chisel- led stone, brnieht from all parts of tke world by Moslem conquerors to adorn this glorious farm of theirs. And islanded in the heart of it, as it were, with an incongruity which increases the magical charm and wonder of the whole, le the gorgeous high altar and choir, ere:ted after the conquest of the city by the Christian kings of Spain Gleams of rosy and golden light fall. from the stained windows on the long lines of pillars, at the end of each colonnade, where the eye loses itself in the warm per- fumed darkness, the lights and gliding of a private chapel gleam out of an arched recess, and if you look above you see the marvellous Arabic frotwork. with its wealth of delicate detail covering roof and arches and ceiling. We heard high mass that mornin.,and it is im- possible to convey in words the effect of the Latin chants as they echoed along those endless arcades, and the voice of the organ, now pealing triumphantly, now low and infinitely pathetic, in the heart of this Moslem temple. After the cathedral, the (Treat lion of Cordova is the magnificent bridge,built in 719,and looking,with its mas- sive towers at either end and enormous buttresses, as if it had been built for eternity. The river was flowing languidly in a half-dry becl,for it was the end of the dry season, and we had a good view of the Moorish mills down by the banks. Beyond the bridge the country stretched away wild and parched and rugged, with a white flat-topped house gleaming mut here and there and a little garden-patch about it.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-11-14_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>CROOKED PATHS Of TRADE.</head>
        <p>THE CROOKED PATHS Of TRADE.</p>
        <p>Fortunately for this country of ours, which Napoleon I contemptuously called a nation of shopkeepers," dis- honest traders are in the minority; nevertheless they are quite nunserous and mischievous enough to justify a standing protest against the :very common rractice of substituting preparations—imitations in outward appearance or in name, for well-known articles in con- stant dNiland. Proprtetors of known brands or designa- tions are compelled to maintain a constant warfare against substitutor?. The greater the merit and the success of the genuine, the greater the immediate profit of the substitutor of an inferior and cheaper Article. Honest dealers are coltent to supply the genuine goods asked for ; they do not attempt to sell inferior and cheaper goods for the sake of larger profit. Amongst successful remedies of late none has been made the means of greater abuses than" Syrup of Figs," a name origin- ating wth the California Fig Syrup Company, tke sole proprietors, to whom alone the process of manufacture is known. The words" Syrup of Figs "are no longer a guarantee of genuineness ; and intending purchasers are therefore earnestly requested to ask for and insist on having California " Syrup of Figs," bearing the name and Trade AlArk of the California Fig Syrup Company, thus indicating the genuine and original Syrup of Figs. "Syrup of Figs" is pleasant to the taste, mild and gentle in action, prompt and reliable in its results. It cleanses the system thoroughly and leaves the stomach in a perfectly healthy condition. The good it does is permanent. But you must be sure you get "Syrup of Figs," manufactured by The California Fig Syrup com- pany; other preparations will be wholly disappointing. Price ls. lid. and 2s. 9d. Depot, 32 and 33, Snow Hill, London, B.C.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-11-14_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>CHAT WITH A FAMOUS FOOTBALLER.</head>
        <p>CHAT WITH A FAMOUS FOOTBALLER.</p>
        <p>Mr. JonN ROBERTSON, a miner, is one of the leading players in the Shettleston Swifts Football Club. At his house at Barrocknie,near Glasgow, he told a story intet- eating to all footballers. He said, "I am 26 years of age,and at the age of 20 I was a prominent member of the football club. One day I was engaged iu a match, and I sustained a nasty twist, which I think was got by my trying to save myself. I came *me from the match,and I felt a stinging pain in my back, but like all football players paid no heed to it,thinking that I would be all right in a few days. But I got so bad that I had to cease work and see the doctors—l had four—but my case baffled them. A lump came on my back like a large marble. The doctor shook his head and said, Poor lad, you have spinal disease, and I would advise you te go into the infirmary.' Well, I was sent to the Glasgow Infirmary, and by this time I could only just crawl about. The pain I suffered was beyond descrip- tion. Sir, I lay for eighteen weeks in the infirmary,and after that they despatched me home as incurable. My folks were communicated with,and poor John Robertson was driven home to die! My jaws were sunken, my legs were merely bones covered with skin. What was to be done P The highest skill in the infirmary had been baffled.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-11-14_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>I WAS AN INC gRABLE.</head>
        <p>I WAS AN INC gRABLE.</p>
        <p>My mother, there, spent £l5O in trying to get me cured.</p>
        <p>"But, to continue my story," said Mr. Robertson, "the doctors plainly said, • Your case is quite incur- able,' At nights I lay moaning. My limbs were power- less, but I had my faculties, and when lying in bed there was always leaping into my mind an article I had once read in a newspaper about Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People curing a man at Lochgilphead, Argyllshire. I remembered that he too had a hump growing on his back, and I was wondering .ind wondering if I shouldn't give Dr. Williams' Pills a chance. I lighted upon the case of Little Nell ' in Glasgow, and I read every detail of her suffering eagerly, and when I had come to the last word of it. I threw up my hands and cried:' Yes, that's ray case! I'll give Dr. Williams' Pink Pills a trial.' I sent for several boxes, and I began with two Pills after every meal, then I tried three, and, though I had no power in my legs, I saddenly felt a spring come into them, just as if the blood were returning to them. I felt a sensation of life returning to my limbs, and for three months I persisted, taking the Pills regularly. I got to be able to stand between two chairs. Then one day I was able to let my whole weight go upon my legs, and I managed to walk half a yard with my hands rest- ing on the tops of the chairs. In a day or two I was able to walk the length of the room. After a while I was able to walk on crutches ; but even them I was soon able to abandon. And, sir, it was Dr. Williams' Pink Pills, and them alone, which wrought the marvellous change in my case." "And you are still able to take a walk now ?" " Walk ! Why, en Saturday I tramped seven miles, and could have gone twice the distance." Mrs. Robertson added : " Yes, sir, Dr. Williams' Pink Pills saved my son's life. Had it not been for them he would have been away to a land where sugaring is unknown."</p>
        <p>"To show you that I can wallr."Mr. Robertson added "I shall be happy to accompany you to the railway station."</p>
        <p>He walked faster than the reporter who had inter- viewed him, and on the way said—" There is another friend of mine who was hurt at sea, and lost the power of his lower limbs. He was home a year. Then he commenced to take Dr. Williams' Pink Pills,and now he is back t 3 sea, and as strong as ever he was."</p>
        <p>Dr.Williams' Pink Pills have cured numerous eases of paralysis, locomotor ataxy, spinal disease, rheumatism, and sciatica; also of diseases arising from impoverish- ment and vitiated humours of the blood, which cause scrofula, rickets, chronic erysipelas, consumption of the bowels and lungs, anemia, pale and sallow complexion, general muscular weakness,loss of appetite, palpitations, pains in the back, nervous headache, early decay, all forms of female weakness, and hysteria. These Pills are not a purgative. They are genuine only with the full name, Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People, and are sold by chemists and by Dr.Williams' Medicine Com- pany, 46, Holborn-viaduct, London, E.G., at Zs. 9d. a box, or six for 13s. 9d. They are unrivalled as a tonic for both sexes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-11-14_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>BRIGHAM YOUNG AS AN ECONOMIST.</head>
        <p>BRIGHAM YOUNG AS AN ECONOMIST.</p>
        <p>It seems that Brigham Young, the Mormon leader, has not had his due as a great practical economist. A sinker in the " Atlantic Monthly," who regards Utah as" an industrial object-lesson," declares that the State has always been prosperous, because it has adhered to the principles formulated fifty years ago by its founder. When the persecuted Mormons fled to Utah they found the country to be an arid desert, with a soil almost too hard for the plough to penetrate it. Brigham Young saw at once that nothing but hard work and artificial irriga- tion could reclaim the land for cultivation. To make his followers work, he divided the first settled district into small holdings, giving no one family more than twenty acres, and insisting that each colonist should, as far as possible, supply all his wants from the produce of his own fields. But to bring water from the rivers and lakes to the land he planned a co-operative irrigation society, in which everyone was encouraged to take shares, proportioned to the size of his holding. The colonists were all poor, and had nothing but their labour to contribute to the society. Thus the State was founded on the basis of individual ownership of land and collective ownership of public works. Later on, when stores and banks became noces- sary to the community, they too were organised on co- operative lines. Both the individual settler and the coin- munity were economically independent, and despite its isolation in the deserts of the remote West, the State flourished from the first. Speculation in land, which has been, to their sorrow, the guiding principle of many American States, was discoutenanced, and the habit of trusting to one crop for sustenance, at the risk of a fall in the marketprice or of an unfavourable season, as in the corn-growing States, was unknown. At the same time, every settler had a direct interest in the welfare of the community, and shared in the unearned increment which came of the growth of the population and the cor- responding rise in the valueof land. This explains why there are fewer .mortgaged farms, a higher percentage of children attending school, and a lower percentage of ill- iterates—the two best and simplest testsof relative pros- perity and culture—in Utah than in any other State of the Union. That Utah has attained so high a position in fifty years is attributed in great measure to the teach- ing of the much-abused Brigham Young.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-11-14_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>HOOTED BY BOYS FOR FOUR YEARS.</head>
        <p>HOOTED BY BOYS FOR FOUR YEARS.</p>
        <p>SAD SUICIDE. Dr. Wynn Westcott, coroner, held an inquest at Hack- ney Coroner's Court, on Monday respecting the death of Kate Jones, aged 16, a laundry girl.—Emily Siddons,the wife of a carpenter, said that she had .had charge of the girl since she was eight months old, her mother being dead. The witness knew of no reason why she should have committed suicide, except that the boys in the neighbourhood were always hooting her and calling her " fire ornament."—Alice Godfrey stated that she was with Jones on the night of the 26th of October. Jones ' then said that she was going to drown herself, as she could not stand the boys hooting her so much. It had been going on for four years, and she could not put up with it any longer. The witness tried to dissuade her from it,but the girl turned round and went off in a hurry. Other evidence showed that the girl was seen standing on the banks of the Lea, near her home, at 9-45 p.m., hut was never seen alive again.—George Harvey, a schoolboy, stated that on Friday last, about five o'clock. while dragging for his hoop, which had fallen into the water, he hooked up •he body of the deceased.—Dr. Marshall stated that death was due to drowning.—The jury returned an open verdict.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-11-14_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>VENEZUELAN DISPUTE.</head>
        <p>THE VENEZUELAN DISPUTE.</p>
        <p>The Venezuelan Bounlary Commissioners have issued a statement in which they say that in view of the probab- ility of the boundary dispute being settled by arbitration at an early date they do not propose to formulate any decision for the present on the matters subjected to their examination. A New York telegram says that the arbi- tration of the dispute •is a settled fact, and that the treaty which has been entered into between Great Britain and the United States, while applying in particular to Venezuela, incluies a general agreement to settle by arbitration all future disputes between the two great English-speaking nations. The Venezuela Arbitration )mmission is to consist of five members—two to be named by Great Britain and two by the United States, and the fifth to be selected by these fo Ir. Venezuela is not to be directly represented. The treaty fixes: sixty years of undisputed possession as the test of conclusive presumption of rightful occupancy.</p>
