      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-11-14_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>D. Tudor Williams, MEDICAL HALL, ABERDARE.</head>
        <p>D. Tudor Williams, MEDICAL HALL, ABERDARE.</p>
        <p>SHIP CANAL.</p>
        <p>LAST WEEK'S TRAFFIC. Daring the week ended Friday, November 6, 111 sea- going carat, vessels, with an aggregate rut registered tonnage of 41,492 tons, arrived at and cleared from the port of Manchester, as Col I/pared with 150 vessels, of 38,408 tons in the c wesponding week of last year. Thirty vessels of 23,532 tons, entered from or cleared for forrign ports, and 81 vessels, of 17,960 tons, arri-ed or sailed coastwise. Five steamers arrived frarr. the Baltic with timber, pulp, paper, grain, iron, Src. ; three steamers from the United States with 7,640 bales o. cotton, 200,000 bushels of grain, and general cargo ; lye steamers from the Con- tinent with miscellaneous -.:argos ; one steamer with fruit from Spain, and a barque with timber from British North America, Five steamers cleared 'for the Continent, three steamers and a barque for the Baltic, one steamer and two banjoes for the Brazils, and one steamer each for Barcelorik, A!exandra, and Bombay. A dense fog hung aver the canal during the greater part of the week, but no casua.ties to shipping were reported</p>
