        <p>THE EXA NUN EE SATURDAY, NOVEMBER 14th, 1896</p>
        <p>t</p>
        <p>Tan "Ninth of November" The Guildhall speech of the Prime Minister doco . Speech. not provide any sensation thi9 year. Lord SALISBURY deals chiefly with tvi° topics, namely, the Venezuelan controversy vritb the United States and the Armenian question. The announcement that the Venezuelan controversy ill at an end has been received with general satisf tion. The Prime Minister refers to it as a"not ve , important controversy," but he is about the onl statesman in this country who has dealt with tl , subject in that light and rather cor.temptuej fashion. However,the main thing is that a friendli agreement . has been .arrived at between thl country and the United States. Like most othti similar compacts between two nations the sole tion of the difficulty in the present case is in tb nature of a compromise. The demand of til Venezuelans, backed up by President CLEY: was that the whole ot the . territory in dispill should be submitted to arbitration. The Goveo *tent of this country objected to the propos:3l from the other side of the Atlantic on the grouo, that British subjects who had settled on the bateable ground, on the assumption that the would be subject to and protected by British la might be transferred against their will to Velif zuela which has not always the advantage of ° settled rule. The compromise that has been of; ranged is a tolerably simple one. On the one hair all the territory in dispute will be submitted t° arbitration, but then on the other hand if it ci°l be proved to the arbitrators that any oi this tef: ritory has been settled upon or used by it party for a period of ,fitty years it BOY be decided that it belongs to such WO Two arbitrators are to be appointed b, Great Britain and two by the Unit' States, whilst a fifth will probably be appoint' by an independent thirdparty, such as the Pr: dent of the Swiss Confederation or the 'Lug ' Sweden. It is understood, however, that t' treaty will cover a great deal more ground th the mere settlement of the Venezuelan difficul' It is believed that it will 'provide more or P completely for eicaling with any dispute that 01 ariee between this country and the other grea/ English-speaking nation. If that be so, it d only be described as a great triumph of diPlJi macy. There could be no greater crime agaifr; civilisation than a rratricidai war between Englo and the United States, and if a method has beeti; found whereby that crime may be permaneo averted it may truly be said that Preside° CLEVELAND'S etartlingly bellicose message ' Congress has had a wonderfully., beneficial nOll, In this instance, Lord SALISBURY has done We but it is only fair to add that he has done well' abandoning the attitude he first took up. In t' first instance lie was disposed to insist that United States had no locus standi in this qua between the Venezeulans and ourselves, and t' in any case there could be no arbitration vi 3, respect to territory within what was known as Schinnburg line—that is, an imaginary line el had been faxed by an official:of our own. If ill SALISBURY had inr.exitly held this position 0 quite evident that there woulci have been, soli poor opening for a friendly arrangement, and til 'the not very important controversy" WO . quickly have developed into a very serious ori Sir Virmaam liarrootacr, Mr. Jowl MORLEY '. other Liberal leaders, and also, it should be ' some unofficial Conservatives, warned Lord $ BURY against the false position that he clrifitng into, and these warnings seem to W 4 had the wished-for effect. It was obviousli use recognising the validity of the Monroe de trine and yet at the same time declaring thst</p>
        <p>mLegi</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-11-14_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <p>-United States had no right to intervene on behalf of one of the smaller American States. The essen- tial meaning of the Monroe doctrine is that the thaited States should protect these smaller States from aggression on the part of any European Power, and that doctrine received the assent and support of this country long before the present controversy was heard of. It was also unwise to as- sent to arbitration,and then to endeavour to keep a considerable portion of the disputed land from ever going before the arbitrators. Upon this point, too, Lord SALISBURY has yielded, though he has seemed the right of prescription for land which has been occupied fifty years. On the whole, it will be generally admitted that a treaty of arbit- ration with the United States is well worth all these concessions and a great deal more besides. On the snbject of Armenia Loid SALISBURY was singularly vague and indefinite. Last year when he spoke at the Guildhall he threatened the Sultan with the wrath of England if he did not mend his ways. This " firm and strong" language was, of course, heartily applauded by the Tory Press and from Tory platforms. The country was asked to admire the powerful foreign minister who had re- placed Lord ROSEBERY. But an extraordinary change has taken place during the last twelve months. It seems to be Lord SALISBURY'S nature to begin bravely and to end weakly. The Sultan has, as everybody knows, paid no attention to the Guildhall warnings. He has, in fact, in the meantime destroyed many more thousands of his Christian subjects by the most inhuman methods. What, then, id may well be asked, has Lord SALISBURY to say to this ? Does he announce an intention to proceed from words to action? Not at all. He simply proceeds to show that, though this county may threaten it has not the power to convert its threats into actions. The British fleets, he tells us, can not operate amongst the mountains of Armenia. He ignores the fact that the Sultan does not live amongst the moun- tains of Armenia but at Constantinople, and that Constantinople has been the scene of one of the most atrocious massacres. The whole speech is a plain indication that Lord SALISBURY has utterly failed in his efforts to protect the Armenians and that his brave words of last year were nothing but meaningless born hact.</p>
        <p>IT may be said that the moto-_--ca era will begin to-day in thir</p>
        <p>Motor-Cars.</p>
        <p>. country. Hitherto the law has placed an effectual barrier against the use of these self-propelled vehicles, but at the end of this week the old regulations give place to the new. The revolution in vehicular traffic will be inaugu- rated to-day by a procession of motor-cars from London to Brighton, and this event will no doubt excite a large amount of interest. The Local 'Government Board has issued an elaborate series of regulations which are to apply to the new cars. The highest speed at which the cars will be al- lowed to run is twelve miles an hour, but the heavier motor-cars are still further restricte d,the heaviest of all being tied down to a speed of not more than five miles an hour. A large amount Of good advice is given to the drivers. They must be competent, considerate for others, cautio us,and be ready to at once stop when a policeman lifts his hand. The machine must emit no smoke, and it must not have the trick of starting on its own account. In fact, the regulations issued by the Government Department appear to be over-elaborate, but they are accepted by the pioneers of the new movement as satisfactory for a beginning. Mr. 11. J. L liwsoic, the ;chairman of the Motor-Car Club, thinks ~that the greatest danger in connection with the introduction of motors will he that from frightened horses. The newest motors are, it is said, comparatively noise- less, and will not make more commotion in the streets than ordinary vehicles. According to Mr. LewsoN there is to be a "boom in motoring.," the orders for motor cars being already extremely numerous.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-11-14_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>ENGLISH CHURCH UNION.</head>
        <p>THE ENGLISH CHURCH UNION.</p>
        <p>MEETING AT WARRINGTON. On Tuesday afternoon a general meeting of the mem- bers, associates, and friends of the above union was held in the Co-operative Hall, Sankey-street, Warrington. The meeting was convened for the purpose of consider- ing the various questions coecerning " The maintenance of the Marriage Law against attacks now being made upon it." There was a fair attendance including a num- ber of local clergy and many ladies. The chair was taken by Mr. W. F. Butler, president of St. Aeaph Diocese Dis- triet Union. On the platform were the Revs. J. Bell Cox, W. J. Stanton, F. Slater, T. Outrana Marshall, Mr. J. White, J.P., and Mr. H. N. Houghton. • Apologies for non-attendance were received from Lord Halifax, the Duke cf Newcastle and a number of other persons. Lord Halifax wrote that with regard to the two sub- jects which would he discussed at the meeting,he should like to say that in regard to the education question he hoped they would unanimously support the resolution which had been agreed to at the meeting of the mem- bers of the joint houses of the Convocation of Canter- bury and York. The Church of England asked for no favour in regard to her schools, but she did ask for jus- tice., both for herself and for the other religious bodies who believed that education must be religious, and that to -be religious it must be denominational. A fair share in the rates was necessary for the support of denominational schools; and if that was to be refused he hoe?d it might be made clear that Churchmew would- refu.geto pay rates in future for a system of undenom- inatioual teachine given by teachers as to whose religi ous,opieions they could have no security, and which, as such., was a violatien of the rights Of conscience in a matter of such vital importance as the education of their 'children. In regard to the marriage law, might it not be desirable to obtain such a change in the law as would make a civil marriage for legal purposes com- pulsory in all cases, leaving the Church of England en- tirely iree to enforce her own law on her own members..? ghat wonld enable all persona to contract marriages valid for all legal purpeses, such as were now or might be in theinture allowed by law, while it would relieve the Church from the difficulties to which she was now exposed in regard to the marriage of divorced persons,or might be exposed in the futureon the event of marriage with a deceased wife's sister being legalised. If the clergy would boldly and unitedly refuse to celebrate the marriage of any divorced person whose husband or wife was alive, they would vindicate the Church law, and miglet rely on the support of the laity iu a course which would do so much to protect the morality of the coun- try, and check the growing laxity in all ranks of society as to the obligations and sanctity of the marri- age bond. (ALepleage ) The Cumincatasaid Lord Halifax's letter intensified their regret that fleas their chairman was unable to be eeresent. He then spoke at length on the E CU. and the Deceased Wife's Sister's Bill, and the question of marriage after divorce. Mr. J. Lowman :Flamseeew (North Staffordshire DX.) spoke on the Divorce Act, He referred to it as a plagee-spot upon the •Statute book of this country. &lt;Hearehear.) Since the Act came into operation there bad been about 8,000 divorces in this couetry,which had taken pace mainly amoug the middle and upper classes of society. That statement showed that the plague spot W 5 Infectious and destructive of the moral health and tone of the nation. (Applause.) The Rev. J. WAKEROOD (vicar of St. Margaret's, Anfiekl), said it was never meant by the Church that what was aow known as divorce should be allowed. (Hear, hear.) Mr. LEDLEr Bnower also addrsseed the meeting, and the Rev. T. 0. MARSUALL (teC.) gave an address on the value of the E.C.U. at ;the present time and its claims upon churchmen for their support. , Mr. E. J. Pats (Liverpool) also speke on the E.C.U. and its future work. A comprehensive vete of thanks to preachers.chairman and the Vllerringtos branch was carried unanimously.</p>
        <p>For NAV 1:C BROOCRIC-3, solid d liver name brooches 101 d any name (Government attmped. Presents or Articles of Yolveelf, you may visit DE)33]ItT'S. 10. CHURCIII-ST4IET Ltyrowtorn, where you can get hundroll of arfloles such 5t watches, Clocks, Jewellery, Rl(2ctro-Plate, Cut ery„Purses Pueket Kifv.e,3, eta at wholesale prices. Sec advertisement (I;lostrated Chronograph Witt3h,lid,, et.), co pap 7 of this Parker,- idvt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-11-14_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>RUNCOIVN AND W 1)N ES.</head>
        <p>RUNCOIVN AND W 1)N ES.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN PRIMITIVE MR.THODIST CHURCH. On Wednesday evening,a married ladies' meeting was held in the schoolroom adjoiningthe chapel in Greenway- road, Runcorn. Prior to the meeting,a splendid tea was served out by Mrs. Et,ynolds, Mrs. Collier, Mrs. Shingler and Mrs. Fleet. The meeting was then held, and Mrs.J. Thomason presided. The chair-woman, in a splendid speech, said they had met there that night to do honour to their God, by helping the officers of the church to clean and renovate his house. She thought as much pride ought to be taken in God's house, as they took in their own domestic homes. (Applause.) Mrs. Thomason later in the evening read two recitations. A recitation entitled " A soft ar w r turneth away strife " was also given. Songs by 1., ver quartette parties were contri- puted. Mrs. Reym di -ead a sermon. The dialogue, re- presenting different nationalitie,,vras very amusing and brought forth loud roars of laughter from the audience. At the conclusion the pastor, the Rev. Mr. Fidoe, pro- posed a veto of thanks to the chair-woman, who,he said, had presided over the meeting in a very able manner. Mrs. Reynolds seconded and the enjoyable meeting was brought to a close. The proceeds were in aid of the renovation fund. RUNCORN CAPTAIN DROWNED AT BIRKENHEAD.</p>
        <p>_ The body of James Whitehead, captain of the " Gipsey,"Runcorn, who left his port a short time ago, was found in the float at Itirkenhead by the local police. The deceased was seen by some of his mates on the Friday evening, but on Saturday he was missed. Dragging operations were commenced and his body was picked up on Monday. RUNCORN CYCLING CLUB.</p>
        <p>The second annii-ai-dZ;c-e.-i;;;;hnection with the Run- corn Cycling Club, was held on Wednesday evening in the Public Hall, Runcorn. The hall had been beautifully decorated for the occasion by a few members of the club with flowers, kc. Bicycles were also in prominent position around the room. Mr. Bramall's band of Liver- pool, supplied the music, and at his call for the first dance a large number f couples turned out. The even- ing's programme con.isted of 23 dances, and these were concluded at about 3.30 on Thursday morning. The chief features of the evening were the " Snow " and "Shadow" dances. By some means an arrangement was erected in the roof of the hall, and whilst the snow dance was proceeding small scraps of paper fell on the dancers and covered thv. room floor. The shadow pro- cess was also thrown on, and both together presented a charming spectacle. The M.C.'s were Messrs. Wright, Mundy, and Le Conteur, whilst Mr. and Mrs. G. Whin- yates and Messrs. Webster and Hill had char;:,,e of the refreshment bar. There was a large company present. A RUNCORN MAN IN FROUBLE AT WREXHAM.</p>
        <p>At tiee—W—re-x-Isam Borough Police Court, on Monday, Martin Gilligan, hawker, of Runcorn, was charged by Police-Constable Henry Jones with gaming in a public place. It appeared that on Saturday night the prosecu- tor was on duty in plain clothes in Hope-street, when he saw the prisoner visiting various public-houses in a sus- picious manner. The prosecutor followed hint into the Talbot Inn and the Green Man, where the prisoner used a number of tickets for gaming. The tickets were all plain with the exception of one, on which was written a figure. All these tickets were placed face downwards on a table, and the prisoner invited persons to back their fancy by placing 411. on any of the tickets. If they spotted the marked ticket they won a silk handkerchief. The:prisoner himYelt won three times out of four. A fine of ss. and costs was imposed. ELLESMERE STREET FREE CHURCH, RUNCORN. The chapel anniversary was celebrated on Sunday, when two able sermons were preached to good congre- gations by Mr. R. Hatton, of Winsford. The choir ren- dered special anthems in an efficient manner, and Mr. Wesley Ceoke gave an admirable interpretation of the solo, "Seek ye the Lord." Mr. John Price presided at the organ. On Monday evening a tea meeting was held in the schoolroom, which was well attended. The mem- bers of the ladies' sewing meeting had obtained subscrip- tions to defray the cost of the tea. The following ladies presided at the tables :—Mre. John Stubbs,Misses White, Paul, Guy, Holford, Rutter Ashbrook, and Carr. The subsequent meeting was under the presidency of Mr. Ogden Rochdele, who was supported by the Revs. J. Stafford and R. Percival,Meesrs. E. J. Weaver,E. Gandy, and S. Scragg. The addresses were agreeably interspersed with songs by Miss Holford,Mr. Wesley Cooke, and Miss M. Paul.; a mandoline solo by Miss Stubbs; and part songs by the choir, under the conductorship of Mr. Hol- ford. The accompaniments were played by Master John Holford.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-11-14_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>RUNCORN BILLIARD HANDICAP,</head>
        <p>A RUNCORN BILLIARD HANDICAP,</p>
        <p>On the occasion of the openinf.Y, of a new billiard room in Mr. Peter O'Coyle's hotel, Weston Point, a grand bil- liard match took place on Wednesday night between Mr. H. Barnes, the steward of the Runcorn Liberal Club and Mr. R. Tottle. of Liverpool, and the runner-up with Mr. R. Golborne, in the Liverpool tournament. There was a good company present. The game was very in- teresting, and ended in Mr. Barnes being recorded the victor by a majority oft 79. The figures were—Barnes, 500 ; Tattle, 421. The company present included a good number of friends from the Runcorn Liberal Club,as well as a number of officials from the Castner Kellner Chemi- cal Company at Weston Point. Songs,&amp;c., were contri- buted up to a late hour. FUNBKAL OF MRS. CLARKE AT RUNCORN. The remains of Mrs. Clarke, whose death occurred at St. Helens, as already recorded in these columns, were interred in the Runcorn cemetery on Saturday after- noon. The cortege left the residence of Mr. INowall, Bold-street. In advance walked the Rev. J. Stafford, Mr. F. J. Norman, J.P., and a number of prominent members of Ellesmere-street,, Chapel, including Messrs. E. Gandy, W. Gandy, J. Gandy, G. White, J. Holford, H. O'Neil, F. Guy, F. Clarke, Captain Paul, B. Booth. G. W. Yates, J. Brown, J. Turnbull. S. Peers,Soragg,and W. Cooke, also representatives from St. Helens, includ- ing Messrs. S. Duckworth and D. Watson. After the hoarse came the mourners. The funeral service was con- ducted by the Rev. J. Stafford. On the coffin were wreaths forwarded by Mr. W. J. Wigg, Erriployos Hull and Liverpool Red Oxide, Messrs. H. Jeacock, R. Fryer, D. Watson, W. Cooke, J. Mayo, and T. Moores, Mrs. Waldon, and Miss Faulkner. A RUNCORN STZLINIER SUNK.</p>
        <p>On Friday morning, through the dense fog which pre- vailed, a serious collision took place opposite the Prince's Landing-stage Liverpool. A small screw steamer, called the Medway, while proceeding up the river came into collision with the Alexandra, Towing Company's screw steamer, Alexandra, which was travelling in an opposite direction. The vessels met end on, the damge to the Alexandra consisting of the smashing of her bow-plates. The impact was severe, and could be distinctly heard by the people on the stage, among whom it caused no little excitement. The vessels were kept together, and ropes were passed from the tug te the flat, the object of the crew of the former being to tow the latter in shore. Af- ter drifting down the river for some distance it was found that the flat had sustained such serious damage that she was filling with water and sinking. The tug had consequently to let go her hold, and the fat shortly afterwards went down in deep water at the south end of the stage, the crew having been previously taken off by the Alexandra and being landed later on in dock. While the Alexanda was getting clear ef the tug she came into a second collision, this time with the Wallasey ferry boat Snowdrop, and received some damage to her stern. THE RUNCORN PERMANENT BUILDING SOCINTY.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the shareholders (it the above society was held oh Thursday night in the Trinity schoolroom. The directors ih their 33rd annual report expressed regret that, owing to the depressed state of trade, the amount of rents received from the property in Widnes was less than last year, but they hoped that the new industries would result in increased rests during the ensuing year. The nett profits of the past year amouuted to £937 Bs. 7d., out of which it was proposed to pay a dividend of 44 per cent, per annum, which will absorb £847 19s. 2d. tt be carried to the reserve fund. The reserve now amounts to £10,503 19s. 5.1. The report was adopted. DEATH OE 1 RUNCORN ODDFELLOTP.</p>
        <p>The death of Mr. William Hitchmough, 81 years of ago, occurred at his residence, South &amp;ink-terrace, Run- corn, on Wednesday night. The deceased was one of the founders of the Ebenezer Lougs 1.0.0., MU., and was the oldeit member. INTERESTING MARRIAGE AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>A marriage in which much interest was manifested, was solemnised at Runcorn Church on Tuesday morning, between Mrs. Letitia Evans, widow of the late Mr. R. Evans, jun., of Runcorn, and Mr. James Lucas, station master at St. helens Junction. The bride whe was charmingly attired was attended by Miss Lucas, sister of the bridegroom; and Mr. John Lucas,brother of the bridegroom officiated as best twin. Mt. W. H.Chad- wick,Leinstsr Gardens,Runcorn,brother-in-law gave the bride away. The ceremony was performed by the Rev. H. B.Firth is the presence of a large congregation. The parties have receivvd a large number of wedding pre- sents.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-11-14_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>RUNCORN FREE LIBRARY.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN FREE LIBRARY.</p>
        <p>LECTURE ON ROCKSAVAGE.</p>
        <p>The second lecture of the third course under the aus- ,Oees of the Runcorn Free Library was given on Friday evening, in the Art-room of the 7Technical institute, by Mr. William Handley, whose subject was " Rocksavage arid its Story : A Study in Local History." Mr. E. IGlnifo£l, C.C., occupied the chair, and there was a large attendance. The Chairman said :he object of the lectutes had been to introduce readers to the contents of the shelves of the public library, it being felt that many persons bad no method in the way in which they should use the books. The first course was specially devoted to this purpose ; but in the second Mr. Pritchard opened a new line by dealing with local history, and now Mr. Handley was following on the same lines. Mr. Handley, in his introductory remarks, alluded to their being an old adage. " What is always seen is never seen," which, while true, was only so in a limited and restricted sense. Their ignorance was born of their fami- liarity, and they knew too often the least of that of which they might now know the most. This condition was due mostly to tho hurry-scurry of the times; they</p>
        <p>were:compelled to be too busy to find time to be original, and to a great extent their pleasures and relaxations were perforce ready made for them, and they were led about and guided, aye, fed and even suitably clothed by the touring agent and the clever advertiser. He was, however, fain to hope that if the same attention was be- stowed on scenes within their ken there were few if any of their surroundings that would fail to become an auri- ferous mine, and yield information which would brighten their lives, quicken their local loyalty, and create withi2 them an interest in, and a reverence for,that past which they were even now making for their successors. Rock- savage was no exception to the rule. Its history was to 'most of them scarcely even a mesnory, and of it as a link which had played a not unimportant part in the making of their nation they had little if any conception. Mr. Handley then dealt at length and •in a very interesting manner with the history of Rocksavage. Mr. Stokes proposed a vote of thanks to the lecturer. _ .</p>
        <p>Mr. Charles Taylor seconded the motion, which was supported by Mr. S. Pope, and carried. On the proposi- tion of the lecturer a similar compliment was paid to the Chairman.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-11-14_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>RUNCORN RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of the Runcorn Rural District Council was held on Tuesday,in the Board-room,Dutton Workhouse. Mr. J. Wintyre, J.P., presided, and there were also present—Admiral Parry, J.P.,. Messrs. T. Baxter, C.C., H. E. Heywood,J. Knowles,J. Darbyshire, J. M. Williams, T. Lewis, J. Weir, J. B. Chadwick, E. Aaron,W. Bradford,J. L. Jones, E. Spruce,C. Mounfiell, T. Wright, J. Ashton (clerk), G. Ashton (deputy clerk), Farrington (nuisance inspector), Diggle, Swinton (sur- veyors), and Dr. Adams (medical offi :er of health).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-11-14_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>FRODSHAM PAROCHIAL COMMITTER.</head>
        <p>FRODSHAM PAROCHIAL COMMITTER.</p>
        <p>The minutes of tLe Frodsham Parochial Committee showed that attention was called to the fact that under an order of the Local Government Board,the clause with reference to the provision of appliances for extinguishing fire was in force in Frodsham. It was moved by Mr. alwards,and seconded by Mr. Barrow,that the question of providing such be referred to the Water Committee. An amendment was proposed by Mr. Heywood, and se- conded by Mr. Williams, that the matter be referred to a committee to be composed of such of the members of the Parochial Committee as were present at the meet- ing. On the casting vote of the Chairman the amend- ment was adopted. —The minutes were carried.</p>
        <p>THE POLLUTION OF RIVERS, &amp;C. Dr. Vacher, medical officer of health for the county of Chester, wrote saying he had been instructed by the County Council to report on the pollution by sewage of rivers, streams and brooks, lie asked for certain information to be supplied to him.—The matter Was. referred to the Inspector (Mr. Farrington).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-11-14_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>LATCHFORD LOCKS.</head>
        <p>LATCHFORD LOCKS.</p>
        <p>Correspondence was read from Mr. Woodcock, clerk of the Grappenhail Parish Council, intimating that his council had no intention of using a threat towards the District Council ,but hoped that by suggesting an appeal to the higher authority it would strengthen the Runcorn Council's hand in dealing with the matter. They reco2; uised the urgent responsibility that devolved on the Dii- trict Council and themselves co protect the public cro? s- ing the locks. They suggestdd the desirability of appoint- ing a day, when the Grappenhall and the District Coun- cils could, meet on the spot.—The Chairman thought the suggestion a very goot one, and said lie could see no reason why they should not appoint a day to meet.—On the proposition of Mr. Williams the committee who were appointed some time ago, were re-3.ppoiated with the addition of Mr. Darbyshire.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-11-14_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>WALTON INFERIOR</head>
        <p>WALTON INFERIOR</p>
        <p>The Chairman moved that the County Council of Cheshire, be requested to take over so much of the new road in the township of Walton Inferior as extended from the main road to the foot of the Ship Canal Swing Bridge, being a length of about 80 yards, and also so much of the said road on the northerly side of the Canal as extended from the Swing Bridge to the fixed bridge over the river Mersey being a further length of about 200 yards, sp as to constitute the said length main roads repairable by the County Council.—Mr. Swinton sub- mitted that to put the length in proper order would cost £s3.—Mr. Knowles seconded.—Mr. Baxter su,gaested that they should offer the County Council £6O 14e. to take over the r( ad at ouce.—The proposition was altered with Mr. Baxter's suggestion and carried.</p>
        <p>MEDICAL.' OFFICER'S REPORT.</p>
        <p>Dr. Adams (medical- officer cf health) reported that the scarlet fever had broken out in the following town- ships: G rappen hall, 1 case; Keckwick 1 ; Sutton 8 ; Stockton Heath 2; Dutton, 2 ; Aivanley, 2 ; and several cases at Preston Brook. Measles were report ,d to have br,ken out in the Dutton district. The usual instructions were given. This concluded the business.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-11-14_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of the Runcorn Board of Guardians was lild on Tuesday, in the Board room, Dutton Workhouse. Mr. J. Knowles presided,and there were also present, the Revs. Dr. Gastaldi and J. Simp- son, Mr. A. R. Norman and tke members of the District Council as well as W. J. Ashton (clerk) and W. G. Ash- ton (deputy clerk).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-11-14_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>VISITING COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>VISITING COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>The master reported that the above committee recom- mended the appointment of Dr. Selby (brother of Dr. Selby, Frodsham) as assistant medical officer in place of Dr. Maclttunan resigned--Mr. Norman moved the adop- tion of the minutes and they were carried.</p>
        <p>APPOINTMENT OF ASSISTANT MATRON.</p>
        <p>Mr. Wright proposed that Miss Cuanah be appointed temporary assistant matron at a salary of £2o.—Mr. Darbyshire waved an amendment that the salary in- clusive for the duties of organist and other work be fixed at £l5. He thought it was quite adequate for a person of her age.—Dr. Gastaldi seconded. He thought they had to do everything in their power to economise. Their poor rate, is. 61. in the £, was heavy enough.— Several members objected to the latter sentence.—Mr. Aaron said their poor rate was not is. 6d. in the £. It was only 7i i. He thought it was a very fair one in- deed.—Mr. Heywood said he did not think that the salary of £2O proposed to be paid to Miss Cunnah was a id. .too much. Mr. Williams thought the remark in refsrence to the poor rate ought to be withdrawn.—Dr. Gastaldi with- drew the remark, and said the remark he made was in reference to Runcorn.—The amendment was put to the meeting and three voted for it. The original proposi- tion was put and nine voted for it. The amendment, therefore was lost and the original motion carried.</p>
        <p>FINANCE COMMITTEE. Mr. Heywood reported that the out-relief for the past fortnight was—Runcorn, £9O; Frodsham, £27 ; Bud. worth, £22. Altogether the accounts, &amp;c., amounted to £217 163, 10d. The balance in hand was £1,209 9s. 7d. He proposed,and Mr. Baxter seconded and they were carried.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-11-14_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>MYSTERIOUS DISAPPEARANCE OF A RUNCORN WOMAN.</head>
        <p>MYSTERIOUS DISAPPEARANCE OF A RUNCORN WOMAN.</p>
        <p>Great ar xiety is felt by the friends of Mrs. Annie Carroll, of 25 Fox-st., Runcorn,at her sudden disappear- ance from home on Thursday evening. Mrs. Carroll had had been ill in bed for the past six weeks, and was only this week allowed to get up by her medical adviser, Dr. Robinson. The medical gentleman called to see his pa- tient yesterday (Thursday) afternoon,and after examina- tion pronounced her to be in a very low condition. Her condition was such that the doctor made another visit later in the evening, but to his surprise his patient had gone. The poor woman had been attended by her mother during her illness, but last night she sent her nurse out on an errand, and also sent her daughter out to play at the front door. The woman was ieft all alone in the house, and it is thought that this was the time that she disappeared by the hack door. A number of children wore playing about the yard door but they never noticed Mrs. Carroll coine out. The unfortunate woman is only partially dressed. A search party was made up on Thursday night, and visited all the likely places where they thought the woman might be, but all of no avail. Up to noon to-day (Friday) no trace had been foeud of her. Any information regarding her will be welcomed by her husband, Mr. John Carroll, at the ab..)ve address, or by the police.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-11-14_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>RUNCOUN MUSICAL SOCIETY'S</head>
        <p>RUNCOUN MUSICAL SOCIETY'S</p>
        <p>CONCE</p>
        <p>The first concert of the season in connection with the above Society was held on Monday evening in the Public Hall, Runcorn. The proceedings opened with au instru- mental trio, " Minuet," by Miss Gordon Nun (violin); Miss Muriel Handley (cello),and Mr. T. H. Noble (piano). " The sailor's gave" was then sung thy Mr. W. Dever. Miss Isa Balfour was loudly applauded for her excellent rendering of " Love smiles but to dcceive." Mr. Charles Manner's appearance on the stage was greeted with an enthusiastic outburst of applause. He rendered " rin a Roamer." He also gage an interpretation of Mendels- sohn's song, for which he was encored. "Father O'Flynn " was sung in response to the re-call and was heartily appreciated by the audience. Madame Fanny Moody fully sustained the reputa- tion she had already established as a singer by her excellent song " Poor wandering one." In re- sponse to the demands, she rendered exceedingly well, the popular song, "Killarney." Miss Muriel Handley made her first public appearance as a celloist in her native town, and was warmly applauded for her effi- cient rendering of ,Popper's ", Nocturne " and Klengel's " Mazurka." The second part of the programme con- sisted of the garden scene from Gonnod's opera "Faust," in costume. The performers were, Marguerits,,Madame Fanny Moody ; Nurse and Siebel, Miss Isa Balfour ; Faust, Mr. John Child ; and Mephstopheles Mr. Charles Manners. The evening's entertainment concluded with the jewel :song which created some enthusiasm. The concert throughout was a decided success, all the per- former g taking tbeir respective parts in an admirable manner.</p>
        <p>SIR WILF RID LA.WSON AT WARRINGTON. ENTHUSIASTIC 34 TING IN PARR Under the auspices ot uu, Warrington Temper- ance Federation, a crowded and enthusiastic meet- ing was held in Parr Hall on Wednesday evening, when the chief speaker for the occasion was Sir Wilfrid Lawson, Bart., M.P. Councillor John Cros- field,J.P., occupied the chair. He was supported on the platform by Mr. Boreland, of the United Kingdom Alliance, the Revs. J. Yonge, J. J. Lang- ham, W. H. Drummond, W. Potts, Jahn Gunnell, J. Waznock, E. T. Reed, J. S. Hughes, G. Harbottle, and Messrs. John Rylands. J.P.,R. Garnett,J.P., Dr.T. S. Smith, J.P., W. Piet point. J.P., H. Roberts, J.P. J. H. Gornall, J.P., J. %V. Hayward,J.P ,Roger Bate, C.C., Thomas Birchall, J. 1"., S. Wright, J.P., A. H. J Crosfield, David Tinnion, J. Shaw Green, Henry Thornton, C. J. Holmes, G. Plumpton G. Clay, Thomas Morris, • F.G.S., R. Storey, S. B. Stringer, W. Beswick, John Harrison, John Drury, J. Bennett, W. Winstanley, F. R. Roberts, J. IPodmore, F. Brown, John S. Green, Colour-Sergeant Mantle, P. T. Winskill, R. Wylie, Joseph Mounfield, Bailey, Dallow, Hough, the Com- mittee of the British Women's Temperance Associ- ation (Warrington Branch), and W. Roberts and J. C. Dalton (secretaries). During the time of waiting for the meeting to commence, several volunlaries were played on the organ by Mr. W. H. Payton. The League of the Cross band also enlivened the proceedings by a musical selection in an interval in the programme. Sir WILFRID Lewsow, who was cheered again and again, said : I am exceedingly obliged to you for your cordial resolution of welcome and I assure you I am very glad to be hero to-night. I am glad to see this great audience and glad to have the Chairman presiding over it. (Hear, hear.) He is not a iteetotaler. I am. (Laughter.) Therefore we are carrying on the proceed- ings of the meeting on what the Church of Enabled Temperance Society calls the dual basis. (Laughter.) But I want to explain to the Chairman and to you that I don't come here to night; to advocate teetotalism. It is a very good thing; it has been advocated in this coun- try for some sixty years,and its advocates will go on ad- vocating it until it triumphs. (Applause.) I have come here to-night to talk to you about the legislative aspect of the question,and whether the State as a State is deal- ing correctly in the rianner in which it deals with alcohol, or what we call strong drink. I have made a great many speeches on this subject. I daresay a great rimanY of you have read some of thene,and I don't know that I have anything fresh to tell you. There was once a an who made a speech and someone described it afterwards that what was new was not true,and what was true wasn't new. (Laughter.) I don't profess to Come here to tell you anything new. I don't think any- one could say anything new on this drink question. It has been thrashed out, through and through for nearly two generations, but I will try and ten vest what- is true and then the Chairman will reply to me. (Laughter.) First of all what I say is that drink, strong drink, alcoholic drink is a national enemy. (Heam, hear.) It doesn't do any good, it deesn't strengthen the bodv,it doesn't enable anyone to do hard work. What harder work could there be than a contested election? (Laughter.) Yoe know people conspire,and perspire; and run about and tell lies—(laughter)—and get excited. I don't know any more thirsty business than electioneering. (Laughtor.) But what took place last week in the United States? There was the biggest poll ever taken in the world in one day. About thirteen millions of People cast their votes some on one side and some on another,and would you,believe it throughout that enormous country—the new world as it called—there was not a drink shop allowed to be opened on the day of the poll! (Applause.) If people can do without drink on an election day they can do without it on any day. (Laughter.) Don't take it from me. I never make this kind of statement on my own authority ' • I always quote great authorities. (Laughter.) Take it from Lord aVols- eley what the nature of driek is. He said we Iliad got many enemies round about us in England but the most pressing enemy strong was drink. I remember a man making a speech on a temperance platform and he described drink as an enemy. There was a man in the meeting who thought he would catch him and he said " Ah sir,but you know we are told to love our enemies." (Laughter ) The speaker was rather nonplussed tor a moment but there was an Irishman in the meeting— (laughter) no always comes to the rescue when a difficulty arises, (Laughter) and he said " Yes sir, but we are never told to swallow cur enemies. (Loud laughter.) I have got better authority then Lord Wonieley in the greatest and most inepired writer ml the world—Shakespeare who wrote—"Oh that men should put an enemy into their mouths to steal away their brains." That is what the enemy does ; he steals away all that is useful to you. lie steals from you the power to make money to keep yourselves and makes you paupers. He steals away the power of reasoning—the grettest power the Almighty has given us, and makes people lunatics ; he steals away the power of discriminating between right and wrong which dettreys their consciences and makes them criminals. It is sad not only for the paupers, criminals, and lunatics but also very sad for you and ice. We are all the sadder people for it. We have to keep these people. (Hear, hear.) If the publicans kept them it would be rather better, but you and I have to keep them rand I think we have quite enough to do. (Hear, hear.) What a lot we taxpayers have to carry. We haw) to maintain that great army and navy to kill all our fellow Chriatians—(laughter)—then there is the great National chum oh to maintain to fight the devil— (laughter)—the great army of schoolmasters to maintain to fight ignorance. and then all the landlords of estates whom the last Parliament decided to inaintaia and give two millions of money. (Laughter.) When you have all these people to maintain I say it is too bad to put on ay no du re rtiamx iensati sh eb ems iadi enst .e n nAcpep7al (Applause.) el o. of lunatics paaruepweerrss,, Exhibi- tion is being held in London and there is, I believe, a collection of casks, bottles, beer-engines, corkscrews— (laughter)—and all kinds of liquors presented to the people who go and see. But there is one thing they don't present and that is the finished article they turn out. (Lauehter.)l don't blame them for that, there is no occa- sionifor them to do ao.You have only to go' to the work- houses, lunatic asylums. and gaols and you see splendid products which these brewers have turned out. (Laugh- ter and applause) I am here to night representing thht United Kingdom Alliance which Mr. Roberts. mentioned, and we are an association or a league or a force if you like to call us SJ who have determined never to stop until we have put a stop in this country to the manufacture of paupers, lunatics and criminals. (Applause.) You and I get on very well without drink I daresay. I daresay there are plenty' of teetotalers here.l see a lot of intelligent faces around me.(Laughter.) You are after mine own heart—(laughter)—and I dare- say there are a lot of moderate drinkers, but I want to convert them. (Hear, hear.) You know what a moder- ate driaker is. He is a man who knows exactly- the right Amount of poison to take into the system without doing very much harm. (Laughter.) They say "Oh ves we don't think much of those wretched teetotalers; they cannot control themselves, and we can take it and want it." (Laughter.) As a friend of mice says "Yes that is qeite true ; they are always taking it or wanting it." (Laughter.) I am appealing to the moderate drinkers and I ask them whether they should not think of others as well as themselves. (Hear. hear.) I am not saying give up your delicious liquor; abandon your glass; I should be very glad if you did that, but I ask you to take a step which I think you would find desirable as being useful to your fellow creatures, and an unselfish step, by doing something for the poor and wretehed. Even these moderate drinkers are in danger. Strong drink is yery often teo strong for them. • I will give you an illustration of how it gets the better of people. I heard of a man in the Western States of America who had a farm which was very much infested with bears who came out of the forest and did a great deal of dam- age. He went to the oldest and most respeetable man in the village and asked him what he was to do, and he replied that the farmer must get a trough, put a lot of molasses and whisky into it and place it outside. The bears would come, get drunk,fall down near the trough, and he could then eanneout and knock them on :he head. He did so and on going out next merniee found the respectable old inhabitant lying by the trough to.* drunk to move. (Laughter.) That was an illustration of the truth which the Band of Elope children sang, " Where there's drink there's danger." We, of the Alliance are not going to stop until we get the people to stop the manufacture of drunkards in this country. (Applause.) We consider the public-houses —thee,. legelised places for the sale of drink—are the principal causes of drunkards. Some people seemed to think that public-qousea grew. (Laughter.) I want to bring tbe responsibility home to you. Where does a man get drunk P—At the public - house. Who supplies him ? The publican. Who allowed the publican P—The Magistrate.— Who made the magistrate ?--Phe member of Parliament. —Who allowed the member of Parliament to have that power P—The electors of this country and the electors of Warrington. You sent a man to Parliament to keep up that law. (Applause.) Verily you and I and all of us, moderate drinkers, teetotalers, drunkards, all are citizees of this country and have power to vote. We are responsible for the drunkennevs of this country and though I don't excuse he poor drunkard, you and I who maintain this system, are far more guilty than the pcor wretch who gives way to it. (Applause.) Coming to meetings like this,and passing kind resolutions congratu- lating me on what I have done, and shouting and cheering and sending up petitions, these will not do much good. They will not do much more than the con- cert; of Europe does to Turkey. (Laughter.) But when you vote then you are in earnest. I don't believe anyone, on this question who does not vote straight. I</p>
        <p>quired a licensed house. Sir William Harcourt was not going to interfere with the magistrates. They are very great people—l am one. (Laughter.) I heard of a , woman who was driving a cow through a town, and as it was going alone a wrong course she shouted to a re- spectable man in front " Oh, man, turn my cow." He did not do o,and when she asked him the reason he re- plied "I am not a man; lam a magistrate." (Laugh- ter.) Sir William Harcourt wanted no further licensing authorities. The magistrates may go on with their work but by hia plan Sir William Harcourt said.if there shall be a place, whether in Lancashire or Cumberland, or Yorks or Cornwall, where the people are sensible enough to say we want to be without drink-shops, they shall have power to say eseand no Board of magistrates shall have power to inflict one upon them. (Applause.) I consider that one of the greatest extensions of local self government we have had proposed. (Applause.) You remember the Local Government Bill aud what speeches there were on curtailing the power of the par- sons in the villages,but one publican does as much harm as twenty parsons.(Laughter). All that Sir William Bar- court's Bill intended to do was to put a weapon in the hands of every district allcwing them to get rid of the publican if they wished to do so: I should have thought there would have been nothing more rational or agreeable to the workingmen of this country than such la proposal as that—making them equal to the magic- , trates—(laughtei)—giving them the power of protecting their own homes, a power the magistrates possessed. I daresay you have had Mr. Rippon here. He carried a severeissn about with him for a number of years and offered it to anyone who could prove that a maeistrate had voted for a licensed house next door to where the ' magistrate and his family lived. At last one was found. (Laughter.) There was cne case and the magistrate must have been a fool. (Laughter...) Magistrates take good care not to have licensed houses near to their own houses, and we want workingmen to decide whether they wig have licensed houses at their own doors. (Ap- plause.) Well,we went to the country with the question and the workins,imen said "No; we will not have it "and they turned out Sir William Harcourt at Derby, Mr. William Crosfield—(applause)—a relative of the Chair- man's—at Lincoln, Mr. John Morley at Newcastle, Mr. Caine at East Bradford, and Sir Charles Cameron in Glasgow. The result in East Bradford is not 'so bad as it looks at first sight, because the publican's man had one candidate and they had two. Unfortunately there was a division and therefore the publican's man beat us, but there was something like 1.500 more votes against the publican than for him. (Laughter.) Still it was sad to see the workingmen think there was something more important than this great cower to rid themselves of this great bane of humanity. There was a sort of overthrow at the last election, but were the United Kingdom Alliance going to give it up because they did not win first time? No. It was the first time the electorate had voted on a distinct question. When I brought it forward in the House I was only a private member. At the last election it was distinctly part of the plan of one of the great parties. (Applause.) The Alliance did not know what:it:was7to be beaten, and they are determinedlas long as they live to go on fighting the drink traffic. (Hear, hear.) We are not here to reclaim the drunkard ; that is a. splendid work done by many taniperance societies. We are not here to punish the drunkards; they have punished themselves, but we are here to prevent the manufacture of drunkards because the policy of the Alliance is that "Prevention is better than cure." We were beaten at the last election—at least we did not succeed—but things are already brighter. All our agents and friends who are working in this matter tell us that never since the General Election have they had such enthusiastic meetings in favour of our policy. Even the Government, which is supposed to be a great friend of the publican, have had to do something, they have aepointed a commission to find out what makes people drunk. (Laughter.) They are very ignorant. , (Laughter.) I seould not have said that unless they said it themselves. (Laughter.) A deputation went to Lord Salisbury and he said the information at our command as to what goes on in this country is very limited. (Laughter.) Nineteen Cabinet Ministers :did not know what made people drunk! (Laughter.) It was quite time they should do something. They wanted informa- tion and so they appointed a commission to get it. No one else wants information. Drinkeellers did not want it ; the teetotalers don't 'want it. But they have appointed a commission. That is the way with Govern- ments 'When they don't know what to do—they appoint a commission. (Laughter.) The Government said they must do something; they had a Commission and it is working away. (Laughter.) When it is over I believe the nineteen Cabinet ministers will be: sadder but wiser men. There are many things to encourage us. Some few months ago Khama the black king was here from South Africa and his great object was to prevent drink being sold to his people. He went to see the Queen and she said she considered the sale of drink a curse to a nation. If I had said that you would have stated that it was some of the wild fanatical talk of Lawson. (Laughter.) But anyone who contradicts the Queen is guilty of high transom. (Laughter.) All the people are beginning to understand what a terrible thing it is to sell drink to black men. (Laughter.) I saw a letter from the Duke of Westminster who is chairman of a society for preventing the sale of drink to native races in which he told Khatnalnot to give his people any drink. But if I propose that you should be kept from having beer, the dukes all desert me. (Laugnter.) oen Paul, that grand friend of Mr. Chamberlain—(laughter)—is ' leis., on our side. What I have got to do now is to per- suade the Rouse of Commons that the Brit,sh people are natives. (Laughter.) As soon as I have done that it will be all right—(laughter)—for they think it is a dread- ful thing to sell drink So natives. not knowing that we are natives. (Laughter.) In this very Parliament, sup- posed to be a publican's Parliament, we carried the second reading of a Sunday Closing Bill. More than that, one member who voted for it was Mr. Bemrose, the member who kad thrown out Sir William Harcourt. The publicans were not ever-suited at his action. (Laughter.) The Government dare not even vote against the Bill and when the division came most of the distinguiseed men walked nut out rather than record their votes against the Bill. These are signs of the times. (Applause.) There was another good thing. Dr. Ternole when Bishop of London went to Lord Salisbury to get him to do something about the drink question. Lord Salisbury spoke to him as if he were a spoiled boy and told him he didn't oare much what he said and turned him out. (Laughter.) Dr. Temple returned and des- cribed Lord Salisbury as a stone wall, and said they would have to dig about him to bring him down. (Laughter.) But the stone wall has made this teetotal Bishop, Archbishop of Canterbury. So you may see that Salisbury is the corner stone of the Temple. (Laughter.) Now, Mr. Chairman,you a Quaker are being beaten into fits by an Archbishop. (Laughter.) Since title General Election in 1895 three seats have been won from the Tories. lam not attacking them as Tories but as sup- porters of the drink power. We have won three seats and everyone has been won by a man pledged to Local Veto. In everyone of these places the great lords of liquor did_ their best, but the people triumphed. (Applause.) In the municipal elections we had suc- ceeded, and you did pretty well here I believe (Applause.) I was reading the "Scotsman "on Monday, and in an article it was stated that the whole system of municipal Government in Glasgow was in danger of fal- ling into dlscreslit. I wondered what it meant, bet on reading further I found the presumption was based on the fact that ten out of fourteen candidates chosen at the recent :municipal election were pledged teetotalers. (Laughter.) Is Lancashire going to help or hinder us in this great movement? More than half a century ago the people of Lancashire rallied to the support of Bright and Cobden—(apptause)—and helped to give the , of this country that cheap food which for the last 7 fifty rears they have enjoyed. And now we are sum- , , moning you against a richer and more powerful and more mischievous monopoly than that of the great land- lords of that day. These people are dominating the country. Lord Randolph Churchill said he believed two- thirds of the members of the House of Commons were ; terrorised by this creat interest. If it was a crime and , cruelty to withhold food from the people as was done fifcy years ago, what crime is it not, what cruelty is it , not, to distribute poison amongst them as is done now to bring an accumulation of evils, of pestilence and famine in this country, or, as the Dean of Canterbury 7 said, " to light the fires of hell in ten thousand homes in England "You electors should think of your duties and of the future of this great country. I ioad in a paper that a friend sent me that one of your leading men in Warring- ton said " Warrington is Conservative te the backbone." I hope you are conservative and will conserve woat is right, and destroy what is wrong. The great poet laur- eate—not the present one; he couldn't write it—(laugh- ter)—wrote</p>
        <p>May freedom's hope for ever live With tresh,ning power from day to day; That man's the true Conservative Who lops the mouldering brAnch away. Then I say if you really wish for a better future for this country; if you wish the children who come after you to have a better chance than you have; if you wish this</p>
        <p>great reproach to be wiped away from our country,come over and help us in this great work to which we have put our hands and yours shall be the exceeding great reward of knowing that you have helped forward the greatest and noblest reform that was ever bestowed on the people of this country. (Loud applause.) The Rev. J. J.LANGHAM said the resolution which had been entrusted to him was as follows :—"That as public houses exist for the supposed convenience of the public, this meeting hereby declares that the people ought to have a direct power over the issuing of licenses for the sale of intoxicating drink in their own districts." (Ap- plause.) He thought very few words were needed from him to commend that resolution to such a large and en- thusiastic meeting. He had himself so often expressed his opinion in Warrington on the question of the drink traffic that be believed his views were pretty well known to most of those present. (Hear,hear.) He was as enthusiastic in support of the great principle now as ever he had been. (Applause.) He would like to ex- press his deep admiration for the speech they had lis- tened to from the Grand Old Man of the temperance cause,Sir Wilfr d Lawson. ( ipplause.) Those who had listened to that speech, whatever their political views might be, could not fail to have been impressed by the vigorous manner in which—tie believed he was right in saying, at the age of nearly three score years and ten, and after a long and tedious journey—Sir Wilfrid had spoken with his usual flowing wit and hu- mour. (Applause.) If the cause of total abstinence needed an argument, he thought he need only poiot to Sir Wilfrid Lawson, who showed that it was possible while being I an abstainer to do vigorous and sterling work at an advanced age. (Ap- plause.) If he (the speaker) wanted another proof he might well be pardoned for pointing to the G-.0.M. of the Church of England who had been recently appointed Archbishop of Canterbury. (Applause.) As a total abstainer himself he did not hesitate to say that it was because of the total abstinence principles to which the Archbishop had been so prone, that he had possessed all through his long life that clearness of intellect, and vigour of action which had enabled him to do work which younger men would have staggered at. (Ap- plause.) If the question of direct veto was to be pushed forward to a successful issue, the want at the present time was to educate the people, and they wanted this meeting to exercise an educational influence in the town and neighbourhood of Warrington_ (Applause.) The resolution was very simple in character,and appeared to him merely to rest upon commonsense and common tice. Whatever our own individual practice might be with reference to the taking of intoxicating drink he believed it would be generally admitted that the sale of drink bore a lot of evil fruits. (Hear, hear.) The number of public- houses up and down the country was a source of very great evil indeed. iThey added teethe rates and taxes and they as individuals had to pay the penalty. It eid seem to him only commonsense and justice that the people who had to pay should have the right of saying whether they would have these nuisances or not.(Hear, heer).The traffic was a great blot upon our nation aud as English people we should wish to remove every blot from a nation that we:loved so well. (Applause.) As English people and Christians they appealed to the public of this great country to do what they could as time and circumstances should permit them, to remove from our country this terrible evil, without the removal of which England could never be sober or never be free. (Applause.) Mr. EDWARD BORELAND, (district agent of the United Kingdom Alliance), seconded the resolution. He said he was delighted to have this opportunity of standing before a Warrington audience and supporting the resolution which Mr. Langham had proposed. He was pleased also to be there, because it was sometimes said that temper- ance meetings were composed exclusively of teetotallers. and that therefore they had no opportunately of reaching a class of people which it would be really interesting te get at. That could not be said of that meeting for it was well known to everyone that the meeting was composed of other than teetotallers. (Applause.) It was just as well to point out to moderate drinkers that there was a duty resting upon them as there was a duty resting upon teetotallers. (Applause.) The publichouses in this coup- try did not exist for teetotallers—that went without saying. The publichouses did not exist solely ler drunk- eras as there was no one a publican detested more than a drunken man or woman. For whom did they exist then? He did not need a publichouse 'nor did Sir Wil- frid Lawson. They must answer the question them- selves. When they asked for Local Veto at the heads of Parliament they were asking that the power to deal with ( the liquor traffic should be placed in the band of the people whether they were teetotallei semoderate drinkers, drunkards, or drunkard-makers. They had so much confidence in the good sense of the people of England— (hear, hear)—that they believed that when this question was placed rightly before them and when the responsi- bility was placed upon their shoulders and consciences they would realise their position and vote accordingly. (Applause.) Mr. W. S. Caine had said there were seven million teetotalers in this country. Seven million men, women, and children, who lived without intoxicating drink, and the speaker ventured to say that these people discharged all the duties of life at least as well and per- haps better than those who took intoxicating drink whether moderately or immoderately. (Applause) Sir Wilfrid Lawson had the power and -did prehibit the sale of intoxicating liquors on his estate, but that power did net belong to him alone, but to every landed proprietor in the kingdom,and to their honour be it said multitudes .4 them have exercised that power. There were 1,200 parishes in England without a publichouse, and, 179 parishes in Scotlaed without a public- house, and there was a little town in Ireland without a beerhouse. (Applause) Teat town was Besbrook, in Meter, and it contained 4,000 people. (Applause.) He (Mr. Boreland), was there a short time ago. They were mill-wotkers, and 4,000 of them without a public-house. That was one P ; without a pawnshop. two P's; with- out a pauper, three P's ; without a policeman, four P's; and without a prisoner, five P's. (Laughter and ap- plause.) They must just imagine 4,000 Irish people without a policeman. (Laughter.) It was a fact. All experience proved that where the public-house was eliminated from any district, there sobriety and every- thing attendant upon it reigned supreme. (Applause.) He had the greatest possible pleasure in seconding the resolution and he sincerely hoped that all would on every occasion demonstrate by voice and vote that they were on the side of sobriety and against the interests of the liquor traffics. (Applause.) The resolution was then put to the meeting and carried unanimously. Mr. Joner RTLANDS proposed a resolution "That the b ,st thank of this meetine be given to Sir Wilfrid Law- son for his address, and also to Mr. John Croefield for presiding, and that the Chairman be requested to for- ward copies of the second resolution to Sir William Bar- court, Mr. A. J. Balfour, M.P., the leader of the House of Commons, Mr. Robert Pierpeint, M,P.,and the press." Re assured them it was a very pleasant duty. He met I the Chairman the previous morning and he (Mr. Ry- lands) was not very well pleased with what he told him. At a celebrated club in Warrington he was told that I some members were chaffing Mr. Crosfield that he would be in his wrong place as chairman of that meeting. But Ilia (Mr. Rylands) thought their Chairman had made a very good temperance speech indeed, and one which no member of the club could find any fault with. Turning to Sir Wilfrid Lawson, Mr. Rylaails referred to an inci- dent in the House of Commons, and in conclusion said he was delighted to see Sir Wilfrid there, and he hoped it would not be the last time. (applause.) 1 The Rev. Joan YONGE said he had very great pleasure in seconding the resolution. It had been a matter of very great regret that au imperative engagement at Wakefield had hindered his arrival at that meeting. Sir Wilfrid was far on in the admirable address he had favoured them with, when he entered the hall, and he considered it to ba a great loss on ,his part. He hoped the press would enable him to read' the speech verbatim on Saturday. (Hear, hear.) He believed the visit et I such a gentleman as Sir Wilfrid Lawson was one of the most significant events which had occurred in the town for a considerable time. He was full of hope from the general aspect of that meeting. It was another instance in which the noble giver of that hall might congratulate himself on having given it to the town. (Applause.)But for his generosity they could net have had so splendid an audience on the question of the United Kingdom Alli- ance or the general question of temperance. He was earnestly hoping that it might be the cause of a revival of the great cause of temperance in the town. He hoped it might be the means of wakening up .a number of somnolent teetotalers. He shad been greatly depressed at what had appeared to him a lamentable apathy on the part of their temperance friends on the great question which they professed te be interested in. He hoped that meeting might also help to lead their temperance men to be perfectly true and consistent in all ques- tions of voting when called upon to exercise ' the franchise on this question. 10 had heel' a matter of blank amazement that in recent elections men had been known to vote for those who were not the friends of temperance. (Hear, hear.) It appeared to him to be a sad thing when brought face to face with that great question, and having an opportunity of in- fluencing it in the right direction, men were not strong enough in their backbone to give the vote which reason and their consciences told them was for the welfare of the community at large. He hoped the result ol that meeting would re-awaken an interest in the temperance cause and make them more etaunch,thorough,end loyal. in the question which lay so near the roots of seciety. He was very glad they had as Chair- man Mr. Crosfield, who had heard a good deal of plain speaking. He hoped Mr. Crosfield would appreciate it. "Mr.Crosfield was known as a plain speaker, and thy liked him all the better for it. (Ap- plause.) He (Mr.Yonge) hoped he would look back upon that meeting as one of the happiest he hal been called upon to preside at. Mr. HENRY THORNTON in supporting the reselution, referred to his connection with the United Kingdom Al- liance, and proceeded to speak of Mr. Crosfield's genero- sity to the temperance cause. Me suggested that as Sir Wilfrid Lawson was the guest of Mr. Crosfield, he might induce Mr. Crosfield to become a member of the the Alliance Committee. (Hear, hear and laughter.) The CHAIRMAN in responding, said it was the first time he had occupied the chair at an Alliance meeting, but it his services were wanted again he should be very glad to do so. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>Sir WILFRID LAWSON said it was pleasa.ut to in to have that resolution moved by Mr. John Rylands because he was a relation of a very old friend of his (Sir Wilfrid's) now, gone to his rest, an old rasmber for that borough—Mr. Peter Rylands. (Applause.) He (Sir Wilfrid) remembered how Mr. Rylands had charge of that Sunday Closing Rill in the days when i was not so popti;ar as at the present time. (Applause.) Referring to the Chair- man,he said they must not suppose that he was not ia favour of licensing reform. (Applause.) The meeting then terminated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-11-14_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <p>don't want you to vote for Tory, Unionist, Liberal, or Radical, or any of the numerous denominations in which we are now distributed, but I ask you to vote for those men only—whatever they call themselves—who are prepared to trust you to protect your own homes. (Applause). I want you to support Sir William Bar- courts plan. (Applause.) Not because he is a great Liberal—l believe he is leader ; someone said so— (laughter)—but because he is the first statesman who has had the courage to bring forward in the House of Commons, and stake his credit in taking a definite plan for striking a blow at the great drink trade. (Ap- plause.) I like Sir William's plan because it is my own. (Laughter.) But when I proposed it they didn't think much of it. (Laughter.) In this country they think more of the man than the means to redress an evil. Sir William Harcourt found that by the present system magistrates had to decide on three questions before they granted a lieense. They had to find out whether the man who wanted the license was</p>
        <p>a good man. I believe they are more particular about gettio,g a man in the drink trade than in the church. (Laughter.) Then the magistrates had to find out whether the hnusea were good. I believe they are quite successful there. (Laughter.) Then they had to find out whether the wants of the neighbourhood re-</p>
      </div>
