      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-11-14_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</head>
        <p>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</p>
        <p>ANOTHER CHARGE OF INFLICTING GRIEVOUS BODILY HAIM.</p>
        <p>On Thursday morning at the Warrington Borough Police Court, before the Mayor, Alderman Sutton and Siderman Wright,John Whitfield, 6 Mersey-street, aged 14, was charged with inflicting grevious badily harm upon Thomas Bertram Allen, 6 Kendrick-Street, aged 19, by striking him on the head with a steel bar in Messrs. Smith and Sons Wire Works on Wednesday evening.— The Chief Constable said the prisoner and prosecutor were fellow work lads at Smith's wire works. About 6 p.m. on Wednesday they were in the mill and a little horse play arose. Whitfield was either wilfully pushed or fell over Allen's feet. Allen began to push him about and the lads pushed him from one to another. Wnitfield lost his temper and rushed to the window from which he took a steel bar. He then rushed at Allen,and the latter stumbling on his knees at the time, Whitfield hit him a very severe blow on the top of the head. Allen had to be conveyeA to the infirmary in a cab, and it was there found that he had a severe scalp wound and perhaps the skull was slightly fractured. It would be at least eight days before he would be able to attend. He (the Chief Constable) did not allege any malice or intent on Whit- field's part, as he probably did it on the spur of the moment. He asked for a remand and was quite willing that defendant should be out on bail.—The magistrates remanded the ease for 14 days and accepted bail in Whitfield's father for £lO.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-11-14_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>MINOR CASES.</head>
        <p>MINOR CASES.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOROUGH POLICE COURT. MONDAY.—Before Mr. F. Monks (in the chair) and Alderman Harrison.</p>
        <p>ALLEGED THEFT OF A HAFF-SOVEREIGN.—THE PRICE OF A DRINK.—Alice Thomas, a woman of loose character, was charged with stealing from the person of Edward Murphy. in the yard of the " Roebuck " at 8 p.m. on Saturday night, a balf-sovereign.—The Chief Canstable said this was a some- what peculiar case. Prosecutor was an engine driver, of 7, Litton's-row. On Satu day evening he went into the yard and coming back met prisoner, who asked him for the price of a drink. He pulled out his money, which included a half-sovereign. He gave prisoner what he thought to be a sixpence. and went inside. Shortly afterwards he found that he had given her the half-sovereign instead of the a xpence.- Prosecutor gave information to Detective-Sergeant Monag- han, who f ound prisoner. She had changed the half-sove reign in Friars' Green. and purchased a pair ot boots. She admitted she had had the coin, but said the man had given it her. The Chief Constable said the offence was one of larceny because the prosecutor had not intended to give pris 3ner half-a-sovereign, and therefore she had deprived him of it against his will.— Prosecutor corroborate ..—Prisoner : I did not steal it off you did I ?—Prosecutor "No you did not steal it that's right enough "—The magistrates ordered prosecutor to have 9s. 24.. the money in prisoner's possession,given to him and sent prisoner to gaol for 14 days, SMASHING WINDOWS AT THE WORKHOUSE.—Catherine Dougherty was charged with breaking five panes effglass in the receiving room of the Workhouse on the 7th November Mr. Shearer prosecute and stated that he believed prisoner -broke the windows because she was kept in the receiving room a longer time than usual. She admitted breaking them. —The magistrates sent her to gaol for one month. DRUNK AND DISORDERLY.—For this offence the following persons were dealt with :—Charles Stewart. drunk and dis- orderly in Market-street, on Saturday night. ord u.ed to pay 2s. He signed the pledge. William Yuile, GO, Fairclough's Avenue, drunk and disorderly in Academy-street. on Satur- day night, fined ss. and costs.—Charles Ashc; oft, 110, Knuts- ford-road, fined 2s. 61. THEFT FROM A DWELLING HOUSE.—Sarah Maloney, was charged with stealing from the dwelling-house of 20 Ship-yard on Saturday night, a chemise, the property of Ellen Douglas. The Chief Constable stated that prisoner lodged at the address in question with Mrs. D mglas. About 1 a.m. on Sunday the police fonnd prisoner in Bridge-street with the chemise in her possession. She was taken to the house where Mrs. Douglas stated that she had placed the chemise on the dresser End prisoner bed no ri..:ht to take it.—P.O. Turner and Mrs. Douglas cerrotoraved end prisoner was committed for 14 days' hard labour. A COSTLY COCK-CROW.—William Jones and George Sixsroith, both young men were charged with being disor- derly in Sankey-street, on Sunday evening, Nov. Ist. P.C. Clough deposed to seeing the defendants parading up and down Sankev-street in a disorderly manner. They were crowing like fowls,—P-0-'8 Beenett and Johnson corrobor- ated.—The Chief Conetable deplored the lack ot a bye-law in the town dealing with the offence. He had one at Kendal.— Defendants were bound over to keep t;te peace in their own surety of £5 and pay costs Bs. each. TUESDAY.—Before Mr. R. Garnett (in the chair), Mr. E. J. Guest. and Dr. T. S. Smith. A LIKING FoR MOUTH-ORGlNs.—Three lads named Thomas Connolly, John Jackson, and William Connor, were charged with stealing from the shop of George Shakeshaft, music dealer, Clara-street, on Sunday afternoon three mouth- organs, value 33.—The boys admitted the thett.—The Chief Constable stated that Mr Shakeshatt left his shop locked up safely on Saturday evening. On Sunday he went to the shop and found a glass pane of the doir broken and a window cracked. At that. time lee missed nothing but at 6 p.m. he called again and found that the cracked piece of the window had been pulled out and three mote h-organs abstracted. In- formation was given to the police and Detective-Sergeant Monaghan traced the three mouth-organs to the defendants. Connolly had given his away, Jackson's was found at his home. and Connor had sold his. When charged they ad- admitted the offence.—Jackson stated that he went and told Mr. Shakeshaft that his window was l•rogen, and as he did not gixe him anything he (defendant) thought he might as well have a mouth-organ —The Chief Constable added that this was the fourth little shon in the neighbourhood which had been broken into.—Mr. Chantry confectioner, said that Conner had been in his employ three or four months. and had been honest and industrious. —The magistrates ordered defendants to receive eight strokes of the birch rod WEDNE3I)IY —Befors Mr. John Fairclough (in the chair) ' Mr. J. Geddes. and Councinor ti, Fleming. DIItYNK AND DISORDERLY.- Michael Cunningham was charged with being drunk and disorderly in Coekhedge-lane at 11.10 on Tuesday night.—The Chief Constable said that on the night in question P.C. Baeratt saw a disturbance in Horsemarliet-st. and found Cunningham and three ot eer men creating a disturbance by shouting and swearing.—This was prisoner's 25th appearance. —The magistrates fined prisoner 10a. and costs or 14 days. THURSDAT.—Before the _Mayor (in the chola.), Alderman -</p>
        <p>Sutton. and Alderman Wright. AN INCoRRTGIBLE.—EIiza Smith, 70th appearance, was charged with indecency, and with assaulting P.C. Taylor On Wednesday night.—P.C. Taylor proved the Case and prisoner was sent to goal for a month on the first charge and three months on the second. —On RUNCORN PETTY SESSIONS. Nnly.—Before Mr. F." 3". ;Norman and Mr. 3. W. Lightburu. Dinnoc.—Joseph Shaw, Rocksavage, was fined ss. and costs for being drunk at Clifton on the 2iilh ult. MuzziaNG ORDER.—A fine of ss. including coats was im- posed on Alfred Thomas, Woodside Farm, Sutton, for allow- ing his dog to be at large unmuzzied and without a collar. A. NUISANCE AT WESTON POlNT.—Alexander Borthwick. 32, Waterloo-road, Whines, was summoned at the instance of the Runcorn Rural District Council, to show cause why an order should not be made against him, to abate a nuisance connected with the out °few of certain houses in South-road, Weston Point. Mr. rarringron, nuisance inspector to the District Council said the nuisance arose from foul and over- flowing privies, in consequence of inattention and bad con- dition. His Council reqnired the removal of four of them and twoashpits. He did not think Mr. Borthwick was alto- gether to blame in the matter, as the situation of the privies was bad, tha public having access to them. - Dr. Adams (medical officer of health) said the nuisance had been perpetually under his notice for two er three year.</p>
        <p>To THE tEAF.—A Gentleman who cured himself of Deaf ness and Noises in the Head attPr fourteen years' suffering will gladly send tull particulars of the remedy post free.— Address F CLIFTON, Amberlev House, Strand, London. W.O</p>
        <p>—The defendant said he had tried to fulfil all the require- ments necessary to keep the property in s sanitary condition. A little time ago, through Mr. Aaron and Mr. Statham, he made certain alterations to the property at a cost of £2O. He admitted that there was a nuisance, but he alleged it was due to continual floods of water from the adjoining land and from Sandy-lane and South-roai, and as the latter road was now about to be made some provision ought to be made for pro- tecting the property from the continual floods, which under the present circumstances could not be avoided. He called Mr. T. Higginson, surveyor, Widnes, in support of his con- tention. He was willing to abate the nuisance, and Mr. Farrington said the most practical way would be to remove thefive privies.—The magistrates decided to make an order to have the privies and ashpits, emptied and cleaned, and suggested that the defendant should consult with Mr. Farrington, to try and devise some means of permanently removing the nuisance. He was ordered to pay the costs £1 19s. 6d. _ _ _</p>
        <p>THEFT BY A LODGER.—William Henry Tonkin, sailor, of Cornwall, was chorged with stealing ss. balonging to Alice Hughes, shopkeeper, 35, Regent-street, Runcorn, on Satur- day morning From the evidence adduced, it appeared that the prisoner hai been lodging with the prosacutrix, but did not stay there on Friday night. On Saturday morning he was seen in an outhouse new the proseadrix's shop. Sub- sequently the child's money box, the contents of which amounted to 5s , was missed. Information was given to the police and Sergeant Lee with P.C. Larkin visited the Anchor Vaults, Brunswick-street, and found Tonkins standing at the bar. He denied having been near Mrs. Hughes's house, and added that he knew nothing about the robbery. In his possession was found three shillingi pieces and 4d. in coppers.—Prisoner was committed to prison for two months' hard labour. _</p>
        <p>BEGGING.—James Miles was sent for a month's confine- ment to Knntsfor4 Gaol for begging in Ha',ton-road en the 7 inst.</p>
        <p>NICE 3101ITHIUL OF TEETH.</p>
        <p>A GREAT many parents think that all their weak children need is a dose of some specific to give them an appetite, and then trust to luck for the rest. The physi- cian knows better. He knows how serious it is to stuff a baby or a child with drugs. You will find that the doctor looks to the nourishment the little ones assimi_ late. If he finds them weak, lifeless, emaciated, and thin, he knrivs Scott's Emulsion will do them a world of good.</p>
        <p>The doctor understands what Scott's Emulsion has done to widen the scope of cod- liver oil. The mother can only find this out by experience. If your child is not developing as you would like ; if you are getting anxious about it, why don't you try Scott's Emulsion? You sill have no difficulty in its administration, for it is sweet to the taste, and you will find it an aid to digestion rather than a hindrance. It agrees with the stomach wonderfully and is admirably adapted to children. Here is a letter which tells one of thousands of similar stories :</p>
        <p>"I think it my duty to give you a true report of our little boy, who was a:very poor, miserable little thing qp till the time that we began to give him Scott's Emulsion When he was thirteen months old he wcighed jost 8 lbs. He could nct sit up without assistance, could not stand. and was without signs of having any teeth. Everyone that saw him thought it impossible for him to come round ; our friends said he would never be well. But he has ,deceived them Everyone that sees him now is surprised, and will believe him to be the same. You may judge for yourself the difference there is in him. He was 8 lbs. on the 3rd of June, 1895, and 28 lbs. on the 30th of January. 1896—a difference of 17 lbs. under eight month, ; independent of that, he can walk anywhere by himself, and he has got a nice mouthful of teeth. If you would like to publish this letter you are quite at liberty to do do. as we believe it is bcott's Emulsion that has brought to what he is at present I remain, yours truly, (signed) HAYWARD ADAMS: The Lev's, Brockmoor, Brierley Hill, Staffs., February sth, 1896." We might etriphasise that " nice mouthful of teeth." In Scott's Emulsion are the hypophosphites of lime and soda. These valuable hyponhosphites are almost in dispensable during the formation of teeth and the frame work of the body. Many a " nice mouthful of teeth ' are made by Scott's Emulsion, and baby is spared distress in consequence.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-11-14_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>WILL OF BARON LILFORD.</head>
        <p>WILL OF BARON LILFORD.</p>
        <p>Probate of the will, which bears date April 27, 1892, with codicils made September 5 1893, and April 29,1896, of Thomas Littleton, fourth Baron Lilford, of Lilford Hall, Oundle, Northampton,and Bank Hall, Preston,who died on June 17 last, aged 63 years, has been granted to the testator's hrother-in-law, Mr. Thomas Henry Bur- roughes. of 17, Lower Berkeley-street, and Mr. Godfrey John Webb, of 167, Victoria-street. to each of whom Lord Lilford bequeathed £1,000; to a game keeper £150; to another gamekeeper £lOO ; and to each -of his otner servants one year's wages; and to Mrs. Wilson, Lady Lilford's maid £2OO. Lord Lilford bequeathed to his successor in the barony the natural history collection at Lilford Hall, and the furniture and effects there, and he bequeathed in trust to devolve as heirlooms his stuffed specimen of the great auk and his egeshell of the same species. He bequeathed to his wife Clementina Georgi- ana Lady Lilford (daughter of the late Mr. Ker Baillie Hamilton), £2.000 and such furniture and pictures pur- chased by him as she may select. By his will Lord Lilford devised the Wilts estate and Chertsey estate, which he inherited under the will of Mary Augusta Lady Holland, to his second son, the Hon. Stephen Fowls, and he devised and bequeathed to his said son all the residue of his real and personal estate. It was Lord Lilford's desire that his son the Hon. Stephen rOWiB should adopt the name of Fox as a pre- fix to his own name, and the will provides that if tes- tator's said son should become a priest of the Roman Catholic Church or a monk, or should take monastic vows or other% ise be in communion with the Roman Catholic Church, he should forfeit the Wilts and Chert- sey estate and the residuary estate. By a codicil Lord Lilford revoked the devise of Wilts estate to his son the Hon. Stephen Fowl-, and devised the same to his eldest son, the Hon. John Fowls, but subject to a charge of such sum as after making allowance for the value of the Chertsey estate, and for a eurn of £lO,OOO, will make up the portion of his said son Stephen to £60,000. He con- firms the devise of Chertscy estate to his said son Stephan, but he devises and bequeaths all the residue of his real and personal estate to his elder son, the Hon. John PoWis, now filth Baron Lilford. Estate duty has been paid on £50,934 7. 7d., as v,ilue of the late- Lord Lilford's personal estate.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-11-14_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>COMMON DISEASES.</head>
        <p>COMMON DISEASES.</p>
        <p>3.-THE KIDNEYS.</p>
        <p>Next in order of importance to the kidneys play a most important part in the internal economy of the excretory, their functions are solely purifying or excretory, by getting rid of effete products conteined In the blood. Should they from any \cause become in- efficient the uric acid which should be excreted is re- tained in the circulatory system an I elves rise to urinic poisoning—ending fatally. There is no doubt that errors in eating and drinking give rise to all kidney troubles. When the Xor Röntgen rays have been turned on to these wonderful organs the,high liver will be able to see what his excesses have led up to. He will see either the small shrunk kidney caused by excessive indulgence in spirituous liquors, or the large fatty kidney, degenerat- ing as the result of over-eating and highly flavoured flesh food, without having taken the necessary exercise to counteract fatty formations. These few remarks are mainly due to the fact which has been demonstrated be- yond the shadow of a doubt, that Kola,kod Hopalin from Hops, both ingredients in Dr. Titibles' VI-Cocoa, exercise a most beneficial effect on the structional tissues of the kidney, and so en its excretory functions. Dr. Tibbles' Vi-Cocoa is not in any sense a medicine. It is simply a nourishing beverage, and in that respect it plays a most impoitant part in the prevention of func- tional disorders. In these important organs, and others. Kola has a wonderful faculty of giving power to the in- voluntary muscles of the body. By involuntary we understand those muscles not controlled by the will. Those muscles which carry on the work of life without our consent, and unless looked at carefelly, in many instances, without our knowledge; such as the beating of the heart when asleep, the breathing of the lungs. the action of the kidneys, and the digestive process. Kola acts on these in a nourishing and strengthening sense, conserves the strength of these inveluntary muscles, prevents undue waste, and by its beneficent action gives health and vigour to men and Women. As people be- coke more intelligent they see that they should try and prevent disease. It seems strange when one comes to consider it that the efforts of medical science are directed to curing when preventing would seem to be a more rational proceeding. The unlque vitalising and restorative powers of Dr. Tibbles' VI-Cocoa are being recognised to an extent hitherto unknown in the history of any preparation. Merit, and merit alone, is what is claimed for Dr. Tibbles' Vi-Cocoa, and the proprietors are prepared to ' send to any reader who names the Warrington " Eraminer "(a postcard will do) a dainty sample tin of Dr. Tibbles' Pi-Cocoa free and post-paid. Dr. Tibbles' Vi-Cocoa is made up in 61 packets, and 9d. and is. 61 tins. It can be obtained from all chemists, grocers, and stores, or from Dr. Tibbles' Vi- Cocoa, Limited, Suffolk House, Cannon-street, London, E.C.</p>
        <p>SWALLOWING A FLORIN AND THE SEQUEL—The Home Secretary has ordered toe release of William Reynolds,of Lye, near Stourbridge, who was convicted of stealing a fbrin at Worcestershire Michaelmas Sessions, and sen- tenced to nine months' imprisonment. rhe man pleaded that he had accidentally swallowed the coin,and eircum- tances have shown that his story was after all true.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-11-14_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>FOOTB ALL.</head>
        <p>FOOTB ALL.</p>
        <p>(1130, G. BUSSEY &amp; CO., are makers of High Class n.npli ances for Football, Golf, Hockey, (across, and at Sports and Games, also for Gymnasia and Physical Exercises. Illustrated Catalogue may be obtained from bet De %lets or the Sports Manufactory, London, S.B. Trade Mark G.G.B.</p>
        <p>THE NORTHERN UNION. WARRINGTON v. BROUGHTON RANGERS.</p>
        <p>The above teams met on the Wilderspool ground on Saturday. There were about 4,000 spectators on the field, which was slippery from the rain. Both teams turned out as selected, and the Warrington combination was a 3 follows :—Warrington : Heesom, full back; Higginbottom, Barber, Cross and Lawless,three-quarters; Bate and Houghton, half backs ; Nevirn, Hill, Barrett. Scholtze, Taylor Roby, Meacock and Swift, forwards. Hill kicked off for Warrington against the wind ,which was blowing towards the railway. Some exchange kick- ing was then witnessed, Heesom finally kicking in touch at the half-way line. Tbe Rangers wheeled" the first scrimmage and Houghton saved. Bate next took the ball and p :ssed to Houghton. The latter transferred to Lawless who passed to Cross and he was tackled. A useful kick put Warrington on the offensive, and B tte again breaking away made a good run past the visitors' 25 but being unsupported he could not pass. A flying kick from the Rangers travelled towards Barber, but Unfortunately, the latter was deceived by the bounce and missed the ball. This gave the Rangers a grand position in front of the home goal. A neat passing movement resulted in Berry eluding Higginbottom, and running over near the corner Donaldson failed to convert. A couple of free kicks were given to the homesters but were not of much use. Heesom saved well, when the Rangers were coming down with a dan- gerous rush. Barrett was hurt and left the field for a short time. Houghron attempted to relieve by kicking from the scrimmage but Barratt (Broughton) ma :lo his mark and returned into touch. Warrington were kept hard at work defending and a free kick was welcomed. Taylor utilised it but Berry secured and almost dropped a goal. Warrington were next penalised and Donald- son's kick was ch aged down. Messenger passed to Barratt who kicked over the line, Lawless just beating Chapman and knocking the ball dead. At this point Bate had the misfortune to get his shoulder hurt. He was unable to play half back and took Higginbottom's place as threequarter. Hardly was the game going again, when Barrett took a pass and jumped over near the posts. Jones failed to convert from an easy place. Good passing between Houghton, Lawless and Cross looked promising when the whistle sounded. Barber next got away and passed to Lawless but the ball rolled in touch. A cross kick from Lawless almost saw Barber over the line. A scrimmage followed right under the posts and Houehten passed back to Cross who dropped a neat goal. The Rangers became dangerous again and Higginbottom and Barber tackled well. Chapman got well away and was transferring to Berry near the line when Heesom brought him down. Berry made another drop which failed and Lawless gavel on the line. The half time score was,Broughton Rangers two tries, War- rington a dropped goal. Cleminson restarted and his side were soon attacking strongly. Houghton had a kick charged down and another score was only just averted. Tpe visitors were still pressing hcwever and Perry was again pulled down at the corner. Several rushes were only stopped by the vigorous tackling of the homesters. Timely relief was given by Higginbottom who landed the ball in toech at the 25. After some exciting play Warriegton entered the visitors' territery for the first time Ws half, and Barber prevented Berry relieving with one of his strong kicks. I good kick put Warrington well on the offen- sive and from a line out, Houghton got over, but the point was disallowed, much to the chagrin of the home- sters. Cross attempted another drop and had hard lines. Hackh3 relieved for the Rangers and play was de- sultory. The whistle sounded with Warrington pressing almost on the line. Play concluded by Broughton Rin- gers winning by two tries (6 points) to Warrington a dropped goal (4 points).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-11-14_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>RUNCORN v. ROCHDALE ST. CLEMENT'S.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN v. ROCHDALE ST. CLEMENT'S.</p>
        <p>These teams met on the Irwell-lane enclosure, on Saturday afternoon, in miserable weather. The attend. dance was meagre. Through the claims of the county,for the contest at Hull, in which Houghton, Richardson, Butterworth, Langley, Hughes, Gayter and Lightfoot were engaged, the home team was very weak. Then J. Faulkner, Robinson, R. Reece,and H. Farmer also stood down. The composition of the team was Runcorn : Jolley, back ; Warder. Myers, J. Lightfoot,and Gregory, three-quarter backs ; Nickson and Poole, half-backs; W. Faulkner, Walker, F. Darlington, Shaw, Shepherd, T. Reece, Potter, and A. Abel, forwards. Rochdale St. Clement's :—L. Rigg, back ; A. Rigg, H. Kershaw, C. Midgeley, and H. Costello, three-quarter backs; G. Gar- nett and E. Butterworth: half-backs; W. Waugh, J. Waugh, J. S. Tweedle, B. Riddihough, F. Farmer,Grind- rod, Durham, and (Mlle, forwards. Butterworth started for Rochdale, Gregory, returned to Midge- ley, who sent tne ball well in the home half. Rigg returned to the centre, and after a free kick the Runcorn contingent engaged in passing, which did not gain much ground. The St. Clement's got in the Run- Corn half by dribbling, but Warder got possession, and, dodging two of his opponents, put in a flue return, the bail going into touch in the visitors' half. Another bout of passing took place by Runcorn, but Costello neatly intercepted but was held before he could get away. Short rustles on the part of the Rochdale forwards took play to the Runcorn quarters. A free kick to the visi- tors was taken by Butterworth, but gained no ground. Rigg immediately after tried for a goal from a second free kick, but the ball fell short, and Myers obtaining possession, ran down the field and kicked into touch. This was supplemented by smart dribbling and kicking, the ball going over the Rochdale line, but Butterworth ran out in fine style, and put his side out of danger by a neat kick into touch. Again Runcorn attacked. A splen- did bit of passing between Warder, Walker. and Myers saw the latter almost over. After scrimmaging on the line, Myers obtained a free kick, and W, Faulkner made a grand attempt at goal, but the ball just fell outside. On resumption Runcorn again attacked. Rochdale were penalized, but Faulkner failed at goal. The visitors were playing on the defensive for a time, but gradually got out to midfield. Half-time—Rencorn nil, Rochdale nil. After the interval W. Faulkner started for Runcorn, and the home forwards rushed the ball almost to the Roch- dale line. The visitors eventually found relief, and got out to the centre, but dribbling by Potter and Shepherd took play in the Rochdale half again. Warder here had extremely hard lines with a dropped goal,the ball going between the posts, but unfortunately touching one of the Rochdale player's hands in its progress. Runcorn again attacked strongly and the forwards made a com- bined rush to the St. Clement's line, where Nickson got over at the corner. W. Faulkner's kick at goal went wide. On resumption Costello got away with a grand spurt and scored after running more than half the length of the field, Midgeley improving the point from a rather difficult position. Midfield was now the venue of play. Rochdale were penalised and Myers made an attempt at goal,but the ball fell short and 'Ulm returned into touch. Runcorn again assn.-Led the aggressive and made des- perate off ,rts to score, but could not get through, and the game ended-- Rochdale S. Clement's, one goal ; Runcorn, one try.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON "A" v. BROUGHTON RANGERS " Saturday the Warriug:on "A " team paid a visit to Broughton where they played a hotly contested game with the Rangers" A." The day was miserably wet and the ground hard and slippery. There were only about 200 spectators watching the match. Warrington were represented by Boscow, full back; Moores Cooper, Isherwood, and Radford, threequarters ; Moores, and Foden. half-backs; Savage, Andrews, Hughes, Jolley, Higham, Fairhurst, Wilcock, and Thornton, forwards. The homesters kicked off and play soon settled down into a series of scrimmages and forward rushes. Na quick play was possible owing to the wetness of the ground and ball. The visitors worked hard and when near the half-way line Radford took a pass from Foden, dodged all his opponents and scored a splendid try under the posts. Foden easily converted. Soon after, the Rangers by a heavy rush grounded under the Warrington goal, and had very hard lines in tne ball striking the cross-bar from the place-kick. Half-trme arrived with Warrington leading by a goal to a try. In the second half play was 'throughout of a scrambling nature. A good dribble by the visitors enabled Moores to kirk over the line and drop on the ball. Foden's kick failed. This roused the homesters up and by another rush they scored in the corner but failed to convert. From this point ti the end they strove hard to scare again but the Warrington defence was admirable:and the homesters had to retire defeated by one goal one try (8 points) to 2 tries (8 points).</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON Locos V. Sr. HELENS RECREATION A.— This Lancashire Junior League match was played on the Locos ground at Folly-lane on Saturday. Ow ng to the fog at the latter end of the week the railway men were short of several of their best men, and for the first twenty minutes had to play with only 14 men. The home team kicked off, the visitors making a good return and for a short time play was of a give and-take nature. Eventually the Recs. showed a little superiority and from a forward rush near the line scored a try, the place kick failing. From the kick off neither side could claim any advantage and half-time arrived with the aeon reading St. Helens 1 try, Locos nil. In the second half the home team now playing 15 men, had all the best of the play but although they made many good attempts they were unable to cross the visitors' lines, and bad to retire defeated by 1 try to nil. The ground was in miserable condition, and this spoiled what would under favourable conditions have been a hard and well con- tested game.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-11-14_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>ASSOCIATION.</head>
        <p>ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON V. LIVE lIPOOL UNIVERSITY COLLEGE. The Warrington team, somewhat weakened by the ab- absence of three of thtir usual players, journeyed to Liverpool on Saturday to try conclusions with the above club in wretched weather. Up to Satutday both teams held unbroken records,consequently a stiff fight was ex-</p>
        <p>pected. The Liverpool players failed to upheld their reputa.tion,and were decisively beaten by 16 goals to 1. The University kicked off, and the visitors immediately pressed. The start was most sensational, Williamson scoring three goals in twelve minutes,ancl this seemed to take all the heart out of the home team, whe were re- peatedly beaten by the fine passing tactics of Connor, Selby, and Jewkes,and goals were put on at an alarming rate,laalfahne arriving with the score 10 goals to 1 goal. In the second half the game continued to be of a very one-sided nature, and the end came with the score 16 goals to 1 in favour of Warington. Tne goals were scored by E. Connor (s),Williamson (4), Jewkes (3), Selby (2), R. Connor (1), Carter (1). The whole team played beautifully together. FRODSFIAM V. RUNCORN ASSOCIATION.—These Clubs met on the Union-st. ground on Saturday, for the pur- pose of playing their second fixture, ander the auspices of the 'Warrington and District Leaeue.The weather was miserable,and when Mr. Bannon sounded ‘attention"the teams appeared as follows :—Frodsham : Goal, Dime- low ; full backs, Eleffern, Rogers ; half backs, Andrews, Webb, Goodall ; forward3,Warburtors, romaliu,Walker, Peacock, and another. Runcorn : Goal, Casson ; full backs,Tirnperley and Burrows ; half backs, Shaw,Jones, Clarke ; forwards, Burrows, Roberts,Deegan, Lit tler,and Williams. Walker started, and play was soon in the home goal, but Timperley relieved with a good kick. Burrows got away on the right,but Regers kicked clear, the ball hovered near Casson for a few moments, and then a glaring mistake by the home backi allowed 'Fomalin to shoot, and he made no mistake, scoring a beauty after ten minutes play. Howevar the home team made some nice movements, and some exceedingly pretty passing was witnessed. A foul against Fracl- sham gave Runcorn an opportunity. The kick was taken by Jones who sent hard in goal and thus placed the teams on an equal footing the ball striking one of the visitors "en route" to Dimelow. The Frodshain score was soon increased by Walker and Tomalin and the score at half-time was Frodsham 3 goals, Runcorn 1. At the restart it was noticed that Shaw one of the home halves had been compelled to leave the field owing to an injury. This left the Blues with only tour forwards, Roberts having fallen back to fill the vacancy caused by Shaw's retirement, but they did not appear disheartened in the least and played up pluckily against their oppe• ents and with sense smart combination succeeded in again gating the score equal. The game now was very fast, each side struggling desperately for the lead, and ultimately Frodsham succeeded ic running out lucky winners by 4 goals to 3. The home team is now chiefly young blood and is certainly improv:ng rapidly and with a little more experience the players will doubtless form a smart combination.</p>
        <p>PENHETH V. FARNWORTH.—The Penketh football Club did not follow up last week's win, on the occasion of their meeting with Farnworth on Saturday. Penketh as a rule, have had little difficulty in former matches in disposing of this team, but Saturday's result, shows them to be slightly improved. The game, ho weyer,was very hardly contested, the teams being fairly well matched as the result showed. Score, Farnavorth, 1 goal; Penketh, nil.</p>
        <p>NORTHERN UNION: LANCASHIRE SECTION.—At a meet- ing of the Lancashire Section in Manchester on TulsdaY night the following players were chosen to oppose York- shire on the Watersheddings Ground at Oldham on the 21st inst. :</p>
        <p>Eagland (Oldham), full back ; Holmes (Morecambe), S. Lees (Oldham), Vilentine (Swinton), and Chapman (Broughton Rangars), three-quarter backs; A. Lees(Old- ham), and IVarnley (Oldham), half-backs; Clemin- son and Robinson (Broughton Rangers), :Worthing- ton (Tyldesley), Simpson ',Rochdale Hornets). Brown, (Wigan), Edwards (Oldham). Briers (St. Helens), and Bonser (Oldham), forwards. Reserves: Cop (Leigh), full hack; Anderton (Leigh) and Martin (Oldham), threequirter backs ; Messenger (Broughton Ringers), half-bick ; Taylor (Warrington). Greenhalgh (Swinson), Jones (Salford), and Furness (Oldham). for- wards.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-11-14_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>LOCAL HOCKEY MATCHES,</head>
        <p>LOCAL HOCKEY MATCHES,</p>
        <p>PENKETH V. PENDLETON.—Tne Penketh Hockey team met Pendleton on the Penketh ground on Saturday. Two thirty-fives were arranged, and Pendleton began to press immediately. Several deserving efforts made by the visitors were frustrated by the capital form of the home custodian, but eventually Partington succeeded in scoring for the visi-ors. The Penketh forwards played up well, but lacked combination, the opposing defence clearing easily. Pendleton kept the home team Isusy,but nothing further was scored, and half-time arrived with the score one to none in favour of the visitors. In spite of the heavy ground the second half was exceed- ingly fast, and, after some scrambling play in the home circle, Whittaker gave Bradshaw a good pass and he shot a goal. The ground was very rough, and the grass too long for good passing, but the Pendleton forwards combined fairly well under adverse conditions. The home goalkeeper cleared time after time with the whole of the visitors' forwards round him. The P3a- keth team worked very hard individually, but combina- tion was at a discount. The game ended in a victory for Pendleton by two goals to nil. ALTRINCHAM V. GRAPPENHALL.—PIayed On the ground of the former, The visitors only had eight men, the home team playing ten. Two 40's were played, and the home team ran out victors by eight goals to nil. Goals were scored by Schwarz (four), Brewer (two), Gilling (one), and Chorlton (one). AurniNcfrAm A V. STOCKTON HEATIL—Played at Stock- ton Heath. Au enjoyable game resulted in a win for the visitors by three goals to one. Goals were scored for Altrincham by E. Brown, Strutt, and Lionel Brown, and by G. Bennett for Stockton Heath.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-11-14_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>PURIFIED PETROLEUM FOR INCREASING WEIGHT AND STREN(iTfL</head>
        <p>PURIFIED PETROLEUM FOR INCREASING WEIGHT AND STREN(iTfL</p>
        <p>THOUSANDS of people who are "run down" or " out of condition," do not know what is wrong or how to treat themselves. They are not ill enough to need a doctor, yet they are steadily losing weight, strength,and energy, It is needless to say that no single remedy can act iri all such cases, due as they are to different causes. Yet Angler's Petroleum Emulsion, the food- medicide,has been found to work wonders in many cases of this description. The reason why can easily be un- derstood. Angier's Petroleum Emulsion combines the antiseptic action, the soothing and healing effect, and the food value of a highly purified petroleum with the well-known tonic effect of the hypophosphites, and is therefore food and medicine. It promotes appetite, aids digestion and assimilation, and corrects any diseased condition of the digestive organs,while at the same time it tones up the whole nervous system. It is pleasant to take and agrees with the most delicate stomach. We will be pleased to send any invalid a free trial bottle on receipt of 3d. to cover postage. Descriptive pamphlet post free. Extensively prescribed by the medical profes- r ion, and sold by all leading chemists, 23. 9d and 45.6 i. Angier Chemlcal Co., 32, Snow Hill, London, B.C.</p>
        <p>WEEKLY REPORT ON THE TIE A.LTH OF WAItRINGTON. During last week, which was the 44th, of the current year, the following births and deaths were registered :</p>
        <p>Births. Deaths. Warrington 26 38 Latchford</p>
        <p>Total 29</p>
        <p>The excess of deaths over births WAS 10. The death rate from all causes was equal to 34 per 1,000, against 18.2 the average of the previous four weeks, and against 24.6 the corresponding week of last year. Tho death rate from the seven chief zymotic diseases was equal to 6•2 per .1,000, against 1.4 the average rate of the four previous weeks, and against 7.2 the corresponding week of 1895. The ages of mortality were- - 1 1-5 5-15 15-25 25-65 65 up. 12 ,14 1 7 5 38</p>
        <p>The deaths incluaed-1 from diarrhcea,s from whoop- ing cough, 1 from measles, 3 from old age, 5 from debility, 4 from heart disease, 10 from lung disease, 3 from brain disease, 1 from drowning.. The follJwing cases of infectious disease have been reported during the week :—scarlet fever, 2 cases. Total number of patients in hospital on Oct. 310,-22 METEOROLOGY. —Th e mean temperature was 37.4 Fa and therefore 3.4 degrees lower than the preceding week The maximum temperature 55F., was recorded on the 25th inst., and the minimum temperature 25F., on the 29th inst.</p>
        <p>The Rainfall measured 16 inch. The general dime. tion"wa4 N.H. the reading of the Anemometer denoting 1015 miles.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-11-14_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>JOHN H. GORNALL, Medical Officer of Health.</head>
        <p>JOHN H. GORNALL, Medical Officer of Health.</p>
        <p>LYMM.</p>
        <p>DEATH OF MISS M. GREGSON.—We regret to have to announce the death of Miss Mary K. Gregson, daughter of Mr. James Gregson, formerly chairman of the Lymm District Coancil,which took place on Saturday morning after a long illness. Miss Gregson who was only 24 years of age, was a general favourite in the village, and only a year or so ago seemed one of the healthiest and most promising women in the district. The funeral took place on Thursday afternoon,at the Lymm Parish Church. Much sympathy is felt in the neighbourhood for Mr. and Mrs. Gregson.</p>
        <p>RANGES.—See the Herald Unproved Lifting Fire liangea.—Sole Agent, GARTAND, Bridge-Ltreet.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-11-14_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>ANNUAL MEETING OF THE TOWN COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>ANNUAL MEETING OF THE TOWN COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>ELECTION OF MAYOR.</p>
        <p>At noon on Monday the annual meeting of the Town Council for the election of Mayor, appointment of com- mittees, and other business, was held in the Council Chambsr at the Town Hall. There were present :—The Mayor (Alderman James Fairclough). the Deputy-Mayor (Alderman Greenall), Aldermen W. Bolton, R. W. Fran- comb, J. Harrison, T. Hutchinson. T. H. Sutton, and .1. E. Wright; Councillors J. Baird, A. Bennett, T. Bur- ton, J. Cannell, J. Crosfield, D. S. Duncan, John Evans, L. Ellisons J. Garner, J. Grace, J. S. Green, R. Heaton, A. Jolley, John Lee, H. J. Long- ton, F. W. Monks, W. Pierpoint, G. Plump- ton, ' H. Roberts, W. Rogers, J. Shillcock, J. Smethurst, D. Tinnion, J. Wilkinson, A. Woods ;J. L. Whittle (town clerk), S. Wilson (assistant town clerk), J. Fairhurst (borough;treasurer), T. Longdin (borough surveyor), J. H. Gornall (medical officer), C. Madeley (librarian). There was a good attendance of the public, which included Messrs. F. Monks, J.P., C.C., G. F. F. White (deputy magistrates' clerk). Thomas Birtles, Wm. Tinsley,W. Gill,Jamas Bolton George Clay,John Taylor, T. B. Malkin, William Dickens, Hugh Fairhurst, John Clayton, and others.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-11-14_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>ELECTING THE MAYOR.</head>
        <p>ELECTING THE MAYOR.</p>
        <p>The Tower CLERK having read the names of the suc- cessful candidates at the recent Municipal Election, Alderman Fairclough then left the Council Chamber' and on the proposition of Alderman Harrison, seconded by Councillor Monks, the Deputy-Mayor (Alderman Greenall) was unanimously chosen to take the chair. Alderman HARRISON said he supposed his paternity was of such an unquestionable character, as he was dubbed the other day " the father of the Council," that it was his duty to make a proposition. That was the third time that the present Mayor had been called upon by his fellow townsmen, with the concurrence of their, representatives who sat in that chamber, to occupy the position of Mayor; in other words to climb the highest pinnacle of municipal distinction. As the Mayor had consented to accept office again, it would not be neces- sary for him to say many words. They must remember that they were not dealing with a young, inexperienced, and erratic man, but with a man of mature age and mature judgment, with a inan possessing those qualities which were calculated to make him very familiar indeed with what honours were and with what duties meant. The year on which they were that day entering was very likely to be both an eventful one and an interesting one. It would be interesting because it would mark the age of their municipality, and it would be eventful be- cause it would enable her Majesty's loyal sub ects to rejoice with her and to congratulate her upon hav- ing attained the 60th year of her reign. That was a long time surely in which it had been given to her Majesty to do what he knew she would do, namely, to impress the beneficence of her reign upon the annals of our time. They were all looking forward with very great gratification to the Mayor taking his seat and being with them for another year. His presence would afford them the greatest gratifica- tion, and he hoped and believed that his Mayoralty would be a gratification to him, for every man who loved his town must feel proud to hold the position of Mayor; and he was holding it with such satisfaction to the public as to ensure his being thrice elected. It was a very good augury indeed,and he hoped the next twelve months would be a happy period to him. (Hear, hear.) On the 23rd September last her Majesty overtopped and broke the record of all her royal predecessors, not even excepting those who had reigned the longest. We began with Edward 111., who reigned 50 years; Henry 111., reigned 57 years, and George 111., her uncle, 59 years. He thought they had reason to be proud of their Queen, and they had also reason to honour the memories of Queen Elizabeth and Queen Anne, the three noble ladies who had presided over the destinies of this country so faithfully and so well. Their reigns were marked by national achievements of great consequence, and all of them deserved well of the public—her Majesty Queen Victoria more especially, because she was living in their lives, and, therefore they were the more intimately con- nected and acquainted with her life than they were with that of her predecessors. Their reigns were magnificent in results that promoted human happiness,but he should not be surprised at all if the "new woman," who was rapidly coming to the front, and who promised at no distant period of time to efface them, were to get up a memorial and march down with it to the Houses of Par- liament, and insist upon having entered upon the Statute Book a sort of inverted Salic law that should preclude any man occupying the throne of England. (Laughter.) But he hoped that would not be passed, and if any at- tempt of the kind were to be made the manhood of the three kingdoms would rise in one eolid phalanx to oppose such a monstrous proposal. But he did not anticipate anything of the kind. Having trespassed upon their time a little further than he ought:to have done—(laugh- ter, and hear, hear)—he now proposed that Alderman James Fairclough be appointed Mayor for the ensuing year, and until his successor be appointed. (Ap- plause.) Councillor Remmers said on behalf of his colleagues and himself, he had very much pleasure in seconding the proposition just made by Alderman Harrison. It was not his intention. to traverse the same ground which be had so eloquently depicted, not that he thought it had a remote connection with that occasion, but because he thought it was not necessary to do so. They wished to recognise how faithfully and assiduously the Mayor had for two years performed the honourable and important duties of his office. And it was because they felt that it was necessary to recognise that spitit on his part, that action on his part, and also to provide fox the office dur- ing the coming year a man who had had considerable experience in all departments, that they felt it desirable to support the re-election of Alderman James Fairclough as Mayor. They also wished to take that opportunity of recognising how exceedingly charmingly the Mayoress had performed the duties of her office. (Hear, hear and applause.) She had by her universal courtesy and per- sonality endeared herself to everyone in the town ; and in according that public testimony to the value of the services of the Mayor, they should not be doing justice to themselves if they overlooked the valuable services which had been rendered to him and to the town by his wife. (Applause.) They supported the nomination on other grounds. They hoped that the little party,bicker- ings which had occasionally arisen, and which had not lent any charm to their discussions, might with this new municipal year come to an end; at any rate they hoped they might be considerably modified. Warrington was threatened during next year with proposals from outside which were of such a character that there was no room for division in their own ranks; at any rate, so far as those proposals were concerned. Again, there was the 1 matter which had been eloquently referred to by Alder- man Harrison, namely, the longest reign in the history of this country, and it made it extremely desirable that we should present a united front in everything that appertained to the good government of the borough during the coming year. He hoped everyone would do his utmost to oppose any movement which threatened either the commercial condition or the health of the town from whatever quarter it might come,so that they might unitedly and strongly support any proposition which had for its object the social, material, and the religious improvement of the town. (Hear, hear and applause.) Alderman SUTTON said he was very pleased to support the resolution. It was his pleasure and privilege two years ago to propose the election of Mr. Fairclough as Mayor,and again last year to- support his re-election,and he thought the excellent way in which he had fulfilled the duties of the office during thefe two years must have convinced everyone of them that their confidence in him was not in the slightest demi:* misplaced. As had been stated, this next year would be an important one,perhaps one of the most important in the history of the borough, and he thought they all knew sufficient of Mr. Fairclough to feel satisfied that he would do his ut- most during the next year to make it one of the happiest and most prosperous years they had known. (Ap- plause.) , Councillor CROSFIELD said es an old and young mem- ber of the Council—(laughter)—he should like to say a few words about hie old friend the Mayor. He had known him for a greet many years, and had always found him an upright and what he celled a strong man. He had read in the newspapers of his conduct in the Council Chamber as Mayor for the last two years,and he had admired the way in which he had conducted the affairs of the town. He was very glad indeed that he had consented to accept the office of Mayor for the third time,and they in the Council and the inhabitants of Warrington might congratulate themselves that this next year, which was going to be one of the most im- portant yeare in the century, they should have a Mayer like Mr. Fairclough.who would be able to do the honours in a proper and satisfactory manner. (Applause.) The motion was then put to the meeting and carried unanimously. The Meson was then apprised of the result, and was brought into the room by several other members of the Council. In acknowledging he said he thanked them all for the honour, they had done him by electing him Mayor for the third time. He might say that in the past he had tried to do his duty with their kind assistance, and now that the elections were over, and each of them had got control of their unruly passions—(laughter)—he hoped they would all settle down to do some real good and honest work for limo town (Applause.) His Worship then informed the Counci, that he would attend the Parish Church on Sunday, and he invited the aldermen and councillors to accompany him. If the weather was unfavourable vehicles would be provided, 89 that the members might not get wet but sit in-comfort during the service. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-11-14_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>DEpIITY MAYOR AND CHAPLAIN.</head>
        <p>THE DEpIITY MAYOR AND CHAPLAIN.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR then re-appointed Alderman Greenall, Deputy-Mayor of the Borough, and stated that the Rev. E. C: E. Carleton, would t.H his (the Mayor's) chaplain. THE MEETINGS AND STANDING ORDERS OF THE COI3NCIL.</p>
        <p>On the proposition or th,A Mayor it was decided that there should be three quarterly meetings of the Council, cra the Ist February,MAy.and Augusts and that monthly meetings be held on the Ist Tuesday in each month (ex- cepting February, May, and August, and an additional</p>
        <p>meeting on the last Tuesday in October. The Standing Orders for the regulation of the proceedings of the Coun- cil and for the government of the comsnittees, as in ex- istence heretofore, were also approved of.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-11-14_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>COMPOSITION OF THE COMMITTEES.</head>
        <p>THE COMPOSITION OF THE COMMITTEES.</p>
        <p>The committees will be composed as follows : WATCH.—It was moved by the MAYOR seconded by Councillor HEATON and resolved that the following mem- bers of the Council, viz.:—Aldermen- Blackhurst, Fair- clough, Francomb, Greenall, Harrison, Sutton' Wright, and Councillors Cannel!, Heaton. and Monks, form the Watch Committee.</p>
        <p>FINANCE AND ESTATE.-011 the motion of Councillor &amp;taw GREEN it was resolved that this committee should consist of Aldermen Blackhurst, Fairclough, Greenall, Councillors Bennett, Fleming, Shaw Green, Lee, Long- ton, Monks, and Woods. STREET IMPROVEMENT AND LIGHTING. Alderman SUTTON moved, and it was agreed to that the following members should sit on this committee :—Aldermen Blackhurst, Bolton,Harrison, Sutton,and Wright ; Coun- cillors Bennett, Furness, Heaton, Longton, and Plump- ton.</p>
        <p>HEsms.—This committee will be composed as follows: —Alderman Sutton ; Councillors Baird. Furness, Grace, Pierpoint, Plumpton, Roberti, Shillcock, Smethurst, and Wilkinson.</p>
        <p>SANITARY WORICS.—Mr. MONKS moved and it was agreed to that this committee should be comprised of the following members :—Councillors Baird, Bennett, Duncan, Evans, Monks, Pierpoint, Shillcock, Smethurst, Tinnion, and Wilkinson. PAVING AND SEWERAGE.—This committee is to consist of Aldermen Hutchinson and Wright; Councillors Baird, Burton,Grace, Heaton, Jolley,Plumpton, Smethurst,and Tinnion.</p>
        <p>Smoot. ATTENDANCE.—To be composed of the follow- ing :—Alderman Hutchinson ' • Councillors Baird, Dun- can, Ellison, Garner, Sha‘v Green, Jolley, Rogers, Shill- cock, Smethurst, Wilkinson, and Woods. MUSEUM, LIBRARY, ARTS,AND TECHNICAL INSTRUCTION. —Councillor ROBERTS moved and it was agreed to that this committee be composed of Alderman Francomb, J.P., C.C. ; Councillors Bennett. C.A., Crosfield. J.P., Ellison,Furni-;s, Shaw Green,F.S.A.A., F.R.S.S., Monks, Pierpoint, J.P., Plumpton, Roberts, J.P., and Woods; and the following non-Members of the Council, viz. : Messrs. William Owen, F.R.1.A., J.P., Robert Pierpoint, M.P. J. E. Worsley, F S.A., A. Jolley, John Peers, F. Monks, J.P., C.C.. J. F. Dutton, L. Greening, F.L.S., M.R.1.A., and Thomas May. CEMETERY.—The committee for the management of the Cemetery to be :—Alderman Harrison ; Ceuncillors Baird, Cannell,Duncan, Evans, Heaton, Jolley,Shillcock, Wilkinson, and Woods. TOWN HALL, PARK AND BATHS.—The following will compose this committee :—The Mavor,Aldermen Bolton, Fransomb, Greenall, Harrison, Sutton, and Councillors Cannell, Ellison. Heaton, and Smethurst. MARKETS.—This committee will consist of the follow- ing members :—Aldermen Francomb, Greenall, Itutchin son ; Councillors Fleming, Garner, Shaw Green, Lee, Rogers, Shillcock, and Woods. _</p>
        <p>Gas.—On the proposition of the MAYOR the following were appointed on this committee :—Aldermen Bolton, Fairclough, Greenell, and Wright; Councillors Duncan, Evans, Heaton, Longton, Pierpoint, and Tinnion. RP/RIM—On this committee there will be :—Aldermen Bolton, Fairclough (Mayor), Francomb, Sutton; Coun- cillors Bennett, Crosfield, Heaton, Monks, Fierpoint, and Roberts.</p>
        <p>Finn ENGlNE.—Alderman Hureimrsox moved, and Councillor SMETHURST seconded that the following be appointed on this committee :—Alderman Hutchinson, Councillors Baird, Burton, Duncan, Grace,Jolley,Plump- ton, Rogers Smethurst, and Tinnion.—Councillor MONKS said he was unable to be present at the meeting when the committees were arranged but it was his in- tention if he had been there to propose that the duties of the Fire Engine Committee should be carried out by the Water Committee. There was not much business for the Fire Engine Committee, ,nd it would be an advantage to have a smaller number of committees. It would perhaps also save any possibility of friction between the two com- mittees—the Fire Engine Committee and Water Com- mittee—such as had happened in the past. He could not help thinking that the duties of the Fire Engine Com- mittee would be just as well carried out by a sub-corn- mittcv of the Water Committee. He mentioned the subject new so that it might be considered on the nest occasion.—The committee was then appointed as selected.—Alderman Francomb in alluding to the sub- ject said it was intended to place the chairman of the Fire Engine Committee on the Water Committee in order to avoid any little feeling or friction. He thought it would work very much better in the future. (Hear, hear.)</p>
        <p>WATER.—For this committee the following were ap- pointed :—Aldermen Blackhurst. Bolton, Francornb, Hutchinson, Councillors Evans, Fleming, Garner, Shaw Green. Lee and Roberts.</p>
        <p>GYMNASlUM.—Councillor Txxxicot moved, and Alder- man WRIGHT seconded that the following form this com- mittee :—Aldermen Sutton, Wright, Councillors Evans, Smethurst, Tinaion, and Messrs. lhomas Morris, D. Jones, H. Ferguson, J. Hesketh, and George Vernon.— Councillor SMETHURST thought this would be a superflu- ous committee. His opinion was that the work con- nected with the Gymnasium should be done by the Parr Hall Committee. He did not see why they wanted two separate committees.—The Mayor pointed out that the Parr Hall Committee was a sub-committee of the General Purposes Committee and not a separate committee. GENERAL PURPOSES.—This committee will consist of the Mayor, Deputy-Mayor, every member of the Council who has held the office of Mayor, and the chairman and deputy-chairman of the several Committees (being mem- bers of the Council).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-11-14_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>PRESIDI NG ALDERMEN.</head>
        <p>PRESIDI NG ALDERMEN.</p>
        <p>The presiding aldermen were appointed as follows : Town Hall Ward, Alderman Greenall ; Whitecross Ward, Alderman Hutchincon ; Bewsey Ward, Alderman Fair- dough; Orford Ward, Alderman Harrison ; St. John's Ward, Alderman Sutton ; Fairfield Ward, Alderman Wright; Howley Ward, Alderman Francomb; St. Austin's Ward, Aiderman Blackhurst ; Latchford Ward, Alder- man Bolton.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-11-14_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>THE_ DEATH OF LADY OREENALD</head>
        <p>THE_ DEATH OF LADY OREENALD</p>
        <p>The MAYOR referred to—th-e--deaiii—Of lady Greenall. Everyone knew Lady Greenall from the good work she bad done for a long time, and how charitable she had been to various local institutions. He then moved the following vote of condolence, "That this Council re- ceives with deep regret the announcement of the death of Lady Greenall, the widow of the late Sir G. Greenall, Bart., and a former member of the Council and for nearly 34 years the representative in Parliament of the borough, and requests the Town Clerk to convey to Sir Gilbert Greenall, Bart., and the Misses Greenall the sympathy and condolence of the Council with them in their bereavement," _ _ _</p>
        <p>Councillor SHAW GREEN seconded the vote, and spoke of Lady Green;tll's beneficence to their institutions. The vote was carried.</p>
        <p>The Council then rose.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-11-14_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>SOURCE OF MOST ILLS.</head>
        <p>THE SOURCE OF MOST ILLS.</p>
        <p>Depression, melancholy, and miserable feelings gener ally must be attributed to a deranged liver. No trouble causes so many disturbances in the entire organism as a sluggish and torpid lirr.</p>
        <p>_ The liver has many functions, the principal being the extraction of bile from the blood—abut 3ilbs. being produced daily. Bile is Nature's true cathartic. Like fire, bile is a good servant and a bad master! If, through any cause, some of it should be thrown back into the blood, trouble instantly ensues—headache, indi- gestion,mal-assimilation, constipation, palpitation of the heart,coated tongue.vomiting,piles, jaundice,irritability, etc., are the most frequent symptoms met with. It was to cure these disorders of the system that Warner's Safe Cure was specially designed. In the most trying cases this great remedy never once fails to give prompt relief. It is popular everywhere, because it is always reliable and effective. It Is the ONLY REAL SPECIFIC FOR LIVER TROUBLES. There is no doubt about this. Thousands have so testified. Be self-convinced through personal proof.</p>
        <p>SUCCESS OF A WARRINGTON SCHOOL OF ART</p>
        <p>STUDENT.</p>
        <p>From the appended cutting it will las Oen that Mr. W. G. Knight, formerly a pupil at the Warring- ton Sohool of Art, is making his mark as Master of the Weston-super-Mare School of Art, which posi- tion he has occupied during the past three years. in the course of a review of the annual exhibition of students' work held last week, the " Weston Mercury "says :—" The ppgress of the local School of Art during recent years`has undoubtedly been of a most rapid character, bet, whilst this has been the case, the golden maxim Festina lente has been kept well in view, with the result that the work turned out by the numerous students is devoid of the least approach to superficiality. This, to a very great ex- tent, is due to the thorough and painstaking exer- tions of the headmaster (Mr. W. G Knight), well backed up as he is by a capable staff of assistants, who must have indeed been gratified at the unanim- ous tribute of praise which was accorded by the numerous Westonians who inspected at the School of r, on Saturday last and the following Monday and Tuesday, the exhibition of students' woik held in accordance with annual customs. Some of the examples were remarkably clever in conception and execution, and would have graced a far more pretentious exhibition. The headmaster (Mr. Knight), had some splendid examples hung, includ- ing his Academy portrait of Miss Tyler—one of the cleverest pieces of work of clever artist; a juvenile head in oils, and a portrait of Miss Theobalds being also particularly noticeable. An aesthetic fail length study in pastels of a lady, also by Mr. Knight, was a most effsctive piece of colouring, and his portraits of Mr. S. E. Baker and Mr. H. St. John Joyner were lifelike."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-11-14_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>GREAT SANKEY PARISH COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>GREAT SANKEY PARISH COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>LIVELY PROCEEDINGS.—A MEMBER LEAVES THE ROOM. A meeting of this Council was held on Monday even- ing limier the presidency of Mr. H. Bate. There were also present :—Messrs. G. Thompson, W. H. L.-Gorse, W. Taylor, Thomas Kilshaw, T. Halton, and J. Wright (clerk). THE CANAL BRIDGE AT SANKEY BRIDGES. The Clerk read the correspondence which had taken place on this subject between the Council and the Lon. don and North-Western Railway Company. In compli- ance with a request of the Council, a representative of the Railway Company (Mr. Andrews) had met a deputa. tion of the Council on the previous Friday with reference to the bridge. A letter was then read from Mr. W. H. Gorse, giving notice to the clerk that it was his inten- tion to refer to the report of the proceedings of the last meeting of the Council as regarded the widening of the Sankey Canal main road bridge.—Mr. Gorse then read a report in which it was stated that Mr. Halton had asked if any member of the Council had written personally to the London and North-Wcstern Railway Company with reference to the bridge at Sankey Bridges, and express. lug regret that Mr. Gorse was absent. Mr. Gorse said that any of the parishioners outside the Council reading that statement would dra.v the conclusion—and they had done so—that he had written to the railway com- pany. Mr. Halton,no doubt,wished to convey that idea, or why did he make use of his (Mr. Gorse's) name so continuously? Be would have been at his post but he was away on business, and could not get home in time; but he might tell them that he never wrote a letter to the company on the subject mentioned, neither had he had any conversation with any official connected with the company, nor yet had be ever said anything against the widening of Sankey Bridge in any shape or form. No gentleman round that Board could say he had (lane so. (Hear,hear.) And there was another matter. He could tell them that he was not prepared to sit there as a parish councillor any longer. That was his last ap- pearance.and he wished to thank them for the kinelness they had given him before. That was all he had to say, and he now left the matter with them. He intended to retire now. The lighting question he left with them, as they seemed to have taken it up. Mr. Gorse then picked up his hat and proceeded to the door. Mr. Halton: You are not giving me a chance to reply. Mr. Gorse : I don't wish to hear your reply. Mr. Halton: That is quite satisfactory. Mr. Gorse: I have, read the extracts, and they are quite sufficient. The Chairman: It would he mere satisfactory if-- Mr. Gorse: There ri els no more ro4ply than is in the paper. There are no two onini ns about the matter. I cannot sit here to have such attacks upon me. lam of a quiet disposition, and won't have them. _ _</p>
        <p>The Chairman: I think it is possible that there is some satisfaction— Mr. Halton : Let Mr. Gorse go if he wishes to go. I am here to defenc, myself. Mr. Gorse then left the room. • Mr. Halton said he thought he had a right to reply even if Mr. Gorse bad disappeared so mysteriously. He proceeded to refer to what took place at the previous meeting. He failed to see where an injustice had been committed when it had been practically admitted by Mr. Thompson to him that he had been in communica- tion with the London and North Western Railway Com- pany with reference to the bridge. Mr. Thompson's words were—" I have verbally inquired from the men at the bridge once or twice about the new bridge, and from what I heard I gathered that the new bridge was already made." He (Mr. Halton) was present that even- ing to defend his position. He was sorry Mr. Gorse had left the room. If he had remained he could have satiS• fled his views by a few questions. Mr. Gorse had run away with a wrong and erratic idea. The bridge Qies. tion had been opposed by one or two members of the Council ever since the last Parish Council election. Why he did not know. He shOuld like it to go forth to the public that some of these erratic proceedings had been caused through certain members who thought a griev- ance had been done to the late Chairman.</p>
        <p>Mr. Thompson : I object to these remarks being made They are foreign to the subject. Mr, Balton: lamon my feet for the first time. Mr. Thompson: Your remarks are foreign to the sub. ject. Mr. Halton: I have a right be speak. I come here to do my duty, and I am not going to be tied by the hands of anyone. One member has left the room. I come here to do a duty, and I mention these facts to show that I stand here fearlessly of anyone. lam not going to have my name read out at a public meeting as having said something ,detrimental to a member of the Council.</p>
        <p>Mr. Thompson said he regretted exceedingly the action Mr. Gorse bad taken in the matter. They must all ad. mit he had been an excellent member of the Council. He was very quiet, but he was a shrewd business man, and he was sure his leaving the Council would be a loss. He thought they all felt that. It had been mentioned that some one had written about the bridge. At the last meeting of the Council when the width of the bridge was mentioned at 24ft., Mr. Halton thought someone had been writing to the Railway Company with respect to it. He (Mr.--Thompson) knew no more than the table before him the width of the bridge, and he could not, therefore, he charged with writing a letter_in that way but before the matter was danitelv brought forvcard at the Council he asked the men about the bridge if they had any information whether th bridge was to be made or not. One said "My information is that the Midge is made." He wrote to the London and North-Western Railway Company as a private individual, and be had a perfect right to do so. He was not tied hand and foot to the Council, and members did not come to the meet- ing to be catechized as to their action outside. He wrote to an official, and he believed the reply he got was that there was no bridge made, and that they did not intend to make one then, but he took so little notice of it that he had nearly forgotten all about the matter. He had told Mr. Halton privately that he had never put it to paper until last week, when he did write to Mr. Steven- son privately to ask him to be good enough to send his deputy to attend to the matter, because they. thought the matter an exceedingly important one. He believed his wilting to Mr. Stevenson had something to'do with Mr. Andrews being sent down to meet a deputation from the Board.</p>
        <p>Mr. Halton : Then it is true you have superseded the Council. We pay the Clerk to write to Mr. Stevenson, and why should you write privately to him or to any gentleman connected with the London and North-Wes- tern Railway Company? If I had written a private letter to the London and North-Western Railway Com- pany with reference to the work of the Council you would have said at once that I had superseded the work of the Council.</p>
        <p>The Chairman : I think if every member wrote privately we should get very much astray. The Clerk: If I am not conpetent for the purpose,Me. Thompson can take my work up. Mr. Thompson: I don't wish to do so. The Chairman : It is looking very much in that light. Mr. Halton: If you (Mr. Thompson) had mentioned this matter at the last meeting it would have saved all this unpleasantness this evening. I am exceedingly sorry. I have known Mr. Gorse for the last twenty years, and I felt very much hurt to see him walk out of the room.</p>
        <p>Tbe Clerk: I have four more meetings to attend tos and they will be my lash. The Chairman: What has taken place is in conse- quence of Mr. Thompson's action. Mr. Thompson: Nothing of the kind. A discussion took place betwoen Mr. Halton and Mr. Thompson. The latter contended that his action had nothing to do with the delay in the matter, but would rather help to mediate it than otherwise. 11,, now left his action in the hands of the ratepayers. He did not care what the members thought of his ac'ion. He left it in the hands of those who elected him. When the time came they could either re-elect or reject him. It did not matter much to him what they did. The Chairman: Little did I th;uk any member of the Council would write independently of that body. The matter then dropped. Mr. Thompson then moved, and Mr. Halton seconded, and it was decided that the Clerk write to Mr. Stevenson thanking him for allowing his assistant, Mr. Andrews, to meet a deputation from the Council on the subject of the bridee.</p>
        <p>PROPOSED ADDITIONAL GAS The Clerk read some correspondence whin.) had taken place between himself and Mr. Taylor, of the Warring- ton Gas Works, on the subj Pct of the cost ot gas lamps from Messrs. Houghton's Works to Sankey Chapel. Mr. Taylor wrote that the charge would be £1 104. 91 per lamp, but the cost of lighting and extinguishing must be added when the number of lamps had been erected be- tween Mr. Houghton's works and the chiir.th Mr. Halton proposed that they have six lamps —Mr. Thompsoe seconded, and the mo'ion was c trried. It was also decided that the Chairman, Mr. 1141t00. and the Clerk consult with the Gas Company as to the Disens where the lamps should be put down.</p>
        <p>MR. HALTON AND THE INCREASE OF RATE. Mr. Halton said it bad been mootel in the clis;tr7ct during the last few days that he was persrm.llv the cause of the rates being increased hy 41.1 n t h S.ich a statement was without the slightest foundati•bo, and at..d he gave it an absolute denial. He shnul I like to know if any member of the Cu Cil had heard anything about the matter. •</p>
        <p>Mr. Thompson and other memhers denkd all know- ledge of the matter. The Clerk said the rates were hia,her. At th.l Iv; town's meeting some filling up of drains was done in the district around Liverpool-road and the Sloop, which )4 £4O. They could not do the work for 3 1., and the rte had ta be made into 4d. It was a speci 41 ra Mr. Thompson : They cannot blame Mr. Halton. Ile is only one member. The Council then rose.</p>
        <p>CUTLERY and Eleetro-Plated GOODS.—C APJAND. - Bridge-street, Warrinton—Advt•</p>
        <p>WESLEY S HYMNS. Sankey '3 Son, Bib'es, Pr4ver. Books. and Stationery in great Variety at J,lty GREEN'S, 34, Bridge-st. &amp; 23, Butterworka-st , Warring ton.—Advt.</p>
