      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-11-14_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>'WARRINGTON BANKRUPTCY</head>
        <p>'WARRINGTON BANKRUPTCY</p>
        <p>COURT. HEAVY FAILURE OF LOCAL BUILDERS.</p>
        <p>Before Mr. Registrar Ridgway at Warrington. on Fri- day,Thomas Cross, residing at 34, West-street, Warring- ton, and Walter Cross, 10, West-street, and carrying on business together under the style of T.-and W. Cross, at Longford-street.as builders,came up for their first public examination in bankruptcy. Mr. A. Browne and Mr. Forshaw represented debtors, and a number of other debtors were also present. The bankrupts affairs show liabilities £1,897,and assets, after deducting preferential debts, £lO6, leaving a deficiency of £1,791. In their statement to the Official Receiver the bankrupts allege as the cause of their failureoverbuilding,want of capital and law costs. The separate statement of affairs of Thomas Cross shows a deficiency of £8 is. Bd., but Walter Cross discloses no liabilities or assets. The bank- rupts state they commenced business,as builders,in 1891, with a capital of £lO. Thomas Cross, who had been carrying on business as a jobbing bricklayer since 1884, without capital, appeared to heve pain a composition of 7s. 6d. in the £, in or about 1888, on liabilities under £100; and Walter Cross, who had been carrying on a like business from the same date, and also without capi- tal,appeared to have executed a deed of assignment for the benefit of his crediters about 1886. He alleged his then liabilities were about £l2o,but that he did not know what dividend the creditors received. The bankrupts further stated that it was impossible from their books tend papers to prepare a reliable deficiency account. They could only state that the deficiency was accounted for by loss on building, law costs, etc. They alleged they had kept no cash book or ledger, or indeed any other book of accoent, nor, during the past three yam, pre- pared any balance sheet or statement of affairs; and that they first knew of their insolvency about the end of 1895. They admitted that they had contracted the bulk of the debts now owing with such knowledge. They had had about 20 actions brought against them during the past six months ; the debts and costs in these bad all been paid. rhey entered appearance in a number of cases,but only with the object of gaining time for pay- ment. About a week before the petition they destroyed all writs and summonses. They thought they were use- less. They had drawn, practically, nothing ou: of the building business for the past three years; they made about 30s. a week each, working at a forge, and they lived an that. The joint creditors fully secured, whose claims were for money lent,held,reapectiveiy,as security, mortgages and charges on the following leaseheed pro- perties :-1.8 houses and land in Melville-strek,l9 houses and land in Sharpe-street and Annie-street,7 houses and land in ale-street, 12 houses and land in Bowes-street, and ten houses and land in Folly-lane, all in Warrington. The joint creditors partly secured, whose respective claims were for goods, money lent, and bank overdraft, respectively held as security charges upon the foregoing properties (except in Folly-lane, and upon unfinished property situate in Earlestownil The bankrupt, Thomas Cross appeared to have handed a ife policy to his son John as security for three pre- miums he had paid ; and the bankrupt, Walter Cross, a life policy for £lOO, payable in 1913, to a company as security for a loan of £4O. The wife ot Thomas Cross claimed the bulk of the furniture at 34, West-steet, on the ground that she derived it•froin her first husband. The mother-in-law and the wife of Walter Cross claimed the furniture at 10, West-street, the latter as having Purchased her share out of money given to her by the former. Mrs. Walter Cross pure lased the yard in Long- ford-street from them about 2 years ago, for £5O. She had lent them £5O on the security of this yard ; and as they could not repay her, they sold her the property for £5O. They could not say what the yerd had cost them. Both bankrupts were examined at considerable length by Mr. Potter, the Assistant Official Receiver. Mr. Burkitt, one of the creditors, then questioned the bank- rupt, Walter Cross. He asked him what amount in wages he received regularly'from the forge?—Sometimes nothing; sometimes £l.—You have told me that you never took one penny piece out of the building trade,and that you and your brother received £4 a week P—l didn't tell you anything of the kind.—You showed me a balance sheet ?—No sir.—Did you produce one properly drawn up with the equities on every block of property and els° the stock-in-trade which brought the balance down in your favour of from £4,000 to £5,000? That was at the time I served you with a writ, and after- wards continued to trade with you because I thought it was a true statement ?—lt was only roughly drawn upi and afterwards destroyed.—How long is it since you bought land at Hindley Green ?—That belongs to John ' Cross.—Did you take it in his name P— It was leasheld and was transferred to him. rhe debtor was neglecting to answer the questicns in a proper manner when the Assistant Official Receiver in- terfered, and addressing the Registrar said the debtor was extremely impudent in answering the creditors' questions at the meeting of creditors, and seeing that he had a tendency to be so again he (Mr. Potter) thought it was time that the debtor was reprimanded for his con- duct.—The Registrar' addressing the debtor, told him to consider the position he stood in, and to answer the questions he was asked in a proper manner. Continuing, Mr. Burkitt asked the debtor what goods had been removed from Longford-street to Hindley Green P—A.beut £lO worth.—ls there any account of it in the statement P—The Official Receiver: No.—ls that all that was sent to Hindley Green P—Debtor Yes.— Wasn't there £lOO worth P—No.--Elow long would it take to cart £lO worth ?—I don't know.—Can you tell me how many loads there were P—About two or three. Mr. Browne said itvas his intention to ask only two or three questions until he obtained the report of the Committee of inspection. He asked the debtor whether two carts were not employed daily for a fortnight cart- ing goods to Hindley Green ?—lt is not true.—Who is the owner of the goods at Hindley Green P—Thomas Cross.—Who is Thomas Cross ?—He is Mr. Thomas Cross's son.—What was the stuff sent P—Some timber; about £lO worth.—How was it paid for ?—He lent me money to file the petition. The Assistant Official Receiver after having warned the debtor, said he had in his hand a letter stating deli nitely that two carts were loaded every morning at the debtors' yard at Warrington for a fortnight, and the materials sent to a place near Wigan. Was that true ? The debtor : No, sir, it is not true.—The Assistant Official Receiver: I think you do not understand the position you are placed in.—Mr.Browne : The creditors think it is a swindle and that the property at Hindley is put in the sores name to defeat the other creditors.—The Assistant Official Receiver (to the debtor Walter Cross) : Rave you anything further to say in respect to this Matter or any explanation to give P —Loads of goods Were not sent away every morning to Hindley Green.— Is it not a fact that you destroyed the writs and sum- Menses and books so that you could conceal from me the time you first became aware of your insolvency P No, sir.—What object had you in view ?—They were of no further use to me.—You have said that you destroyed them about a week before you filed your petition P—l eestroyed them from time to time.—You prevaricate so Much that I don't understand what you mean. Did you destroy them about a week before you filed your Petition P—l destroyed what I had up to a week before that date.—The Assietant Official Receiver then applied for an adjournment. He said he seldom came across such men as the two debtors out ef whom it was so difficult to get the truth. He recalled Thomas Cross and asked him whether he desired to qualify the answers given by Walter Cross. or to make any addition 'to them. He replied that he did not wish to do so. If they had told an untruth they had done it ignorantly._ In answer to further questions he denied the truth of the Information contained in the letter with reference to the removal of material to Hindley. He did not thick more than £lO worth was removed. -</p>
        <p>The hearing was then adjournel until December 11th. BANKRUPTCY OF A WARRINGTON CYCLE AGENT. John Waddington, a well-known local cyclist, of 16, St. James'-ltreet. Latchford. formerly of 52, Sankey. Street, E3.rlestown, and carrying on business at 3, Mersey-street, Warrington, lately at Sankey-street' Etrlestown, and 16, St. James'-street, Latchford, as a Cycle agent, underwent his first public examination in bankruptcy. on Friday. His statement of affairs showed liabilities £349 33. 101., assets £55 154. 9d., deficiency e 293 83. 14. Want of capital was alleged by the bank- rupt to be the cau4e of his failure. He stated in answer to the Assistant Official Receiver that he had been in business since the early part of 1892, having commenced Without any capital. He had not kept pr per books of account, and was unable, therefore, to explain, in detail how his defi iiency had arisen;but that it was attributable to household and personal expenditure in excess Of income. He first became aware of his insolvency in 1891; and had contracted the whole of his outstanding liabilities (almost wholly trade liabilities) with such knowledge. The household furniture in his dwelling- house from £lO to £ls's worth belonged to his wife, as hirer thereof under a hiring agreement. He estimated that his weekly househould expenses had been about £3 P"r week. He had not kept proper books because he hadn't a knowledge of book-keeping.—The examination Was closed. A LOCAL FARMER'S FAILURE.</p>
        <p>John Frederick Cooke, of Leigh Villas, G-rappenhall, latc4y residing and carrying on business at Orford Farm, Orford, as a farmer, also underwent his first public</p>
        <p>examination in bankruptcy. His liabilities were £125 18s. 11d., and assets £l4 17s. 6d., leaving a deficiency of £lll is. sd. He alleged as the cause of his failure, bad times in the farming business, and high rental. He stated in answer to the Assistant Official Receiver that he commenced business about six years ago, at Orford Farm, Orford, and carried it on down to June last, when the whole of his effects were sold by the landlord for rent. His father had previbusly carried on the farm for 12 months, and on his marriage he transferred the farm, with all the implements and crops and £5O in cash, to him. He also tock over the liabilities. He could not say what the liabilities were or what was the value of the assets. He had no capital of his own. Bankrupt had not filed the prescrib,d deficiency account.,saying he had not kept books of account, except for a very short time and could not file a deficiency account based on a profit and loss account. He further stated that he kept an income and expenditure account for a few months at the beginning of 1895, and that be took stock and made out a statement ot his assets and liabilities on the Ist of January, 1895. The statement showed assets .£299 15s. 01. and liabilities £l9B 12 Od, He alleged he only first became aware of his insolvency in June last, and that he had not contracted any debts with such know- ledge. With the exception of £6 2s. Od., for clothing, medical attendance, the whole of the unsecured liabilities were for goods'and work done. The contingent liabili- ties in respect of two promissory notes given by the bankrupt as surety for a gentleman. Bankrupt also stated that his wife was tenant of the dwelling house, Leigh Villas, Grappenhall. The house was taken in her name because the landlord bought the furniture at the sale by auction, and gave it to her. He also gave here a horse, which he sold to file his petition. His father owned part of the furniture at Leigh Villas. It was also on the farm, but was not sold by the landlord. It was worth - ROOM £3o."—ln answer to Mr. Browne, the debtor said a number of farm implements which were not sold be- longed to his father.—Mr. Browne said if the debtor would produce his father's receipts his client would be satisfied.—The examination was acc=mdingly adjo u rned for the debtor to produce the receipts. "</p>
        <p>- - A\IC 97447/°? MP' I x.NtE\ MUSTY FLAVOR . - • Ask your Groer for free Sample.</p>
