      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-11-14_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>R.S.P.C.A. PROSECUTION AT \ WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>R.S.P.C.A. PROSECUTION AT \ WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>RECEIVING A " LOAN " FROM A DEFENDANT. THE CASE DISMISSED. On Friday morning,at the Warrington Borough Police Court, before Couneillor Pierpoint (in the chair), Alder- man Wright, Dr. Gornall, and Mr. S. Wright, Arthur Morris, coal dealer, and William Drewitt, both of 5. Mary's-street, Warrin gton,were charged, the latter with working a horse in an unfit state on the 24th October,: and the former with causing it to be worked. Mr. F. R. Roberts, solicitor defended. Inspector Bilson, of the Royal Society for the Preven- tion of Cruelty to Animals, prosecuted, and said that on the 24th October, he was in Golborne-street, when he saw Drewitt in charge of a horse drawing a lurry of coals. The anim d was lame in every limb. Witness afterwards saw defendant who admitted that the horse was lame but did not know it was so bad. It was shortly afterwards destroyed. Mr. Roberts: Was it not dark when you saw Mr. Morris at your house ? Inspector Bilson : It was 8 o'clock. Mr. Roberts: Did you take 4s. 64. from Morris? Inspector Bilson: I repudiate most strongly such an assertion. I received absolutely nothing whatever. I saw Morris again on the 28th October to see what bad become of the horse. He said he had sold it to Toole's for slaughtering. I told him I had not received in- structions from London. Mr. Roberts: Did you not say your instructions would probably be to deal with the case as you thought fit? Inspector Bilson: I swear I did not. Mr. Roberts: Did Morris see you off the premises? Mr. Bilson: Yes. He saw me to the garden gate. Mr. Roberts: Did you take any money off him on that occasion ? Inspector Bilson : Certainly not. I swear most positively I received no money from him. Mr. Roberts: Did you not receive 2s. from him? Inspector Bilson: Positively, No ! Mr: Roberts: Did you say that if the worst came to the worst Morris could say the horse was out without his knowing it ? Inspector Bilson: No, I did not. It is a fabrication entirely. Mr. Roberts: Did you write a letter saying you would come and see Morris ? Inspector Bilson: Yes. Mr. Roberts: Why did you write? Inspector Bilson: Because I wanted to see him. Mr. Roberts: Why? Inspector Bilson: Because I had received instruc- tions from London and wished to communicate those instructions to him. Mr. Roberts: Did you see him? Inspector Bilson : Yes. I had a conversation with him bearing upon the horse. Mr. Roberts: You knew the horse was killed? Inspector Bilson : Yes. Mr. Roberts: Why did you want to see him again? Inspector Bilson: I wanted to know whether he had got another horse or not. Mr. Roberts: What had it got to do with you whether be had another horse or not Inspector Bilson: I heard he was about purchasing a horse which- Mr. Roberts What had that got to do with it? Did you say" Let's get to business ?" Inspector Bilson: I have no recollection of saying such a thing. Mr. Roberts: Dil you say you could either do him a" lot of harm or a lot of good ? Inspector Bilson: I swear no. Mr. Roberts: Had the defendant any money In his hand at the time ? Inspector Bilson: I did not notice that he had. Mr. Roberts: Did be not show you fls. 6d. Inspector Bilson : No. Mr. Roberts : Did he show you money and you said it was not enough? Inspector Bilson: No. Mr. Roberts: Did you say he must give you 10s. ? Inspector Bilson: Certainly not. Mr. Roberts: Did you ask him to lend you £2? Inspector Bilson: No. Mr. Roberts: Do you swear he did not lend you £2 Inspector Bilson: Yes. Mr. Roberts: Nor any sum of money at all? Inspector Bilson : Yes, (with hesitation). He lent me a sovereign. (Sensation.) Mr. Roberts: And you as a prosecutor g 9 and borrow a sovereign from him. Did you take it? Inspector Bilson : Yes. As a loan. Mr. Roberts: Do you think it consistent with your duty as prosecuting Inspector for the Society to borrow money from the man you are causing to appear before the Court? Inspector Bilson : It was purely a private matter. Mr. Roberts: You had seen him about the case? Inspector Bilson : Yes. It W 33 necessary. Mr. Roberta: Do you think the officials in London would approve of your borrowing moriey from a man you intended to prosecute? Inspector Bilson: I have no idea what they would like. Mr. Roberts : Did you not, altogether, receive from Morris, £1 17s 6d. ? Inspector Bilson : Most positively and emphatically no. Mr. Roberts: You received money down,which you call a loan ? Inspector Bilson : It was a loan most positively. The coss•examination then ceased. and , Mr. Roberts said it was one of the most scandalous cases ever brought into the Court. It was not necessary to say much about it. They would see from the character of the evidence the Inspector had given that morning, that he Must be a very bold man indeed after what had taken place between him and Morris to have the audac- ity to bring such a case before their worships. Some days before he received the money the Inspector saw the horse and told Morris it was in a very bad state and that proceed- ings would be taken. The horse was destroyed, and the Inspector made an appointment with Morris and borrowed a sovereign, as he admitted. What could be said of such conduct as that? How could they convict ,a mats on evidence like that? His (Mr. Roberts') infor- mation, and he believed it to be correct, for Mr. Morris was, p they knew, one of the most honourable and hardworking men in Warrington, was that not only did the Inspector recei ee a pound, hut on the first occasion he took 43. 6L On the next he took 24., and on the last occasion the SUM he admitted receiving. Mr. Roberts asked if he might pot Mr. Morris in the box to confirm his statement. The Deputy Magistrates Clerk : He cannot give evi- dence for hfmseilf. Mr. Roberts said he was perfectly satisfied to leave the ease in the magistrates' hands. He asked them to say that the facts had revealed such a state of things that they must decline to convict. The inspector had instituted a reign of terror in this town and it was a well-known fact that it was a hopeless thing to defend a case against Bilson. It was that fact that led Morris to offer Bilson a certain sum of money, which, he was in- formed, Bilson took. He thought they could scarcely, convict Morris after Bilson admitting that he had re- ceived a pound. Councillor Pierpoint : It is the unanimous decision of the bench to dismiss the case, and the majority of us ar , of the opinion that Bilson has acted most improperly in I the conduct of the case.</p>
      </div>
