      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-11-14_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>Eetttrs to the</head>
        <p>Eetttrs to the</p>
        <p>WARRISGTON'S FI APPLIANCES. Sir,—Would you oblige w Is. :pace in your valuable paper for the purpose of poiziting out the advantages that would be derived from the establishing of water stations. We have recently purchased a powerful fire engine which is capable of discharging five hundred gallons per minute. At the recent fire at Jubilee Build- Inge the hydrants numbering six were supplied from an eighteen inch pipe and the supply was quite inadequate as the engine had to repeatedly stop for want of water. At a more recent fire at Mr. Daintith's Bridge-street, this engine was again brought out, and was supplied frc, e a twelve inch pipe. Knowing the deficiency of the w .I•er supply, nozzles were put on accordingly and the er_ n P therefore only discharged one half of What it would I done had there been a sufficient supply of water. '-refere what will be the case in those streets where the hydrants are only supplied by three, four and six inch pipes? By establishing the water stdtions the fire engine would go direct to the station nearest the fire, and the one-horse reel carriage capable of holding five men, driver, and two reels,each three hundred yards long would go direct to the fire, and could,in my opinion, get to work in half the time and with half the number of men, as compared with the present arrangements,which is very important in cases of conflagration. Now, it appears to me that this new engine with all its advantages cannot work in a single street in War- rington, therefore it is necessary that the said stations be established for the purpose of supplying the engine with sufficient water, which would be available in case of fire for the different large works and the more valu- able property in the centre of the town, and the smaller engine could be worked with the present hydrants, so that both engines could be brolght to bear on the same fire. Finding that the cost for men and horses (for flushing purposes) in 1894 was £91,0.nd the tank used being very small, rendered it of very little service, nearly all these proposed water stations could be made available for that purpose, as the water which they are supplied with is supplied from the reservoir at Appleton, which is con- tinually running to waste in the Manchester Ship Canal. The pipes are laid from Appleton to all the larga works, and therefore could be made use of at a very small cost. —Yours truly,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-11-14_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>WILLIAM DIGGLE.</head>
        <p>WILLIAM DIGGLE.</p>
        <p>TIIE TITLE "REVEREND."</p>
        <p>- -- Sir,—l believe the ministers of all religious denomina- tions. except the Society of Friends, prefix the word " Reverend " to their names. And the prominent way in which they do it through advertisements and other methods of publicity naturally excites one's curiosity to know the reason for their appropriation of the adjective. Considering their sacred profession we instinctively turn to Holy Writ for the desired information. Strange to say, however, the New Testament, the Christian's text book, does not contain the word at all, nor any precept or precedent for the use of any title whatever by minis- ters of Christ. On the contrary, tho example of Jesus and His disciples is dead against it. The founder of , Christianity was content to be known among men as "Jesus of Nazareth," and the Apostles imbued with the lowly spirit of their Master. hat.. Jeither prefixes nor affixes to their names. Petor,and James, and John and Matthew, sufficed for them. Besides,such Scriptures as " But be ye not called Rabbi, for one is your master, even Christ, and all ye are brethren " ; "And call no man your father upon the earth : for one is your Father , which is in heaven," may fairly be construed as prohi- bitive of any titular distinctions among Christian minis- ters. So far as the New Testament then is concerned, the users of the title " reverend" have neither precept nor precedent in their favour,but are rather condemned by its direct teaching. But turning to the Old Testament I find the word Reverend occurs once, and only once there ; but it has no reference whatever to men. In the ninth verse of • Psalm cxi. we read " He sent redemption unto His people ;He hath commanded His Covenant for ever; holy and reverend is His name." Here than we see reverend used as one of the attributes of the Almighty ! And yet creatures of sinful dust and ashes like ourselves do not hesitate to use the title as a professional appen- dage. If this be not daring presumption I know not what it is ; and I should be pleased indeed to learn from yourself or some of your able correspondents why our religious teachers require U 3 to regard them asßeverend, Right Reverend, and Most Reverend gentlemen? For a long time past have entertained the view that all ec- clesiastical titles emanate from below, and not from above, and are the incipr:nt stages of that spirit of Antichrist which will one day culminate in a human be- ing exalting himself as God !—Yours c. lONA.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-11-14_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>SATURDAY EVENING CONCERTS AT WARRINUTON.</head>
        <p>SATURDAY EVENING CONCERTS AT WARRINUTON.</p>
        <p>The elements on Saturday evening were of such a character as to keep many indoors, but notwithstanding this drawback, a large number of persons braved the rain and the muddy streets in order to attend the second of the Saturday evening concerts of this season, pro- moted by the Parr Hall, Committee of the Warrington Corporation, and held in the Parr Hall. Inside the hall, all was bright and cheerful ,although we shall perhaps be forgiven for expressing the opinion that the building is not yet free from draughts. The large attendance on Saturday was very encouraging and must have been gratifying Vs the promoters who are endeavouring to make the best use of the Hall for the good of the commu- nity. The programme was provided by artistes of re- pute, chiefly from Liverpool, and on the whole, a good evening's enjoyment was provided. The soloists were : —Miss Meta Aederson, soprano; Madame Edith Parker, centmlto ; and Mr. George Green, tenor. Mr. Wilson Blackledge provided the humour of the evening,and Mr. Robert Horst and Mr. J. E. Cave, gave instrumental se- lections. Madame Parker was decidedly the best of the trio of vocalists. She is the possessor of a fine contralto voice of good range and quality,and her vocalisation was characterised by artistic culture, clear enunciation, and admirable expression. In her first contribution "The Toilers" she exhibited a considerable depth of feeling and expression, and in her second essay, she secured a well-merited encore for her effec- tive rendering of "Home dearie Home." In re- sponse to the continued applause, alas complied with "Darby and Joan" which further pleased the audience, and it was evident that they would be pleased to renew her acquaintance on some future occasion. Miss Anderson was not so successful. In both her con- tributions "The dear little Shamrock" and " When all was young" (Gonnod), there was a palpable quivering of the voice which somewhat marred the effect of what otherwise would have been two capital songs. "Alice, where art thou?" was Mr. Green's initial effort in which be was fairly successful. Later in the evening he gave " Nina " which seemed less suited to his voice. Mr. Wilson Blackledge made his first appearance at the Perr Hall this season and it must be admitted that he rather enhanced his reputation as a humorist. His items in,the first part of the programme were of his own composi- tion, and both of them, "The Village Choir Cencert " and" Once upon a time," were well suited to his style. Mr. Blackledge has a happy way of rapidly changing his voice, and in mimicry he is perhaps unexcelled. In the second half of thc programme he recitoct Ben Brierley's well-known piece entitled" Th' adventures of a Stuffed Monkey," which kept the house in a roar cf laughter. His concluding sketch "Tommy's Birthday Party" was spiced with witticisms and sarcasm,and his contributions added greatly to the Fuccesi of the even- ing's entertainment. Amongst the instrumental items, Mr. Robert Horst's cla.rionet solos aroused the audience to a marked demonstration of approval, and for his neat and delicate execution of " Ernani " (Lazarus), he was t nailed. Mr. J. K. Cave in addition to fulfilling the duties of accompanist, performed two selections on the organ, the triumphal march from " Naaman " being especially good. The arrangements for the concert were complete in every respect, due largely to the foresight of the Town Clerk (Mr. J. Lyon Whittle), the bon. secre- tary to the Parr Hall committee. •</p>
        <p>INTERCLOB STEEPLECHASE AT WARRINGTON. An important interclub race took place on Satur- day from the Bowling Green Hotel, Warrington,be- fore a very large gathering of spectators. The con- testing clubs were.the Warrington Orford Harriers and the South Cheshire Harriers (Crewe) and it was the latter's first engagement this season. The visitors were strongly represented, and were confi- dent of winning, although they were without the services of two or three prominent runners. The hares were despatched at three o'clock, supplied with a plentiful supply of trail, and the distance covered would be some five and a half miles of country, which was veryheavy, owing, no doubt,to the thaw that set in on Friday evening. It was not until close upon four o clock when the pack,which numbered upwards of forty runners were set on their journey, and great excitement prevailed as to the result. After an absence of about 30 minutes, H. Grounds, of the Orford club, was the first man home, and was received with loud cheers from the home supporters. J. Hossack, one of the Cheshire Club, come next, closely followed by J. Pricesof the home team. On counting up the points it was found that Warrington had won a close and excit- ing contest by the narrow margin of three points, the first six men from each club being as follows —Warrington: 11. Grounds, 1; J. Price, 3; J.Riley,. 6; W. Price, 7 ; J. W. Mather,B ; and T. Barnett,l3 total, 38 points. South Cheshire : T.Hossack, 2; P. Peach, 4; P. Blackwell, 5 ; J. Simpson, 9; F. Arden, 10; and L. Towudey, 11 ; total,4l points. After the race the visitors were entertained by the home team to tea provided by Mr. Dutton (an old Crewe- ite) and after the inner man bad been attended to a concert was held and a pleasing programme was gone through. Mr. Lincoln Scott, of Warrington, was chairman, and the proceedings terminated by the singing of "Auld Lang Syne!</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-11-14_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>NOTES AND NEWS.</head>
        <p>NOTES AND NEWS.</p>
        <p>The Maharajah of Coach Behar, who has just left Sandringham, where he was for nearly a week the guest of the Prince and Princess of Wales, has (says the "St. James's Gazette") an Eastern palace in Bengal. The palace contains a skating rink, tennis courts, and a beautiful library, furnished with English books, and English papers abound. Sentries guard the doorways and the head of the staircase,while a band plays through- out dinner. In the hot weather, accompanied by the Maharanee and the children (the eldest girl is twelve years old), the Maharajah goes to Darjeerling and to Calcutta for the season.</p>
        <p>The" Matin " announces that Sidney Odanne,who has just been released from prison in Berlin, has arrived in Paris. He protests against the German method of ad- ministering justice. He was condemned for being con- cerned in intrigues calculated to bring about war be- tween France and Germany. The journal adds that owing to his disbelief in tha possibility of obtaining jus- tice in Prussia he will bring an action against the Ger- man Emperor in the Paris Courts, claiming 37,650,000 francs damages.</p>
        <p>Some health officers in the United States have been devoting time to discovering the principal direct factors of injury to health. Of special interest to women is the fact that sewing silk sometimes contains acetate of lead, which is added by unscrupulous manufacturers to give it weight, and seamstresses are known to have been poisoned by biting off the ends of the silk threads. Women may know when this danger is present, by the fact that the bitten thread gives a sweet taste. Alpaca is also said to be contaminated witk lead. Lead colours are used in artificial flowers and in wall paper.. In the manufacture of hats a strong solution of acid nitrate of mercury is used to promote the felting of the hair or fur. Arsenical poisoning occurs most frequently in the manu- facture of artificial flowers, and in dyeing, and in the manufacture of carpets and dress goods print.</p>
        <p>The lens of the telescope for the observatory at Lake Geneva, Wisconsin, which is toe largest in the world at the present time, has just been completed. It has taken two years and a half to make, and will be shipped to its destination as soon as the necessary orders have been received from the authorities of the Chicago University. The extreme diameter of the clear aperture of the lens is (says the "Chronicle") 41gin., and its focal length is 611 t. The crown is 3m, thick at the middle and thick at the outer edes, and weighs 2051 b. The whole of the lens, with its iron ring, weighs altogether about 9cwt. The cost of the glass plates, which were manu- factured in Paris, was something like £B,OOO, and the lens itself in its completed condition has resulted in an expense of £20,000. The greatest care has been exer- cised in packing it for transport in order to prevent the possibility of its being scratched or damaged in any way, for that would seriously impair its value. Before it is shipped it will be wrapped in thick cases of flannel, and will be placed in a bedding of curled horse- hair, the spring of which will diminish its liability to movement. The box in which it is contained will, in addition, be mounted on springs, and will be placed, not in an ordinary freight car, the running of which would be too rough, but in one of the best parlour cars. Furthermore, to insure against accidents by the way, four men will take turns in watching it during the journey.</p>
        <p>At Novara, in the province of Palermo, lately, a young Sicilian, Antonino Buemi the lover of a country girl named Carmela, abandoned her for, the purpose of marrying another woman. Carmela dressed herself up as a man, and accompanied by her mother, hid herself at twilight close to the road by which her ex-lover was accustomed to go home. Both she and her mother were armed with long knives. After waiting for some time they perceived, as they thought, the man they were ex- pecting. As soon as ha came up to them they rdshed out and thrust at him with their knives. His cries as he fell reached the ears of Antonino Buemi the intended victim, who just then came along the road, only to find that his brother had been killed by mistake for himself. He had a spade over his shoulder. With this he smashed in the elder woman's skull. He then turned against his former sweetheart but she had fled before him,and,arriv- ing at a neighbouring mill-pond, desperately threw herself in, and was drowned. Antonino has escaped in- to the woods, but the carabiniers are on his track.</p>
        <p>The Paris correspondent of the " Telegraph," writes :—Sensation has undoubtedly been caused by the death of Pal,karus Perko, as he was known in Paris.' Ferko was a Hungarian violinist,who came here in com- mand of a Tsigane band engaged at the last exhibition. He was a small, thin, ugly little man, but his playing was marvellous, and made him an immense favourite. Everybody wanted to hear his Czardas, and when the exhfe,tior was over he was inundated with invitations to play in restaurants and at private con- certs. While at the zenith of his popularity Perko dis- appeared, and his companions, after much difficulty, found that a wealthy young woman, an orphan, had in- duced him to live with her and to attune his violin for her ears only. She paid his wife £BOO, and prevailed upon her to go back with her two children to the land of the Magyars. The violinist, promoted to unbounded wealth, began to gamble and to drink, 'and spent £40.000 in one year. The relatives of the young woman , interfered ,caused her to be subjected to the tutelage of a Conseil Judiciaire, or supervisor of her property • but she still had sufficient money to supply the Wants of the spendthrift gipsy musician, with whom she was infatu- ated. Ferko continued to lose money on the race-courses and in clubs, drinking the while champagne and brandy, until he utterly ruined his constitution. He has now died a victim of opulence, hashed a magnificent funeral, with a full muiical requiem, and is buried beneath a mound of flowers in the cemetery of Pere La-chaise. The young person who paid for his obsequies and his tomb is inconsolable over his loss, and weeps daily be- fore his violin, which she treasures as a sacred trust to be touched no more by human bands.</p>
        <p>It appears (says a Paris correspondent) that Mar- guerite Boyenval, who has remained in a state of somno- lent catalepsy for thirteen years at the little village of Origny-Sainte-Benolte, near St. Quentin, is a sleeping beauty. Although the body is reiuced to bone and parchment, M. Charles Chincholle, of the "Figaro," in a very graphic description, states that the sleeper looks ecstatic. The first shock came on in consequence of a calumny accusing Marguerite of infanticide. The poor girl saw the gendaimes approaching the house for some other business, and swooned away. She was then nine- teen, and has grown into womanhood in her sleep.from which neither Dr. Charcot nor the whole group af spec- ialists in suggestion and magnetism have been able to awaken her.</p>
        <p>There is no practice more common with a woman who uses a needle than to stick the needle in the front of her dreer on putting down her work. In some classes it is quite a common thing to turn a bodice into a pincushion. Perhaps the sad death recorded this week mav d 3 some- thing to -correct this dangerous practice. Here was a woman, going to Sing's-cross with her children, seen to stagger and fall unconscious. At the hospital it was found a needle had pierced her heart, and she died from internal bleeding. It is a lesson women will do wt.ll to remember, although the danger of glass paraffin lamps has been pointed by many hundred fatalities, and yet people go on using them utterl7 regardless of the danger.</p>
        <p>We notice says the " Daily Chronicle" that Mrs. Castle has been released, and that she is to leave at once with her husband for the States. We do not quarrel with the decision. Mrs. Castle did not require the things she stole, and to steal for the sake of stealing, without any plausible motive, is a sign of a morbid mental state rather than of grave moral delinquency. But we wish our, ltw were a little more even-handed in its method of dealing out punishments. Kleptomania is not confined to rich folk, or to women. Our prison rec'ords are full of cases of people whose offences are clearly due to mere aberration, but who have had no *suah consideration from her Majesty's judges or from the Home Secretary as Mrs. Castle has received. We have before us the story of a poor man, who, at fifty, began to steal looking-glasses. Ile stole nothing but looking-zlasses, and he stole them continuously. Ho spent several years in prison for .separate offences of this character, and easily died in prison at the age of sixty- five. Of course this man's case was not considered as Mrs. Castle's was considered—i.e., in relation to the condition of the prisoner's mind as well as with refer- ence to the actual offence.</p>
        <p>TEB ORDER OF DEITII2B.-WARRINGTON DISTRICT</p>
        <p>A special meeting 9f the above district was held on Monday evening .in the Druids' Hall to consider very important business, under the presidency of D.G.M. James Massey. In opening, the Grand Master referred to the loss D.T., George Jolley, had sustained in the sudden death of his wife,when it was resolved that a letter of condolence be sent to him. He also very feelingly referred to the sud- den death of the late D.C.S., T. Roberts, and re- marked that for nearly 20 years he had fulfilled many important offices for the advancement of his lodge, and for twelve years had occupied the posi- tion of District Secretary. He had only recently relinquished that office. It was resolved that a letter of condolence be sent to his widow and family and that out of respect to his memory the meeting be adjourned until after his interment. The officers and trustees decided to represent the district at his funeral, which took place on Tuesday in Grappen- hall Church yard.</p>
        <p>Dry bread is much easier of digestion than fresh. It is estimated by physiologists that over 10 per cent. of dry bread undergoes sliivlry digestion while being masti- cated, while of fresh bread, less than 2 per cent. is thus changed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-11-14_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>EXAMINER- SATURDAY, NOVEMBER 14 1996</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER- SATURDAY, NOVEMBER 14 1996</p>
        <p>would not involve the ratepayers in any expense what- ever.and although they intended it to be an experiment it was one that would cost the ratepayers nothing, and de no harm if it failed, whilst if it succeeded it would materially benefit a number of the most unfortunate inmates in their institution,and with whom,he thought, they should deeply sympathise. If the scheme were adopted under the few simple rules approved of by the House Committee there would not be the slightest dan- ger either to personal property or discipline,and in view of that fact, instead of putting any hindrances in the way, the scheme ought to have a fair trial. The ladies who were willing to do this unpleasant work deserved every praise for their zeal and self-sacrifice and the Guardians ought to help them as far as they could. If the scheme succeeded it would do a little good to per- haps the most unfortunate and helpless inmates whose cases were the most pitiable. Mr. Know Las said he wished to speak against the pro- posal. He felt that it would boa mistake to bring about the schools or workhouse too many irresponsible or hon- orary officials. With regard to the employment of the inmates of the workhouse they had to leave that matter largely in the hands of the Master and Matron, and if there was too much interference in the management of the house he thought it would lead to the work being done in an" eye-satisfying style." Too much super- vision was a very bad thing. He did not think the scheme could be carried out without hindering their officials in the discharge of their duties and their life would not he worth that of a dog. He (Mr. Knowles) went to some trouble in seeing what sort of people they intended to employ by this scheme,and he did not think there were any that could he engaged in this work. Wtth regard to able-bodied men it was not likely that they wanted to learn how to make fancy baskets,and he was unable to find any persons to whom that system would be applicable. Those who were able to do any- thing were doing something. It would be a great mis- take to have too much officialism, and the carrying out of the scheme must interfere with the work. of paid officials,who would be hampered by being required to at- tend to little requests. Mr. BILLINGS said he was rather surprised at the temerity of the Chairman of the House Committee in introducing the subject. Its introduc3on was liable to give rise to the opinion that there was a houseful of people at the Workhouse out of employment, and it should have occurred to the Chairman of the House Com- mittee that if there were any idle hands at the house they should have been employed for the good of the ratepayers. He (Mr. Billinge) had paid both surprise and official visits to the house and he was not aware of any unemployed inmates who could be engaged in the work proposed. He was not in favour of the scheme, and the Ladies Committee, instead at being a committee of good, mightffiecome a committee of busy bodies, and to avoid that and any unpleasant feeling he hoped the Board would defer the scheme to some indefinite period. Mr. MALKIN said he was rather surprised at the fear of some members in introducing a new feature into the house. He had gene through the house and some of the inmates had told him that time hung heavily on their hands. He thought it would be a vere geed and useful thing to introduce this system into the house. The inmates had been used to work in the busy lives of itidustry, and they now found the hours thee/ Passed away doing nothing very wearisome. When the Brabazon scheme had been introduced it was found that the inmates became brighter and more cheerful. Another point in its favour was that it could be introduced with- out any cost to the ratepayers. He thought the least they could do would be to give the scheme a trial. Mr. Hickman said when the scheme was first brought before the Board he had not had an opportunity of look- ing into the matter, but since it had been adjourned he had read the ieport of the Brabazon Society, and he was now prepared to support the scheme. He wished they could also introduce the classification question which would benefit paupers all round. Mr. WILLIAM :FIOUGHTON thought that by the scheme the Board would create a new department which would be an encumbrance, and he was afraid there would be very little result to justify it. Be would vote against the scheme. Mr. A. J. JOLLEY, :in reply, pointed out that any meddling in the proper work of the institution was care- fully guarded against by the few rules which had been drawn 'up. He thought that disposed of the point which Mr. Knowles laboured. With respect to.the point of the able-bodied people being employed by the scheme, it never was intended to apply it to able-bodied people. Since ho (\dr. Jolley) had been a member of that com- mittee he had seen to it that every able-bodied person was found work to do. But those who visited the hos- pital regularly and also the house knew that there was a certain proportion of the inmates who could not be called able-bodied. They were not imbeciles, but sink- ing down to that ievel, and the scheme was to brighten the lives of these people. There was a possibility of do .ing a considerable amount of good. A vote was then taken as follows :—For tha scheme : Mrs. Clay, Messrs. J. Eioughton E. Dignum, Hickman, Malkin, Lang, Bent, A.J. Jolley, and the Chairman (9) ; against: Messrs. Pilling, Moore, W. Houghton, Ball, Halton, Snowier., Billinge, Johnson, A. Jolley, and War- burton (10). The scheme was therefore rejected by one vote.—The Clerk said in view of the scheme being de- _</p>
        <p>_ cided upon he wrote to the Local Government Bard asking what the ladies were empowered to do in the house, and if the scheme had been decided upon the ladies' serviees would have been confined to the female side of the house and the children. THE NEW INFIRMARY.</p>
        <p>The preparatory plans by Mr. Owen were suspended in the Board room for the inspection of the Guardians. The Infirmary Committee's minutes stated that Mr. William Owen, the arehitect,produced a plan of the land about to be purchased for the erection of the new infirm- ary, which had been surveyed and upon which the present workhouse buildings had been shown. He explained the position on the plan in which he proposed to place the new infirmary buildings giving as his reason 4 for the position, convenient acesTss to the back of the infirmary buildings for the carriage of food and con- venient access to the front of the buildings for approach of the central administrative block, and the escaping of the line of sewer which crosses the land. He also pro- duced the preparatory working plans of the infirmary, showing the accommodation to be provided as 190 beds (90 male and 100 female), and also showing where a future kitchen arrangement could be erected. After consideration and discussion it was resolved, " Toot the preparatory plans of the intended new Workhouse Infirm- ary and Buildings having been produced and explained to the committee, by Mr. Owen, as architect,the Medical Officer and Workhouse Master being also present, the same be approved, and that after approval by the Board the preparatory plans be forwarded to Mr. Gordon Smith, architect of the Local Government Board, and that the Clerk be instructed to attend,after appointment with Mr. Gordon Smith and Mr. Owen at Whitehall."— Mr. WILLIAM HOINMITON4moved that the minutes of the Infirmary Committee be approved and adopted subject to any modification thought desirable after an interview with Mr. Gordon Smith, and that the committee be empowered, after interview by the Architect and Clerk with Mr. Gordon Smith, to proceed with the further steps necessary for obtaining approval of the full work- ing drawings and of the specification, Sze., by the Local Government Board,and for obtaining their order empow- ering the Guardians to borrow money, and, subject to previous confirmation thereof by the Guardians, for the terms of loan."—Mr. A. J. JOLLEY seconded, Mr. HICK- MAN supported. and it was unanimously agreed to. The Board then rose.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-11-14_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>WARRINGTON BOARD OF</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOARD OF</p>
        <p>GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>Ihe monthly meeting of this Board was held in the Board Room on Wednesday morning. Mr. S. Davies presided, and there were also present :—Mrs. Clay, Rev. Father Moore, Messrs. G. Pilling, J. W. Knowles, W. Houghton, A. J. Jolley, W. Hickman, T. B. Malkin, A. Lang, T. Billinge, T. Flalton, J. Ball, J. Dignum, J. Houghton, A. Jolley, J. Warturton. J. Johnson, E. L. Bent, with Messrs. J. C. Sutton (clerk),S. Sutton (assist- ant clerk), C. W. Ward (master), A. Wright (superinten- dent Padgate schools), and W. Owen (architect). DR. WATKINS' EXTRA REMUNERATION.</p>
        <p>The Local Government Board wrote approving of the payment of LlOlO3. as extra remuneration to Dr. Watkins for extra medical services, as recommended by the Newton committee.</p>
        <p>COUNTY COUNCIL GRANT TO POOR LAW SCHOOLS. The CLERK stated that he had received a cheque for £241 123. 9d. from the County Treasurer towards the payment of teachers in Poor Law schools.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-11-14_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>CHRISTMAS DINNER FOR TUB INMATES.</head>
        <p>CHRISTMAS DINNER FOR TUB INMATES.</p>
        <p>The House Committee recommended that the usual Christmas dinner with beer be provided for the inmates, and than the Master be authorised to purchase th usual quantity of fruit, for the puddings, etc., at once.—The recommendation was adopted on the motion of Mr. A. J. Jolley, seconded by Mr. tk.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-11-14_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>SCHOOLS COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>SCHOOLS COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>The sub-committee appointed to consider the estim- ates for laundry work reported that Messrs. Bradfords was deemed most satisfactory. Some alterations had been suggested and a revised tender was now submitted, the amount being £212. The Schools Committee recom- mended that the tender be accepted s .bject to Messrs. Bradford also undertaking the necessary brick and mas- onry work without extra charge. The committee also recommended that Mr. E. T. Evans' tender for blankets at 79. 10d. per pair be accepted ; also that the usual Christmas dinner be given to the children and officials, and that £5 be allowed for prizes and toys for the chit- dren.—The CHAIRMAN in moving the confirmation of the minutes, referred to the recommendation with regard to the laundry, and said they coqld not do without the alterations. He felt quite sure the work would repay itself in the saving of labour, fuel, and other things.— Mr. Pilling said the cc mmittee was very careful in going through the contracts. Messrs. Bradford's tender was reduced from £250 to £212, by alterations made by the sub-committee. There was no doubt that the Board would get the benefit of the amount they were now being asked for in the saving of labour. The work would also be better done.—Mr. William Houghton supported, and the minutes were confirmed.</p>
        <p>THE ALTERATIONS IN TEL REGISTRATION DISTRICTS.</p>
        <p>A letter was road from the Local Government Board sanctioning the a!terations with regard to the Warring- ton sub-districts for registration purposes ; and the</p>
        <p>Board suggested that the Guardians should pass a reso- lution altering the registration districts accordingly.— The Clerk said it was a matter in which the Board had no choice now.—The Chairmaß proposed that the regis- tration districts be as suggested by the RegiLtrar.—Mr. Pilling seconded, and it was agreed to. THE REPRESENTATION OF LITTLE BANHEY.-MR. THOMPSON</p>
        <p>RETAINS lIIS SEAT.</p>
        <p>The CLERK stated that in the Warrington Extension Act it was provided that after November 9th,Mr.Thomp- son should cease to hold office as Guardian for Little Sankey. and that the Guardian for St. Austin's Ward should represent the whole of the extended ward conse- quent on the extension of the Borough. After some cor- respodence with Mr. Hutton, the Clerk to the Lancashire County Council, he received an order which Mr. Hutton proposed to submit to the Parliamentary Committee of tbe County Council. The Order provided for the division of St. Austin's Ward, which was to be represented on the Board of Guardians by two members, one to repre- sent what was formerly the Little Sankey portion. At his (Mr. Sutton's) suggestion the order was made to re- serve the rights of Mr. Thompson as the member until the usual election. He (Mr. Sutton) had _not. yet re-</p>
        <p>C.:i-ved the Order which had been confirmed by the Coun- cil, but he hoped to do so shortly, and they might con- clude that Mr. Thompson was now a Guardian. Mr. Thompson was aware of the Order, but he thotr_tht it better not to attend until the Order had been received. —The: Chairman :said they would all be pleased to see Mr. Thompson back again.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-11-14_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>EXTENSION OF THE 'UNION.</head>
        <p>THE EXTENSION OF THE 'UNION.</p>
        <p>The CLERlCreported that on and after Monday, Nov- ember 9th, 1896, the porton of Runcorn Union to be added to the Warrington Union do form part of and be included with the N. 2 medical relief district and No. 3 outdoor relief distriß, and that the Clerk be empowered to act for the Board in all matters requiring financial ad- justment and as authorised by previous instructions of the Board.—Mr. A. J. JOLLEY : Will it affect the salaries at all of the medical or relieving officers.—The CLERK : Oh, no,—The CLERK also reported that he had received a notice from the County authorities stating that the ad- dition to the Union would increase the assessments by £4724.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-11-14_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>SUPERANNUATION ACT.</head>
        <p>THE SUPERANNUATION ACT.</p>
        <p>After some discussion it was decided that Messrs. Davies, Pilling, A. J. Jolley, A. Jolley, Biltinge, and Mrs. Clay form a committee to consider the provisions of the Poor Law Officers' Superannuation Act. 1896, and to re- commend to the Board the value to be put upon the emoluments of the Board's indoor officers and generally thereon.</p>
        <p>THE BRABAZON EMPLOYMENT SCEIEME.-RE- JECTED BY THE BOARD.</p>
        <p>The CLERK reported that in obedience to instruction 40 Clerks of Unions reported to have adopted the Bra- bazon Employment Scheme were asked to report upon its success, and hefted received 33 replies. Some thought it was a mistake that the scheme was introducel,others that they had after taking it UD dropped it because it could not be worked out, some few were doubtful of its success, but the bulk of the replies spoke in high terms of the success attending the scheme. The CHAIRMAN said perhaps it might be out of place for him to make a few remarks at that point, but he would like to say that he hoped every member had come to discuss the matter with an unprejudiced mind. He wished to set at rest the growing feeling both on,that Board and eutside that whatever subject the Ladies' Committee introduced there were certain members who always took opportunity to oppose it. He did not think there ought to be such a spirit as that. The Board ap- pointed the Ladies' Committee to do certain work— whether they had exceeded that work he was not pre- pared to say—and whatever subject the Ladies' Com- mittee recommended for their consideration they did so , honestly, with the best motives, and a desire to increase the comfort of the pemple in their workhouse. Therefore he sincerely trusted that in discussing the subject before them they would avoid personalities as much as possible. Mr. A. J. JOLLEY said he should bear in mind the little lecture which the chairman had made. (Laughter.) Before proceeding to speak in support of the minutes with reference to the Brabazon scheme.he would like to read a letter he had received because it referred to the scheme in one Union from which the Clerk had not re- ceived a reply. The letter was as follows :—" The Ladies' Visiting Committee have noticed with !egret that there is a feeling amongst the Guardians against the in- troduction of the Brabazon Employment Society into the Warrington Workhouse, and they sincerely hope that further information as to the working of the scheme in other Unions may remove the objections and decide the Guardians in its favour. One of the members of the Ladies' Visiting Committee has recently been visiting the Chester Workhouse—where the Brabazon employment scheme was adopted three years ago—and obtained the following opinion about it from the matron there. It lis the greatest boon to all who have worked under it. There has never been the slightest friction either between the ladies and the metro"( or between the ladies and the inmates. It has dode especial good amongst imbeciles,making them very much happier and quieter : At Chester, eight ladies are engaged in teach- ing and there are about .twelve pupils—both men and women. While sitting with their pupils the ladies often read aloud. There have been three sales of work, and out of the proceeds of the last one a tea was given to the whole of the Infirmary and the expenses of maintaining consumptive girl at Rhyl during 1... he winter were paid. In the event of the scheme obtaining the approval of the Board the Ladies Visiting Committee. beg to point out that unless they may obtain the services of some extra ladies, the work will be very much hampered and crippled and its ultimate success rendered very doubtful There are probably only four or five of the present com- mittee who have the time to undertake regular weekly teaching, and at first at all events, each puoil would re- quire a teacher to him or herself. These extra helpers will of course confine themselves entirely to the Bra- bazon work,and will have nothing to do with the ordin- ary work of the Ladies Visiting Committee. With regard to the organisation of the Brabazon Employment Society, if it is to be methodically managed there most be a com- mittee and secretary—but this committee will only deal with such matters as wools, knitting needles, &amp;c., and will not come into contact with the Guardians in any way. The work will be extremely arduous and in the case of the imbeciles, probably often unpleasant, but the ladies are willing to undertake it for the benefit and welfare of the inmates. They trust therefore that the Guardians will permit them to carry out their charitable intentions and will furthermore not detract from the usefulness of the work by unnecessary and hampering restrictions.—Yours faithfully, J. H. Edeleten, hoe. sec- retary, Ladies Visiting Committee." Proceeding, Mr. Jolley said the minutes which were deferred were now before them for consideratiod and he moved their con- firmation. He did net think it was necessary to sty any- thing in favour of the Brabazon scheme for it w4s ad- mitted that in large unions it was eminently successful. But doubt was expressed at the last meeting as to its success in similar unions to Warring. ton. It was decided to write to forty unions about the size of theirs, and he (Mr. Jolley) was well within toe mark in saying that the proposition in favour ofithe scheme was four to one. That established a strong presumption of the practibility of the scheme. He was aware that local circumstances differed and that there might be circumstances which he might :nodify their opinions,but that large proportion had mentioned established a presumption in favour of the scheme. It was important to remember that the scheme</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-11-14_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</p>
        <p>DRUNKENNESS.</p>
        <p>At the Barlestown police station on Monday morning, before Mr. C. B. F. Borron' Mary Ann McCarthy, a tramp, was charged with being drunk in Earlestown on Monday night, and in default of paying a fine of 2s, 6d. and costs, was sent to gaol for seven days.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-11-14_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>VULCAN WESLEYAN FOREIGN MISSIONS.</head>
        <p>VULCAN WESLEYAN FOREIGN MISSIONS.</p>
        <p>At the Vulcan Wesleyan Chapel on Sunday. afternoon and evening special services were held, conducted by Mr. F. Sinclair, who also delivered a couple of sermons. On Monday evening in the schoolroom there was a public meeting for the same object, when there was a fair audience. Mr. H. H. Constantine presided, and an address on mis- sionary work was given by the Rev: J. H. Doddrell. Collections in aid of the funds were made.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-11-14_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>DARING. BURGLARY AT NEWTON.</head>
        <p>DARING. BURGLARY AT NEWTON.</p>
        <p>Some time between half-past eleven on Monday night, and seven o'clock on Tuesday morning, .a burglary of a daring character was perpetrated at the residence of Mr. Samuel Baker, 140 Mercer- street, Newton.—An entrance was effected by the back kitchen window which had been left un- fastened, and before taking their departure, the thief or theives had removed a very lalge number of articles including a ladies silver watch, gold ring, brooch, spoons, suit of clothes and other wearing apparel, blankets, sheets &amp;c., sufficient to load a hand cart. How the things have been got away without discovery is a mystery.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-11-14_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>GOOD TEMPLAR ENTERTAINMENT.</head>
        <p>GOOD TEMPLAR ENTERTAINMENT.</p>
        <p>On Saturday evening a public entertainment was given under the auspices of the Dope of Earles- town lodge of Good Temptars, in the Co-operative Ilan. Bro. Q.M. Roberts P.C.C.T.of Warrington pre- sided and an interesting address was given by Bro.R. D.Patterson, D.Traas.of Birkenhead.The programme consisted of cluets,songs and instrumental duets and was sustained by Mrs. Latimer, Misses Williamson, A. Clarke, Smith, Conway, Messrs. W. Limond, W. Monks, and A. Barton. The programme was ar- ranged by Mrs. Latimer. A.R.C.O. organist of St. John's Church, Earlestown,who also accompanied on the piano.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-11-14_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>DISHONEST SERTANT GIRL.</head>
        <p>A DISHONEST SERTANT GIRL.</p>
        <p>On Friday, Elizabeth Carlin, of Robin's Row, a girl 14 years of age, employed as an occasional ser- vant at Mr. Haynes', boot dealer. Market-st., Rarles- town, was arrested on a charge of stealing a pair of boots the property of her employer. Shortly after noon on Friday the girl offered the boots in pledge at the pawnshop of Mr. Jones, Market-st.. and on being asked where she had obtained them, replied that she had bought them at Mr. Birkhead's. When asked to produce the receipt she was unable to do so, and she was then handed over to the custody of P.C. Burns. She was brought up before Colonel Wood at Newton on Saturday, and remanded on bail for a week.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-11-14_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>GOOD TEMPLADY.</head>
        <p>GOOD TEMPLADY.</p>
        <p>The weekly session of the Hope of Earlestown Lodge of Good Templars was held in the Co-opera- tive Hall on Monday evening. Bro. W. Wilson,C.T., presided over a good attendance of members. One candidate was admitted to membership. The digest of business for No. 1 Convention to be held at Earlestown was read by the Secretary, and repre- sentatives were duly instructed. Bro. G. Clarke, P.C.T., read the circular from Bro. Malins, G.C.T., which contained a number of useful items. The programme for the ensuing quarter was read by Bro. Nicholson (secretary) and adopted. The Secre- tary was instructed to send a letter of thanks to Mrs.! Latimer for preparing programme for en- tertainment on Saturday night, and to Bro. Q.-M.- Sergeant Roberts (chairman) and R. D. Peterson (speaker). The programme of the evening was entertainment by new officers. The C.T. was res- ponsible, and after a few opening remarks a pro- gramme of songs, readings, &amp;c, was given by Bro. G. Clarke, E. Newman, R. W. Nicholson, Sister A. Newman, and W. Willis.</p>
        <p>'l. DEATH AND FUNERAL OF MRS. BARTLEY.I:7O On Friday morning last Mrs. Bartley, wife of the Rev. J. Bartley, resident pastor of the Brunswick Wesleyan Chapel, Earlestown, died at the residence of her husband, Cross Lane, at the age of 37 years, after a long illness. Tne funeral took place on Tuesday afternoon, amid general manifestations of regret and sympathy, the burial service being read in the Wesleyan chapel by the Rev. W. Arrowsmith with whom were several circuit ministers. The pulpit in the chapel was draped with black and white ribbon. There was a large gathering in the chapel, amongst those present, besides the relatives being Revs. H. S. Sandiford, and J. 11. Doddrell, and Dickens of Golborne. Messrs. T. T. Cooke, Jones, J. W. Emmett, J. Gooding, P. Parr, T. Clare, W. Caw- ley, J. Burgess, E. Alderson, G. ITowarth, J. Mit- chell, &amp;c. The bearers were Messrs. J. Gooding, Hipwood, profit, and J. R. Barr. Led by a full choir, with Miss Greenall at the organ, the cozgre- gation sang "What are these arrayed in white?" and "Christian dost thou know them." The Rev. W. Arrowsmith read a portion of Scripture from I Corlsth,and in the course of a short address dwelt upon the many virtues possessed by the deceased lady, and the good works she had accomplished. The body was afterwards interred at the Newton- in-Makerfield Cemetery, all those mention2d above accompanying it to the grave side. There was a large number of splendid wreaths sent by the vari- ous classes and societies connected with the chapel, and by many friends.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-11-14_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>SCALDING FATALITIES AT</head>
        <p>THE SCALDING FATALITIES AT</p>
        <p>RUN CO RN.</p>
        <p>INQUESTS.</p>
        <p>On Saturday morning at Runcorn Police Court, Mr. Worsley held inquests on the body of Raymond Frkins, aged one year and eight months, son of -Joseph Firkins, waterman, 12, Ellesmere-street,and Margaret Ann Dodd, aged three years and seven months, daughter of John Dodd, engine driver, I, Byron-street. In the first case Mrs. Firkins stated that at noon on the previous Monday she had prepared a jug of cocea and placed it in the centre of the table. She went into the shop to serve a customer, ond whilst there heard a E cream. She ran into the kitchen and found her son with the jug in his hands. He was left sitting at the table and dragged the cloth, thus pulling the jug to him, the contents of which ran over his head, face and neck, which were severely scalded. She took him to Dr. Robinson, whose assistant attended. The child since had been under the care of Dr. Delaney. Death took place at two o'clock on Thursday. Dr. Delaney deposed to having, at the request of Dr. Robinson, attended the child, from Tuesday morning op to its death. Everything possible was done for the little one. He attributed death to pneumonia, following shock to the system through scalds. In the second case John Dodd stated that on October 18th he was pouring tea out of the pot when deceased ran against him, with the result that the contents of the teapot ran over her face. Oil was speedily applied and Dr. Robinson was summoned, and that gentleman had attended her up to her death on Thursday. She was severely scalded on the face and chest. Witness was also scalded badly. on the foot by the tea being upset. He had been off work a fortnight. Dr. Robinson explained that he was called in soon after the occurrence,and had been almost daily in atten- dance. The little girl also received great attention from Mrs. Deakin, who for years was matron of the Widnes Accidental Hospital. Everything possible had been done to save the child's life. The cause of death was the re- sult of shock, consequent on the scalds. The Coroner expressed his and the jury's appreciation of the kindness of Mrs. Deakin in voluntarily giving her services. They were very sorry for the parents in the sad loss they had sustained. A verdict in eaeh case in accordance with the medical testimony was returned.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-11-14_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATH..</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH..</p>
        <p>HILL CLIFFE AND STOCKTON HEATH UNITED BAND OF HOPE.—The first meeting of the season was held on Monday night in Hill Cliffe schoolroom and was very well attended, the schoolroom being full. The Presi- dent, the Rev. J. S. Hughes, was in the chair and gave an address on " Bravery, obedience and helpfulness "as the motto for the Band of Hope, and an excellent pro- gramme was submit:ed and efficiently rendered, the fol- lowing taking part :—Joseph Whitfield, Bessie Fuller, Ellen Holland. Jane Cook, Herbert V. Hughes, Samuel Brereton, Lizzie Fleetwood, Amy Corbett, Fred Beqwiek, Bradfnrd Kenworthy, Mary Fuller, Arthur Dunn, Sarah Ellen Whitfield, George Moss, Horatio Cook, Polly Brere- ton. An excellent address was also given by Councillor Luke Ellison, of Latchford. A large number of persons joined the Society at the close, and this premises well for the success of the meetings during the winter, as the Society is now in a better state than it has ever been in before.</p>
        <p>Mrs PONSONDY'S HAIR RESTORax, a great discovery, a Most Refreshing Wash, Nlarvellous Results, Innumetable Testimoniali, Patroni.ed by Royalty. In great demand by Professionals, makes the Hair Grow, prewmits -Bald- ness, Invigorating, restores hair to its naturalcolour, gives healthy and youthful appearanzte. I!dostastonish- ing results ; 2s 61 Bottle, post free.—A, ddt tss'Alts. PON- BONDY. 25, Lime-st.. Liverpool—Advt.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>On Wednesday afternoon a meeting of the above authority was held in the Bewssy-street Chambers, Mr. W. Houghton presiding. There were also present Messrs. J. W. Knowles, J. Houghton, J. Ball, T. Halton, J. Dignum, T. Matner, J. Warburton, J. Johnson, and S. Sutton (clerk), W. Owen (surveyor), Dr. Sefton (medic officer), and F. Kerfoot (nuisance inspector).</p>
        <p>FINANCE.</p>
        <p>The CLERK reported that the balance in the hands of the treasurer on the general account was £3Ol 193. 4d. and on the Burtonwood sewerage account £1,190 53. No payments had been made to the treasurer during the past month.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-11-14_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>OUR LOCAL CFITJRCHES.</head>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CFITJRCHES.</p>
        <p>CFIURCH OF THE SACRED HEART.</p>
        <p>On Sunday evening tbe fortnight's mission was hrought to a close with the Papal blessing given by the RPV. Father vabian, C.F., wild' has conducted the mis- sion. After the blessing,the choir sang the Te Deum as a thanksgiving for graces received during the mission' There was a large congregation, and Father Ryan gave the solemn Benediction. At the close of the service the choir,assisted by the congregation, sang "Faith of our Fathers." Miss M. Bellian presided at the organ.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-11-14_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>CAIRO-STREET CHAPEL.</head>
        <p>CAIRO-STREET CHAPEL.</p>
        <p>Rev. W. H. Drummond delivered' on Sunday the second of his series ,of discourses on the early stories of the Bible, taking ashis subject the creation of man, last Sunday even- ing. His remarks were based upon Genesis i. 27: "God created man in His own im.ge, in the image of God created He him." The preacher accepted the evolution theory as modifying our early teachings as to the crea- tion of man, but argued that behind evolution God was, and that although the physical powers and construction of Lan had been evolved from a very low form of life, yet the sniritnal part of man was of divine nature.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-11-14_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>WYCLIFFE P.S.A. SOCIETY.</head>
        <p>WYCLIFFE P.S.A. SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>The meeting of this society on Sunday was well attended. The chair was occupied by the Rev. John Yonge, the president of the eociety, who briefly intro- duced Mr. Cox, of Manchester, the speaker for the after- noon. Mr. Cox based his remarks on the text " Our Father who art in Heaven," Matthew vi., 9V. ft, said we recognised God as our Father, and in doing so we were bound to recognise every maa as our brother, and every woman as our 'deter. The Lord's prayer recog. nised the great principle of a universal brotherhood. The recognition of that great principle would do more than anything else to settle trades disputes. Were that principle agted upon we should not have tyrannical employers or capitalists trampling on the rights of their workmen cbtio helped to make that capital and on the other hand there would not be men who scamped their work. Troceeding the speaker applied the same princi- ple to the victims of intemperance, and said Christian people should recognise that these fallen ones were their brothers and sisters.and that it was their duty to remove the stumbling block which had (mused others to fall.—A recitation was admirably given by Miss Furness. The service was also taken part in by Mr. Joseph Hands and Mr. B. Farrar.</p>
        <p>General Hr R. Wilbraham, K.C.8., of Rode Hall, a large landowner in Cheshire, has, in consideration of the wet weather and small crops, remitted 12/1 per cent in the half-year's rent off his agricultural tenants.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-11-14_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>PENKETS WATER SUPPLY.</head>
        <p>PENKETS WATER SUPPLY.</p>
        <p>The CLERK read a letter from the Town Clerk of War- rington dated 30th October, 1896, which stated that he returned the draft agreement with a suggested clause to meet the case of Great Sankey if they should hereafter require the Corporation to provide that township with water. The difficulty they had had with the L. and N.W. Railway Company in obtaining their sanction to the construction of a culvert for conveying the mains under the Sankey Canal, was removed, and the work would be proceeded with with the utmost despatch.—The Clerk said the suggested clause was that " If at any time hereafter the Ruial District Council require the Corporation to supply any person in the township of Great Sankey with water, the Council shall pay to the Corporation such a sum as when added to the rates and charges paid by persons supplied with water within the said township (not including the amount now paid by any person supplied with water by the Corporation) as shall be equal to a sum calcu- lated at £8 per cent, upon the actual cost of providing, constructing and laying the necessary mains or pipes for supplying the township with water. And an equitable adjustment shall be made as between the two town- ships of Penketh and Great Sankey, of the sums paid and to te paid by the Rural District Council to the Cor- poration under this agreement in respect of all mains or pipes used in common for supplying the townships with water."</p>
        <p>The CLERK also read the following letter dated Novem- ber 9th, from M. Jonas Foster, clerk to the Penketh Parish Council :—" Dear deputation of this Council has discussed the draft agreement with the Town Clerk: of Warrington, and the Council has also considered the amended clause, and now desires to re- commend the same to the Rural District Council for completion. Mr. KNOWLES asked where the right of the Penketh Parish Council came in, to arrange terms with the War- rington Corporation without consulting the Rural Dis- trict Council. They made this bargain and then asked the Rural District Council to confirm it. Was it not for them, the District Council, to make arrangements? The CLERK explained that all that was done prier to the matter corning before the Council was simply by cor- respondence,and no one was bound on either side. When the Parish Council had got it into a definite shape they forwarded it to the District Council for consideration. Mr. KNOWLES :If Penketh Parish Council had done their work direct through us instead of this way we should probably have made a better bargain than we have done now.</p>
        <p>The CLERK : I don't think so. Mr. Owen and myself waited upon the Town Clerk and got better terms than Penketh Parish Coancil could themselves. Mr. KNOWLES : If these Parish Councils do not have some check put upon them they may get too forward. Mr. DIGNUM proposed that the terms of the agreement supplied by the Corporation for the provision of a water supply for Penketh be accepted by this Council. Mr. JOHN' HOUGHTON seconded, and the motion was carried.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-11-14_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>GREAT SANKEY.</head>
        <p>GREAT SANKEY.</p>
        <p>The CLERK then reported a long letter from Messrs. I Jeans and Son, dated 28th October which stated that they were instructed on behalf of certain owners and oc- cupiers of houses, etc.' in Great Sankey to request the Rural District Council to provide a supply of water for the use of ths. owners and occupiers requiring it. The letter pointed out that it was incumbent upon the Dis- trict Council to provide a supply of water and the modes of compulsion were available in default. Messrs. Jeans and Son also understood that the vicar and several ether persons, and probably the Railway Company also,would take a supply at once, and as the Corporation mains were now at Penkekh-lane end, they could be continued along further into Sankey at comparatively little ex- pense. They asked that the matter might be brought forward and requested information as to the Council's decision.</p>
        <p>Mr.KERFOOT said the water supply in Sankey was very good. Mr. Jorm HOUGUTON : Have they had a meeting of ratepayers there ? Mr. KERFOOT : Not to my knowledge. The CLERIC suggested that the matter be referred to the Parish Council of Sankey, and that if a meeting of ratepayers was advisable, to call one upon the subject. Mr. HALTON said if the matter was left over tor a month he would test the feeling in Sankey. Mr. KNOWLES moved that the matter be adjourned for a month and that it be referred to the Parish Council. Mr. VVARBURTON seconded, and it was carried. MEDICAL OFFICER'S REPORT.</p>
        <p>Dr. SEPIITON, in his monthly report, stated that the number of births was 25 and deaths 10. The death-rate was equal to 105 per 1,000 against 11 for each of the last three months and 16 for the corresponding month of last year. There was one death from whooping-cough at Burtonwood. Of scarlet fever there were eight cases, enteric fever one case The drains of Railway View had been examined and nothing of an insanitary nature found.</p>
        <p>The report was adopted. • BURTONIVOOD, SANKEY, AND PADGATE DRAINAGE. Mr. WILLIAM OWEN (surveyer) re ported that the Bur- tonwood drainage work was now completed, with the exception of the planting of the willows, which would be done during the month. He had read a letter from the Clerk of the Burtonwood District Council which stated that at a meeting of the Parish Council held on Thursday, 23rd October, it was resolved that the Coun- cil take over the filter beds and that no one be allowed to tip any refuse on the land belonging to the filter beds etc.—Mr. Owen also reported that Mr. Bennie had now completed his contracts and he(Mr. Owen)had inspected the work which was satisfactory. With regard to Pad- gate drainage he had not yet received any communica- tion from the Town Clerk of Warrington respecting the matter. The SURVEYOR'S report was accepted without ques- tion.</p>
        <p>A MISUNDERSTANDING AT GREAT SANKEY.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. U. SUTTON obtained permission te explain a matter connected with Great Sankey. He said it seemed that an entire misunderstanding had arisen in the parish of Great Sankey with reference to the cost of making a small sewer and also with reference to the circumstances under which it had been done. Some two or three years ago their attention was called officially to the matter by their Inspector, and the subject had stood over from then until now, being occasionally re- ferred to when serious complaints were made. The last complaint was made by the gentleman who was the Rural district councillo4for Little Sankey,Mr.Thompson, who complained to the Inspector. The subject coming officially befere them it was absolutely necessary that they should deal with it. Ile only way in which it was dealt with was by asking their Inspector to make a re- port upon it. His report was that with reference to the nuisance at Little Sankey the length of sewer required would be about 138 yards of 9-inch pipes at a cost of something like 4s. 6d. a yard. They (the District Coun- cil) then referred it to th •jr Surveyor, and the result of Mr. Owen's report was that the pipes would cost £2l 2s. That was the only cost affected by that particular work. Contribution orders of heavier character than usual were served upon the overseers of Great Sankey. In consequence of the overseers of Sankey having called for what barely saw them through the previous half- year, and in csnsequence of having other charges than this to meet, he (Mr. Sutton) believed there was a substantial addition to their rate. The mis- take was to put that substantial addition to the work of makinv the sower, which would only cost ld. in the 2. It was other works whicts realty raised the amount of the special rate to something like £5O. It was a mistaken impression that one of the members of their Council was the cause of this being done, and it was only due to him that it should be clearly shown that it was a mistake. The only part that gentleman did take was, when a complaint was made to the Council, he asked that that particular nuisance should be remedied. With regard to the course taken by their clerk, it was his bounden duty to explain to the overseers who came to him, how it was that these contribution orders were heavier than usual. • Mr. T. HALTON : With reference to this matter I thank Mr. Sutton most heartily for his kindness in mentioning it before the Council this day. This concluded the business.</p>
        <p>LANTERN LECTURE AT THE NATIONAL SCHOOL. —On Monday evening a lantern lecture was given in the National School, Church-street, by Mr. F. 'Morrell Holmes (deputation 1 !cturer to the League of Christian Compassion), entitled "The life story of popular favourite." The subject of the " life biol.)" was that well-known magazine the Quiver The lecturer began by explaining how the Quiver was founded and how it became so popular. He explained that it was a supporter of the poor and sick, and showed pictures with the lantern of waifs and strays who had been cared for by the Quive . He also showed some cots in a hospital which was kept by it. Most of the lantern slides were copies of illustrations which had from time to time ap- peared in the magazine... The chair was occupied by the Rev. Canon Willis (rector of Warrington). There was a fairly large audience, who thoroughly enjoyed the proceedings. At the close the lecturer was warmly thanked for his trouble.</p>
        <p>JOHN WALKER &amp; CO.,</p>
      </div>
